BCoISC Financing Interest Rate by Industry[Industry Category]=
	GET DIRECT CONSTANTS(
	   'InputData/indst/BCoISC/BCoISC-FIRbI.csv',
	   ',',
	   'B2*'
	)
	~	Dmnl
	~	Real (inflation-adjusted) PRETAX cost of debt by industry, from Damodaran \
		(NYU) "Cost of Debt", Jan-2026 vintage, deflated at the 2.5 percent \
		expected inflation on the source workbook's NYU Data tab.  Pretax is the \
		correct basis here: this rate sets the actual loan repayments in Annuity \
		Factor by Industry Fuel and Process, and those repayments are booked as \
		revenue to ISIC 64T66 (financial and insurance activities) - a lender \
		receives pretax interest.  Do NOT compare this value directly against \
		BCoISC Weighted Average Cost of Capital by Industry without reading that \
		variable's note first; before 2026-08-13 the two were on different tax \
		bases and the WACC of one industry sat below its own cost of debt.  \
		Caveats on the source: Damodaran builds this synthetically as risk-free \
		plus a global default spread plus a basis spread bucketed by stock-price \
		volatility, so risk differentiation is coarse (several unrelated \
		industries share an identical value - coal mining, farming and \
		construction are all 5.291 percent nominal), and the level reflects large \
		listed investment-grade borrowers rather than the mid-market, \
		project-level industrial equipment borrowers EPS applies it to.
	|

BCoISC Weighted Average Cost of Capital by Industry[Industry Category]=
	GET DIRECT CONSTANTS(
	   'InputData/indst/BCoISC/BCoISC-WACCbI.csv',
	   ',',
	   'B2*'
	)
	~	Dmnl
	~	Real (inflation-adjusted) PRETAX weighted average cost of capital by \
		industry.  Built on the "Pretax WACC Build" tab of \
		InputData/indst/BCoISC/BAU Cost of Industry Sector Capital.xlsx as E/(D+E) \
		x cost of equity + D/(D+E) x pretax cost of debt, where cost of equity = \
		current risk-free rate + mean beta across four Damodaran vintages (Jan \
		2023 through Jan 2026) x current equity risk premium.  Two deliberate \
		departures from Damodaran's published Cost of Capital column, both dating \
		to 2026-08-13: (1) PRETAX rather than after-tax, because the industrial \
		fuel choice discounts pretax flows and models no corporate income tax on \
		fuel, carbon tax or capital, so an after-tax discount rate understated \
		required return - it simultaneously inflated the present value of future \
		fuel savings and understated annualized capital cost; (2) betas averaged \
		over four vintages, because single-vintage regression betas are noisy, \
		mean-revert, and had collapsed to implausible values (Oil/Gas Integrated \
		fell 0.978 to 0.671 to 0.484 to 0.299 across the four vintages, which gave \
		refining a real WACC of 2.5 percent - below its own cost of debt, \
		arithmetically impossible on a common tax basis).  A four-vintage mean \
		lands close to the standard Blume/Bloomberg shrinkage for every affected \
		sector.  Consumers: Cost of Capital by Industry Fuel and Process, which \
		forms the decision-side Capital Recovery Factor by Industry Fuel and \
		Process and deliberately carries NO hurdle premium; and Present Value \
		Discount and Lifetime Window Denominator, which adds HRPfIEP.  COUPLING - \
		read before editing these values: HRPfIEP Hurdle Rate Premium for \
		Industrial Equipment Purchases is defined as 0.20 minus the simple mean of \
		these 25 values, so any change here requires recomputing HRPfIEP or the 20 \
		percent target decision hurdle rate silently drifts.  The 2026-08-13 \
		rebuild moved the mean from 0.04483 to 0.05245 and HRPfIEP from 0.1552 to \
		0.1475, leaving the decision discount rate unchanged on average and \
		landing the pretax correction on the capital recovery factor alone.
	|

