	   'InputData/indst/_cement/CDSC/CDSC.csv',
	   ',',
	   'B4'
	)
	~	kWh/metric ton CO2e
	~	Electricity to purify and compress the nearly pure CO2 stream, per tonne \
		of CO2. US 105 kWh (LEILAC 2021 and Varnier et al. 2025 agree), about a \
		tenth of the reboiler heat of amine capture.
	|

CDSC Direct Separation Capture Operating Cost=
	GET DIRECT CONSTANTS(
	   'InputData/indst/_cement/CDSC/CDSC.csv',
	   ',',
	   'B3'
	)
	~	$/metric ton CO2e
	~	Operating cost of direct-separation capture per tonne of CO2, excluding \
		electricity (2012 USD). US 10.3 (LEILAC techno-economic summary 2021).
	|

CDSC Direct Separation Capture Unit Capital Cost=
	GET DIRECT CONSTANTS(
	   'InputData/indst/_cement/CDSC/CDSC.csv',
	   ',',
	   'B2'
	)
	~	$/(metric tons/Year)
	~	Capital for the purification and compression unit on the electric kiln \
		with CCS, per tonne of annual clinker capacity (2012 USD), on top of the \
		electric kiln itself. US 28.7 (CemZero 2018). See the About tab of the \
		workbook in its folder.
	|

CECpUES CHP Equipment Cost per Unit Energy Shifted:INTERPOLATE::=
	GET DIRECT DATA(
	   'InputData/dist-heat/CECpUES/CECpUES.csv',
	   ',',
	   '1',
	   'B2'
	)
	~	$/BTU
	~		|

CECRCbI Cost Effective CCS Retrofit Capacity by Industry[Industry Category,energy related emissions\
		]=
	GET DIRECT LOOKUPS(
	   'InputData/ccs/CECRCbI/CECRCbI-energy.csv',
	   ',',
	   '1',
	   'B2'
	) ~~|
CECRCbI Cost Effective CCS Retrofit Capacity by Industry[Industry Category,process emissions\
		]=
	GET DIRECT LOOKUPS(
	   'InputData/ccs/CECRCbI/CECRCbI-process.csv',
	   ',',
	   '1',
	   'B2'
	)
	~	Dmnl
	~		|

CEIbPaF Cement Energy Intensity by Pathway and Fuel[Cement Production Pathway,Industrial Fuel\
		]=
	GET DIRECT CONSTANTS(
	   'InputData/indst/_cement/CEIbPaF/CEIbPaF.csv',
	   ',',
	   'B2'
	)
	~	BTU/metric ton
	~	Final energy per metric ton of clinker by production pathway and fuel \
		(kiln boundary). Conventional kilns: DOE Bandwidth Study 2017 thermal \
		energy (about 3.43 MMBtu/t) split by the PCA 2023 fuel mix (coal and \
		petroleum coke divided by the MECS 2022 ratio), plus MECS 2022 plant \
		electricity; petroleum coke is split between petroleum diesel and heavy or \
		residual fuel oil, and alternative fuels ride biomass. The dry kiln with \
		CCS row is kiln only - the capture unit's energy comes from the CCS \
		sector's CC Energy Use per Ton Industry CO2 Sequestered. Electric kiln \
		4,398,176 BTU/t (CemZero 2018 and Quevedo Parra and Romano 2023 kiln heat \
		plus ordinary plant electricity) and alternative chemistry cement \
		4,565,177 BTU/t (Lu et al. 2024; Prancevic et al. 2026), both \
		electricity-only.
	|

Cement Allocation Benchmark Value=
	CABI Cement Allocation Benchmark Intensity
	* SUM(Clinker Production by Pathway[Cement Production Pathway!])
	* Carbon Tax Rate[industry sector]
	* Fraction of Industry Category Subject to Carbon Tax[cement and other nonmetallic minerals 239\
		]
	~	$
	~	Value of a full output-based allocation this year: the benchmark intensity \
		times clinker produced, at the industry carbon price and coverage. What \
		the industry would be handed back if every tonne were allocated at the \
		benchmark.
	|

Cement Alternative Fuel Fossil CO2 Emissions Intensity=
	0.077
	~	g CO2/BTU
	~	Energy-weighted combustion CO2 intensity of the fossil-derived alternative \
		fuels (tires 86, plastics 75, used oil 74 kg CO2 per MMBtu, EPA Emission \
		Factors Hub 2025): about 77 kg/MMBtu, range 70-86. Applied to the fossil \
		share of the biomass-labelled alternative-fuel energy and booked with the \
		kiln's process CO2, since that stream is not added to industry 239's fuel \
		use. Entered in Vensim, 2026-10-01.
	|

Cement Alternative Fuel Fossil Fraction=
	0.8
	~	Dmnl
	~	Share of the kiln's alternative-fuel energy (booked on the biomass element \
		because BIFU carries no alternative fuels for industry 239) that is \
		fossil-derived: solvents, tire-derived fuel, plastics, waste oil. US 2016 \
		PCA composition with the EPA 24 percent biogenic default for tires gives \
		about 80 percent (range 65-90); see Cement_AltFuels_Research.md. The \
		remaining 20 percent is biomass and emits no combustion CO2. Entered in \
		Vensim, 2026-10-01.
	|

Cement Border Fee Charges Full Import Intensity=
	0
	~	Dmnl [0,1,1]
	~	1: the border fee is charged on the import's whole embodied CO2 intensity \
		(EU CBAM style); 0: only on the excess over the domestic fleet's intensity \
		(Clean Competition Act style). Policy design setting entered in Vensim.
	|

Cement Border Fee per Ton Imported=
	Cement Border Fee Rate This Year
	* MAX(0, CIBP Cement Import CO2 Intensity
	  - IF THEN ELSE(Cement Border Fee Charges Full Import Intensity > 0, 0,
	                 Last Year Cement Fleet Net CO2 Intensity per Ton of Cement / Kilograms per Metric Ton\
		))
	~	$/metric ton
	~	Border fee per tonne of imported cement this year: the fee rate times the \
		import's embodied CO2 (whole or in excess of the domestic fleet's \
		intensity, per the switch).
	|

Cement Border Fee Rate=
	0
	~	output currency unit/metric ton CO2e [0,200,5]
	~	Policy lever (cement pollution border fee): a charge per tonne of CO2 \
		embodied in imported cement and clinker, at full implementation. By \
		default it is charged on the import's intensity in excess of the domestic \
		fleet's (the Clean Competition Act design: 60 dollars per tonne in 2026 on \
		emissions above the US average); with the full-intensity switch on it is \
		charged on the whole embodied intensity (the EU CBAM design, where \
		importers buy certificates at the ETS price). The fee raises the delivered \
		price of imports, and the import share of cement demand falls with an \
		Armington-type elasticity (CIBP inputs), shifting production, energy use \
		and emissions in-region; the fee revenue goes to government. Rides the \
		indst cement border fee implementation schedule.
	|

Cement Border Fee Rate in Dollars=
	Cement Border Fee Rate
	* OCCF Dollars per Small Output Currency Unit
	~	$/metric ton CO2e
	~	Cement Border Fee Rate converted to the model dollar year.
	|

Cement Border Fee Rate This Year=
	Cement Border Fee Rate in Dollars
	* Selected Policy Implementation Schedule[indst cement border fee X]
	~	$/metric ton CO2e
	~	Cement Border Fee Rate as implemented this year, on the indst cement \
		border fee implementation schedule.
	|

Cement Border Fee Revenue=
	Cement Border Fee per Ton Imported * Cement Imports in Cement Tons
	~	$
	~	Border fee collected this year on imported cement. Booked as \
		carbon-tax-type government revenue, paid by foreign exporters (a \
		simplification: in practice part of the fee passes to domestic cement \
		buyers).
	|

Cement Calcined Clay Needed This Year=
	Fraction of Additional Clinker Substitution This Year
	* Domestic Cement Production
	* CSCC Calcined Clay Share of Clinker Substitution
	~	metric tons/Year
	~	Calcined clay the policy-driven clinker substitution uses this year: the \
		substitution fraction times domestic cement production (the same tonnage \
		basis as Cement SCM Production Energy This Year) times the calcined clay \
		share. Zero at defaults and in BAU.
	|

Cement Calcined Clay Plant Capital Cost=
	New Calcined Clay Production Capacity
	* CSCC Calcined Clay Plant Capital Cost
	~	$
	~	Capital spent this year on calcined clay plants for policy-driven clinker \
		substitution. Added to industry 239's new equipment capital cost, so it is \
		financed, booked and passed through like the kilns.
	|

Cement Capture Pathway:
	dry kiln with CCS,
	electric kiln with CCS
	~	
	~	Subrange of Cement Production Pathway with a capture unit: the dry kiln \
		with amine-type post-combustion capture and the electric kiln whose nearly \
		pure calcination CO2 is purified and compressed (direct separation).
	|

Cement Carbon Price Allocation Fraction=
	MIN(1,
	  BCAF BAU Cement Carbon Price Allocation Fraction
	  + Fraction of Cement Carbon Price Rebated by Output Based Allocation This Year
	)
	~	Dmnl
	~	Share of the cement industry's carbon-price liability handed back against \
		output in the policy case: the BAU allocation plus the output-based \
		allocation lever, capped at one.
	|

Cement Carbon Tax Liability Eligible for Allocation=
	(Cement Process CO2 Emissions - Cement Process CO2 Captured by Pathway Capture)
	/ Grams per Metric Ton CO2e
	* Cement Process Emissions Carbon Tax Rate
	+ (Cement Kiln Combustion CO2 Emissions - Cement Combustion CO2 Captured by Pathway Capture\
		)
	  / Grams per Metric Ton CO2e
	  * Carbon Tax Rate[industry sector]
	  * Fraction of Industry Category Subject to Carbon Tax[cement and other nonmetallic minerals 239\
		]
	~	$
	~	Carbon tax the cement module's kilns owe this year: post-capture process \
		CO2 at the process-emissions rate (which carries the process exemption, \
		coverage and pollutant gating) plus post-capture kiln-fuel CO2 at the \
		industry rate and coverage. The ceiling on the output-based allocation \
		rebate: allocation never pays out more than was charged.
	|

Cement Carbon Tax Rebate from Output Based Allocation=
	Cement Carbon Price Allocation Fraction
	* MIN(Cement Allocation Benchmark Value, Cement Carbon Tax Liability Eligible for Allocation\
		)
	~	$
	~	Carbon tax handed back to the cement industry this year under output-based \
		allocation: the allocation fraction times the benchmark value, capped at \
		the tax actually owed. Paid government to industry 239 through the \
		industry carbon-tax rebate chain (with the CCS rebate), so it lowers the \
		carbon cost passed through to cement buyers while the pathway choice keeps \
		seeing the full marginal carbon price.
	|

Cement Carbon Tax Rebate Rate on Captured CO2 by Emissions Type[process emissions]=
	0 ~~|
Cement Carbon Tax Rebate Rate on Captured CO2 by Emissions Type[energy related emissions\
		]=
	Carbon Tax Rate[industry sector]
	* Fraction of Industry Category Subject to Carbon Tax[cement and other nonmetallic minerals 239\
		]
	~	$/metric ton CO2e
	~	Carbon tax handed back per ton of CO2 captured by the cement CCS pathway, \
		by emissions type. Kiln-fuel combustion CO2 is taxed when the fuel is \
		burned, so its capture earns the industry carbon tax rate times industry \
		239's coverage, the same terms as Industry Sector Carbon Tax Rebate due to \
		CCS. Captured process CO2 earns nothing here: the process carbon tax is \
		charged on emissions after capture, so those tons were never taxed.
	|

Cement Carveout Share of Energy by Fuel[Industrial Fuel]= INITIAL(
	MIN(1,
	ZIDZ(
	  Cement Start Year Module Energy Use by Fuel Type[Industrial Fuel]
	  * (1 - Cement Fuel Feedstock Share[Industrial Fuel])
	  * Boolean Cement Fuel Present in Industry 239 Input Data[Industrial Fuel],
	  BIFU BAU Industrial Fuel Use for Energy[cement and other nonmetallic minerals 239,\
		Industrial Fuel]
	  * SUM(BAU Percentage Fuel Use by Industrial Process with Fallback Data[cement and other nonmetallic minerals 239\
		,Industrial Fuel,industrial process end use!])
	  * (1 + Perc Change in Sectoral Energy Use due to Exogenous GDP Adjustment[industry sector\
		])
	)))
	~	Dmnl
	~	The cement module's start-year share of 239's process-end-use energy by \
		fuel: data-only numerator (start-year module energy, energy- purpose part, \
		fuels in the 239 input data only) over the BIFU data's start-year \
		process-end-use energy. Since Phase 3a this share sits INSIDE the generic \
		structure's data spine (BAU Industrial Equipment Energy Use from Input \
		Data), scaling the whole 239 equipment structure - output, stocks, \
		purchases, capex - to the non-cement residual; both sides of the ZIDZ are \
		pure input data, so no initialization loop exists. Frozen at the start \
		year BY DESIGN: the residual is a fixed set of physical plants (lime, \
		gypsum, ceramics) and should not shrink or grow because cement's own \
		trajectory changes. Capped at 1 (a module slightly exceeding the legacy \
		stream takes the whole stream; the small excess is a documented inventory \
		refresh). Roughly 0.75 for hard coal, 0.24 natural gas, 0.23 electricity \
		at draft values.
	|

Cement Carveout Share of Feedstock by Fuel[Industrial Fuel]= INITIAL(
	MIN(1,
	ZIDZ(
	  Cement Start Year Module Energy Use by Fuel Type[Industrial Fuel]
	  * Cement Fuel Feedstock Share[Industrial Fuel],
	  BIFU BAU Industrial Fuel Use as Feedstock[cement and other nonmetallic minerals 239\
		,Industrial Fuel]
	  * (1 + Perc Change in Sectoral Energy Use due to Exogenous GDP Adjustment[industry sector\
		])
	)))
	~	Dmnl
	~	The cement module's start-year share of 239's feedstock use by fuel -
	|

Cement Carveout Share of Process CO2= INITIAL(
	MIN(1,
	ZIDZ(
	  BAU Cement Process CO2 Emissions,
	  BPE BAU Process Emissions[cement and other nonmetallic minerals 239,CO2]
	  * (1 + Perc Change in Sectoral Energy Use due to Exogenous GDP Adjustment[industry sector\
		])
	)))
	~	Dmnl
	~	The cement module's start-year share of 239's exogenous process CO2 (BPE, \
		a blend of cement calcination, lime, and other carbonate sources per its \
		source workbook): module start-year process CO2 over the BPE data. Roughly \
		0.71 at draft values - consistent with GHGI's cement 41.9 vs lime 12.2 MMT \
		split. Anchored to the BPE DATA (init-stable), same convention as the \
		energy carveout shares.
	|

Cement CCfD Abatement per Ton of Clinker by Pathway[Cement Production Pathway]=
	MAX(0, Cement Pathway Net CO2 Intensity per Ton of Clinker[dry kiln with precalciner\
		]
	     - Cement Pathway Net CO2 Intensity per Ton of Clinker[Cement Production Pathway\
		])
	/ Grams per Metric Ton CO2e
	~	metric tons CO2e/metric ton
	~	CO2 a kiln on each pathway avoids per tonne of clinker relative to a \
		conventional precalciner kiln, the reference a cement contract for \
		difference pays against (German contracts fix a conventional reference \
		system for the term). Zero for the conventional pathway, so it never gets \
		a contract.
	|

Cement CCfD Average Contracted Strike Price by Pathway[Cement Production Pathway]=
	ZIDZ(Last Year Cement CCfD Contracted Strike Value by Pathway[Cement Production Pathway\
		] + Cement CCfD Strike Value Entering Contracts by Pathway[Cement Production Pathway\
		],
	     Last Year Clinker Capacity Receiving Production Support by Program and Pathway[\
		carbon contract for difference,Cement Production Pathway]
	     + Clinker Capacity Entering Production Support by Program and Pathway[carbon contract for difference\
		,Cement Production Pathway])
	~	$/metric ton CO2e
	~	Average strike price of the contracts for difference in force this year, \
		weighted by capacity. A contract keeps the strike it signed at for its \
		whole term, so raising the lever later only affects new contracts.
	|

Cement CCfD Contract Length=
	15
	~	years [5,20,1]
	~	Years a cement carbon contract for difference pays after the kiln is \
		built: 15 in Germany, the Netherlands and Denmark, 10 plus 5 in the UK. \
		Policy design setting entered in Vensim (no implementation schedule).
	|

Cement CCfD Other Support per Ton CO2 Abated by Pathway[Cement Production Pathway]=
	Industry Sector CCS Subsidy by Industry Category[cement and other nonmetallic minerals 239\
		]
	* ZIDZ(SUM(Cement CCS Captured CO2 per Ton of Clinker by Emissions Type[Cement Production Pathway\
		,Emissions Type!]) / Grams per Metric Ton CO2e,
	       Cement CCfD Abatement per Ton of Clinker by Pathway[Cement Production Pathway\
		])
	~	$/metric ton CO2e
	~	Per-tonne support a contracted kiln already receives on its abatement and \
		that the contract nets out (German contracts deduct other aid): the \
		industry CCS subsidy (45Q) on the captured share of the abatement. Zero \
		for pathways without capture.
	|

Cement CCfD Payment per Ton CO2 Abated by Pathway[Cement Production Pathway]=
	IF THEN ELSE(Cement CCfD Strike Price This Year > 0
	  :AND: Cement CCfD Abatement per Ton of Clinker by Pathway[Cement Production Pathway\
		] > 0,
	  MAX(0, Cement CCfD Strike Price This Year - Cement CCfD Reference Carbon Price - Cement CCfD Other Support per Ton CO2 Abated by Pathway\
		[Cement Production Pathway])
	  + CCTS Cement CCfD Two Sided Share * MIN(0, Cement CCfD Strike Price This Year - Cement CCfD Reference Carbon Price\
		),
	  0)
	~	$/metric ton CO2e
	~	What a contract signed this year would pay per ton of CO2 abated: the \
		strike less this year's carbon price and other per-ton support (45Q on \
		captured CO2), floored at zero; for the two-sided share, a carbon price \
		above the strike turns into a payback of the excess. Other support can \
		bring the payment to zero but is never clawed back. Used for the \
		investment decision and for entry into a contract.
	|

Cement CCfD Payment per Ton CO2 Abated on Contracted Kilns by Pathway[Cement Production Pathway\
		]=
	IF THEN ELSE(Cement CCfD Average Contracted Strike Price by Pathway[Cement Production Pathway\
		] > 0
	  :AND: Cement CCfD Abatement per Ton of Clinker by Pathway[Cement Production Pathway\
		] > 0,
	  MAX(0, Cement CCfD Average Contracted Strike Price by Pathway[Cement Production Pathway\
		] - Cement CCfD Reference Carbon Price - Cement CCfD Other Support per Ton CO2 Abated by Pathway\
		[Cement Production Pathway])
	  + CCTS Cement CCfD Two Sided Share * MIN(0, Cement CCfD Average Contracted Strike Price by Pathway\
		[Cement Production Pathway] - Cement CCfD Reference Carbon Price),
	  0)
	~	$/metric ton CO2e
	~	What the contracts in force pay this year per ton of CO2 abated: as for a \
		new contract, but at the strike price locked in when each kiln signed \
		(averaged over the contracted kilns).
	|

Cement CCfD Reference Carbon Price=
	Carbon Tax Rate[industry sector]
	* Fraction of Industry Category Subject to Carbon Tax[cement and other nonmetallic minerals 239\
		]
	~	$/metric ton CO2e
	~	Carbon price the cement industry already faces per tonne of CO2 this year: \
		the reference price a contract for difference tops up to the strike.
	|

Cement CCfD Strike Price=
	0
	~	output currency unit/metric ton CO2e [0,300,5]
	~	Policy lever (cement carbon contract for difference): the guaranteed price \
		per tonne of CO2 abated that government offers new low-carbon clinker \
		capacity, at full implementation. Each year a contracted kiln is paid the \
		strike price less the carbon price it already faces (and less any \
		per-tonne capture credit it already receives) on every tonne it abates \
		relative to a conventional precalciner kiln, for the contract length; with \
		the two-sided switch on it pays the difference back when the carbon price \
		exceeds the strike. This is how Germany's Klimaschutzvertraege, the \
		Netherlands' SDE++ and Denmark's and the UK's industrial carbon-capture \
		contracts work. Denmark's 2026 cement contract pays about 117 EUR per \
		tonne captured for 15 years (roughly 95 2012-dollars); German bids are \
		capped at 550-600 EUR. With no carbon price in the model the contract is a \
		straight payment per tonne abated. Zero turns the programme off. Rides the \
		indst cement CCfD implementation schedule.
	|

Cement CCfD Strike Price in Dollars=
	Cement CCfD Strike Price
	* OCCF Dollars per Small Output Currency Unit
	~	$/metric ton CO2e
	~	Cement CCfD Strike Price converted to the model dollar year.
	|

Cement CCfD Strike Price This Year=
	Cement CCfD Strike Price in Dollars
	* Selected Policy Implementation Schedule[indst cement CCfD X]
	~	$/metric ton CO2e
	~	Cement CCfD Strike Price as implemented this year, on the indst cement \
		CCfD implementation schedule.
	|

Cement CCfD Strike Value Entering Contracts by Pathway[Cement Production Pathway]=
	Clinker Capacity Entering Production Support by Program and Pathway[carbon contract for difference\
		,Cement Production Pathway]
	* Cement CCfD Strike Price This Year
	~	$/Year
	~	Capacity signing a contract for difference this year times this year's \
		strike price: the strike each new contract locks in, weighted by its \
		capacity.
	|

Cement CCfD Strike Value Leaving Contracts by Pathway[Cement Production Pathway]=
	DELAY FIXED(Cement CCfD Strike Value Entering Contracts by Pathway[Cement Production Pathway\
		], Cement CCfD Contract Length, 0)
	~	$/Year
	~	Strike value of the contracts that expire this year.
	|

Cement CCS Capture Operating Cost per Ton of Clinker[Cement Production Pathway] :EXCEPT:\
		 [electric kiln with CCS]=
	SUM(
	  Cement CCS Captured CO2 per Ton of Clinker by Emissions Type[Cement Production Pathway\
		,Emissions Type!]
	  / Grams per Metric Ton CO2e
	  * (
	      CC CCS Total OM Cost per Industry Ton Sequestered[cement and other nonmetallic minerals 239\
		,Emissions Type!]
	      + CCSTaSC CCS Transportation and Storage Costs
	      - Industrial CCS Transportation and Storage Subsidy
	      - Industry Sector CCS Subsidy by Industry Category[cement and other nonmetallic minerals 239\
		]
	      - Cement Carbon Tax Rebate Rate on Captured CO2 by Emissions Type[Emissions Type\
		!]
	    )
	)
	+ SUM(
	    CC Energy Use per Ton Industry CO2 Sequestered[cement and other nonmetallic minerals 239\
		,Industrial Fuel!,Emissions Type!]
	    * Cement CCS Captured CO2 per Ton of Clinker by Emissions Type[Cement Production Pathway\
		,Emissions Type!]
	    * Industrial Fuel Cost per Unit Energy[cement and other nonmetallic minerals 239\
		,Industrial Fuel!]
	  )
	  * (1 - RnD CCS Fuel Use Perc Reduction This Year) ~~|
Cement CCS Capture Operating Cost per Ton of Clinker[electric kiln with CCS]=
	SUM(
	  Cement CCS Captured CO2 per Ton of Clinker by Emissions Type[electric kiln with CCS\
		,Emissions Type!]
	  / Grams per Metric Ton CO2e
	  * (
	      CDSC Direct Separation Capture Operating Cost
	      + CCSTaSC CCS Transportation and Storage Costs
	      - Industrial CCS Transportation and Storage Subsidy
	      - Industry Sector CCS Subsidy by Industry Category[cement and other nonmetallic minerals 239\
		]
	      - Cement Carbon Tax Rebate Rate on Captured CO2 by Emissions Type[Emissions Type\
		!]
	    )
	)
	+ SUM(Cement CCS Captured CO2 per Ton of Clinker by Emissions Type[electric kiln with CCS\
		,Emissions Type!])
	  / Grams per Metric Ton CO2e
	  * CDSC Direct Separation Capture Electricity
	  / kWh per MWh / MWh per BTU
	  * Industrial Fuel Cost per Unit Energy[cement and other nonmetallic minerals 239,electricity if\
		]
	  * (1 - RnD CCS Fuel Use Perc Reduction This Year)
	~	$/metric ton
	~	What running the capture unit adds to a CCS kiln's cost per ton of \
		clinker, for each kind of captured CO2: capture O&M, transport and storage \
		net of any transport-and-storage subsidy, less the 45Q-type subsidy and, \
		for captured kiln-fuel CO2, the carbon tax handed back on it, plus the \
		capture unit's energy at this year's fuel prices (after the CCS fuel-use \
		R&D lever). O&M and capture energy use the CCS sector's industry 239 \
		inputs by emissions type, because that is how the CCS sector books their \
		cash flows from the captured tons. A decision cost only. Zero for pathways \
		without capture.
	|

Cement CCS Captured CO2 per Ton of Clinker by Emissions Type[Cement Production Pathway\
		,process emissions]=
	Cement CCS Captured Process CO2 per Ton of Clinker[Cement Production Pathway] ~~|
Cement CCS Captured CO2 per Ton of Clinker by Emissions Type[Cement Production Pathway\
		,energy related emissions]=
	Cement CCS Captured Combustion CO2 per Ton of Clinker[Cement Production Pathway]
	~	g CO2/metric ton
	~	CO2 captured per ton of clinker on each pathway, split the way the CCS \
		sector books it: process emissions (calcination and the fuel carbon the \
		module books as process CO2) and energy-related emissions (kiln-fuel \
		combustion). Prices the capture equipment and the capture operating cost.
	|

Cement CCS Captured Combustion CO2 per Ton of Clinker[Cement Production Pathway]=
	Cement Pathway CO2 Capture Fraction[Cement Production Pathway]
	* Cement Kiln Combustion CO2 per Ton of Clinker by Pathway[Cement Production Pathway\
		]
	~	g CO2/metric ton
	~	Kiln-fuel combustion CO2 captured per ton of clinker on the CCS pathway: \
		the capture share times the kiln's combustion CO2. Zero for the other \
		pathways. Sizes the capture equipment, O&M, capture energy, transport and \
		storage cost and subsidy for these tons, priced from the CCS sector's \
		energy-related industry inputs, and earns the carbon tax rebate on \
		captured combustion CO2.
	|

Cement CCS Captured Process CO2 per Ton of Clinker[Cement Production Pathway] :EXCEPT:\
		 [Cement Capture Pathway]=
	0 ~~|
Cement CCS Captured Process CO2 per Ton of Clinker[Cement Capture Pathway]=
	CCF Calcination CO2 Factor by Pathway[dry kiln with precalciner]
	- CCF Calcination CO2 Factor by Pathway[Cement Capture Pathway]
	+ Cement Pathway CO2 Capture Fraction[Cement Capture Pathway]
	  * Cement Fuel Carbon Booked as Process CO2 per Ton of Clinker by Pathway[Cement Capture Pathway\
		]
	~	g CO2/metric ton
	~	CO2 the CCS pathway captures per ton of clinker from the CO2 the module \
		books as process emissions: the calcination gap between the uncaptured \
		factor (dry kiln with precalciner) and the CCS pathway's net factor (CCF), \
		plus the same capture share of the kiln fuel carbon booked as process CO2. \
		Zero for the other pathways. Sizes the capture equipment, O&M, capture \
		energy, transport and storage cost and subsidy for these tons, all priced \
		from the CCS sector's process-emissions industry inputs. Captured \
		combustion CO2 is in Cement CCS Captured Combustion CO2 per Ton of Clinker.
	|

Cement CO2 Intensity Standard=
	0
	~	Dmnl [0,1,0.01]
	~	Policy lever (cement carbon-intensity standard): the required reduction in \
		the direct CO2 per tonne of cement produced in-region, relative to the \
		start-year fleet, at full implementation (California SB 596: 40 percent \
		below 2019 by 2035, net zero by 2045, so 0.4 on a schedule reaching full \
		implementation in 2035). The fleet complies in cost order each year: first \
		by substituting supplementary cementitious materials for clinker up to the \
		SCM supply limit, then by building the cheapest low-carbon pathway as the \
		required share of new kiln capacity. No cash flow is booked for the \
		standard itself; the compliance costs land in the existing capital, \
		operating and clinker-substitution chains. Zero turns the standard off. \
		Rides the indst cement intensity standard implementation schedule.
	|

Cement CO2 Intensity Standard Level This Year=
	Cement Start Year Fleet CO2 Intensity per Ton of Cement * (1 - Cement CO2 Intensity Standard This Year\
		)
	~	kg CO2/metric ton
	~	The intensity cap in force this year, in kilograms of CO2 per tonne of \
		cement.
	|

Cement CO2 Intensity Standard This Year=
	Cement CO2 Intensity Standard
	* Selected Policy Implementation Schedule[indst cement intensity standard X]
	~	Dmnl
	~	The cement intensity standard as implemented this year (the required \
		fractional reduction).
	|

Cement Combustion CO2 Captured by Pathway Capture=
	SUM(
	  Clinker Production by Pathway[Cement Production Pathway!]
	  * Cement Energy Intensity Multiplier by Pathway[Cement Production Pathway!]
	  * Cement CCS Captured Combustion CO2 per Ton of Clinker[Cement Production Pathway!\
		]
	)
	~	g CO2
	~	Kiln-fuel combustion CO2 captured this year by the module's CCS pathway. \
		Added to industry 239's sequestered energy-related CO2 in the CCS sector, \
		so the emissions netting, transport and storage, subsidies, capture O&M, \
		capture energy and the carbon tax rebate are booked once by the CCS sector \
		(the module books the capture capital itself).
	|

Cement Content Lever Component of 239 Output Change=
	-Cement Content Reduction in 239 Product Requirements
	* SUM(
	    TIOT Total Input Output Table[ISIC 239,Industry Category ISIC Code!]
	    * BAU Output by ISIC Code by Year[Industry Category ISIC Code!]
	  )
	* BAU Domestic Content Share of Consumption by ISIC Code from Exogenous Data[ISIC 239\
		]
	~	$
	~	The cement-content lever's contribution to Change in Output due to \
		Material Efficiency by ISIC Code[ISIC 239], computed with the same formula \
		the material-efficiency machinery applies to the injected matrix term. \
		Used ONLY to divide the content component back OUT of the PHYSICAL \
		production-percent fold-in (Percent Change in Production due to Material \
		Efficiency by Industry Category[239]): the lever's physical cut is carried \
		at full strength and cement- only by the (1 - lever) factor on Total \
		Cement Demand, so folding the diluted 239-wide percent into the physical \
		chain would both double-count cement and wrongly shrink the lime/ceramics \
		residual. The DOLLAR booking stays in (supplier revenue loss via the IO \
		injection; buyer cost savings, price index, and passthrough via the \
		savings-share chain).
	|

Cement Content Reduction in 239 Product Requirements=
	Percent Reduction in Cement Content of Concrete This Year
	* CSoIO Cement Share of Industry 239 Output
	~	Dmnl
	~	The cement-content-of-concrete lever expressed as a material- efficiency \
		improvement in 239 PRODUCT terms: buyers need x percent less cement, which \
		is CSoIO of the 239 product they buy, applied uniformly across buying \
		industries (construction dominates 239's sales; buyer heterogeneity beyond \
		the value share is not modeled). Injected into the Material Efficiency \
		Improvement matrix's ISIC 239 source row so the supplier revenue loss, \
		buyer cost savings, buyer price index, and passthrough all book through \
		the standard machinery (2026-08-27, Dan-approved: the lever was \
		PHYSICAL-ONLY before this - GDP and jobs did not respond; \
		adversarial-review finding 2 of the levers block).
	|

Cement Direct Separation CO2 Captured=
	Clinker Production by Pathway[electric kiln with CCS]
	* SUM(Cement CCS Captured CO2 per Ton of Clinker by Emissions Type[electric kiln with CCS\
		,Emissions Type!])
	~	g CO2
	~	CO2 captured this year by the electric kiln with CCS (direct separation). \
		It is part of industry 239's sequestered process CO2 in the CCS sector, \
		which books its transport, storage and subsidies; the CCS sector's \
		amine-capture energy and O&M are replaced by the CDSC electricity and \
		operating cost for these tons.
	|

Cement Domestic Production in Cement Tons=
	ZIDZ(SUM(Clinker Production by Pathway[Cement Production Pathway!]), Clinker to Cement Ratio\
		)
	~	metric tons/Year
	~	Cement produced in-region this year, from the module's clinker production \
		at this year's clinker-to-cement ratio.
	|

Cement Energy Intensity by Pathway and Fuel after Kiln Fuel Shift[Cement Production Pathway\
		,Industrial Fuel]=
	MAX(0,
  	Cement Energy Intensity by Pathway and Fuel This Year[Cement Production Pathway,Industrial Fuel\
		]
  	- Kiln Energy Shifted Away per Ton of Clinker by Pathway and Fuel[Cement Production Pathway\
		,Industrial Fuel]
  	+ Kiln Energy Shifted In per Ton of Clinker by Pathway and Fuel[Cement Production Pathway\
		,Industrial Fuel]
	)
	~	BTU/metric ton
	~	Energy intensity by pathway and fuel after the kiln fuel shift lever: each \
		fossil fuel less the energy drawn from it, each destination fuel plus the \
		energy arriving, electricity and total thermal intensity unchanged. \
		Policy-branch consumers (fleet and new-build operating costs, module \
		energy use, the fuel carbon booked as process CO2, kiln combustion CO2) \
		read this; the BAU branch reads the pre-shift Cement Energy Intensity by \
		Pathway and Fuel This Year directly, so it needs no separate twin. At \
		lever settings of zero this equals the pre-shift intensity exactly; the \
		MAX keeps a fully drawn fuel at zero rather than at floating-point noise \
		below it.
	|

Cement Energy Intensity by Pathway and Fuel This Year[Cement Production Pathway,Industrial Fuel\
		] :EXCEPT: [Cement Production Pathway,electricity if]=
	CEIbPaF Cement Energy Intensity by Pathway and Fuel[Cement Production Pathway,Industrial Fuel\
		]
	* BAU Cement Kiln Fuel Mix Index[Industrial Fuel]
	* Cement Fuel Mix Renormalization Factor by Pathway[Cement Production Pathway] ~~|
Cement Energy Intensity by Pathway and Fuel This Year[Cement Production Pathway,electricity if\
		]=
	CEIbPaF Cement Energy Intensity by Pathway and Fuel[Cement Production Pathway,electricity if\
		]
	~	BTU/metric ton
	~	Energy intensity by pathway and fuel THIS YEAR: the start-year intensity \
		(CEIbPaF, PCA mix on the DOE bandwidth thermal basis) with each thermal \
		fuel scaled by its AEO share index and the set renormalized so total \
		thermal intensity per pathway is unchanged (fuel switching only; \
		efficiency change is CNBEIR's job). Electricity is not shifted. Feeds \
		fleet and new-build operating costs, module energy, and the process-CO2 \
		petcoke carbon term; the start-year carve-out keeps CEIbPaF. Same in BAU \
		and policy (BAU data), so no BAU twin. Added 2026-09-30 after validation \
		against the AEO cement block.
	|

Cement Energy Intensity Multiplier by Pathway[Cement Production Pathway]=
	1
	- Share of Clinker Production Capacity Built During Model Run[Cement Production Pathway\
		]
	  * (1 - CNBEIR Cement New Build Energy Intensity Ratio[Cement Production Pathway])
	- (1 - Share of Clinker Production Capacity Built During Model Run[Cement Production Pathway\
		])
	  * Overhauled Share of Preexisting Clinker Capacity by Pathway[Cement Production Pathway\
		]
	  * Percent Improvement in Overhauled Kiln Energy Intensity This Year
	~	Dmnl
	~	Production-weighted energy-intensity multiplier per pathway: one for an \
		all-preexisting fleet, CNBEIR for an all-new fleet, less the \
		overhauled-kiln improvement lever on the overhauled share of the \
		start-year fleet (policy case only). Applied to CEIbPaF in the module \
		energy accounting. The process-CO2 intensity deliberately keeps the \
		unscaled CEIbPaF basis (calcination dominates it; the petcoke \
		feedstock-carbon term's small overstate- ment for new builds is documented \
		rather than modeled).
	|

Cement Energy Use Process Shares[Industrial Fuel,industrial process end use]= INITIAL\
		(
	ZIDZ(
	  BAU Percentage Fuel Use by Industrial Process with Fallback Data[cement and other nonmetallic minerals 239\
		,Industrial Fuel,industrial process end use],
	  SUM(BAU Percentage Fuel Use by Industrial Process with Fallback Data[cement and other nonmetallic minerals 239\
		,Industrial Fuel,industrial process end use!])
	))
	~	Dmnl
	~	Start-year distribution of each fuel across cement and other nonmetallic \
		minerals 239's process end uses, taken from the model's own BAU \
		apportionment (BPFUbIP with fallbacks) renormalized over process end uses. \
		Spreads the cement module's energy-by-fuel into process bands so WHR, \
		process-efficiency, and band-specific logic keep working. Phase 2 can \
		assign kiln fuel physically to heat above 1000 C instead.
	|

Cement Expected Production Support per Ton of Clinker by Pathway[Cement Production Pathway\
		]=
	SUM(
	  Cement Production Support Offered per Ton of Clinker by Program and Pathway[Cement Production Support Program\
		!,Cement Production Pathway]
	  * MIN(1, ZIDZ(Cement Production Support Term by Program[Cement Production Support Program\
		!], CPAL Cement Pathway Asset Life[Cement Production Pathway]))
	)
	~	$/metric ton
	~	Production support an investor counts against a new kiln's levelized cost: \
		each program's per-ton payment spread over the kiln's life by the share of \
		the life the program pays (an undiscounted approximation of the payments' \
		annuity value; expected carbon prices are taken as this year's).
	|

Cement Expected Utilization for Investment Decisions=
	SMOOTHI(
	  Cement Fleet Utilization,
	  Cement Utilization Smoothing Time,
	  SYCCU Start Year Cement Capacity Utilization)
	~	Dmnl
	~	The utilization an investor assumes when spreading kiln capital over tons: \
		the fleet's recent utilization smoothed over Cement Utilization Smoothing \
		Time, starting at the observed start-year value. Used by BOTH sides of the \
		overhaul-or-replace comparison (continuation cost and greenfield levelized \
		cost), replacing the earlier asymmetry (continuation at this year's \
		utilization, greenfield at the fixed SYCCU norm) and the transient-shock \
		myopia it caused. New-build SIZING still uses the SYCCU norm. No same-year \
		loop: SMOOTHI reads its own state.
	|

Cement Fleet Marginal Operating Cost per Ton of Clinker=
	ZIDZ(
	  SUM(
	    Last Year Clinker Production Capacity by Pathway[Cement Production Pathway!]
	    * Cement Pathway Operating Cost per Ton of Clinker[Cement Production Pathway!]
	  ),
	  SUM(Last Year Clinker Production Capacity by Pathway[Cement Production Pathway!])
	)
	~	$/metric ton
	~	Capacity-weighted average operating cost of the surviving fleet: the cost \
		at which spare capacity can absorb a retiring kiln's output (sunk capital, \
		marginal operating cost only). Capacity-weighted rather than \
		production-weighted to avoid a same-year loop through this year's decision \
		retirements.
	|

Cement Fleet Net CO2 Intensity per Ton of Cement=
	ZIDZ(Cement Taxable CO2 after Capture * Kilograms per Metric Ton, Cement Domestic Production in Cement Tons\
		)
	~	kg CO2/metric ton
	~	Direct CO2 of the in-region cement fleet per tonne of cement produced this \
		year, after capture: the quantity a cement carbon-intensity standard caps \
		and the domestic reference for an intensity-based border fee. Starts near \
		0.78 t per tonne for the US.
	|

Cement Fleet Net CO2 Intensity per Ton of Clinker=
	ZIDZ(Cement Taxable CO2 after Capture * Kilograms per Metric Ton, SUM(Clinker Production by Pathway\
		[Cement Production Pathway!]))
	~	kg CO2/metric ton
	~	Direct CO2 of the in-region kiln fleet per tonne of clinker this year, \
		after capture.
	|

Cement Fleet Tightness=
	MIN(1, MAX(0,
	  ZIDZ(
	    Cement Fleet Utilization - CULB Cement Utilization Lower Bound,
	    CUUB Cement Utilization Upper Bound - CULB Cement Utilization Lower Bound
	  )))
	~	Dmnl
	~	Where the fleet sits inside the region's historical utilization envelope \
		(0 = at the lower bound CULB, 1 = at the upper bound CUUB), clamped to \
		0..1. One dimensionless index drives the replacement blend weight and the \
		congestion adder, so decision costs shift smoothly as the fleet APPROACHES \
		the band edges rather than only upon hitting them, and both tails are \
		normal operating regimes (sustained 0 = chronic overcapacity, sustained 1 \
		= tight growth market). Design memo: \
		Cement_Utilization_Tightness_Design.md.
	|

Cement Fleet Utilization=
	QUANTUM(
	  ZIDZ(
	    Domestic Clinker Demand,
	    SUM(Last Year Clinker Production Capacity by Pathway[Cement Production Pathway!]\
		)
	  ),
	  0.0001)
	~	Dmnl
	~	This year's clinker demand over the fleet's beginning-of-year nameplate \
		capacity, gross of this year's decisions (same no-same-year-loop basis as \
		the retirement decision pool: capacity is a beginning-of-year stock and \
		demand responds to costs only via Last Year chains). The utilization the \
		incumbent fleet would run at if it alone served demand - the state \
		variable of the tightness structure. QUANTUM carries the 10 kt headroom \
		convention onto the ratio scale (1e-4 of utilization = 10 kt on a
	|

Cement Fuel Carbon Booked as Process CO2 per Ton of Clinker by Pathway[Cement Production Pathway\
		]=
	SUM(
	  Cement Energy Intensity by Pathway and Fuel after Kiln Fuel Shift[Cement Production Pathway\
		,Industrial Fuel!]
	  * Cement Fuel Feedstock Share[Industrial Fuel!]
	  * Cement Fuel CO2 Emissions Intensity[Industrial Fuel!]
	)
	+ Cement Fuel Carbon Correction per Ton of Clinker by Pathway[Cement Production Pathway\
		]
	~	g CO2/metric ton
	~	Kiln fuel carbon per ton of clinker that the module books with its process \
		CO2 rather than in industry 239's fuel accounts, before any capture: the \
		carbon in petcoke carried on the residual-oil feedstock element (feedstock \
		does not combust in the generic accounting), the petcoke emission-factor \
		correction, and the fossil share of the alternative fuels. Fleet-intensity \
		basis.
	|

Cement Fuel Carbon Correction per Ton of Clinker by Pathway[Cement Production Pathway\
		]=
	SUM(
	  Cement Energy Intensity by Pathway and Fuel after Kiln Fuel Shift[Cement Production Pathway\
		,Industrial Fuel!]
	  * Cement Petroleum Fuel Flag[Industrial Fuel!]
	  * (Cement Petroleum Coke CO2 Emissions Intensity - Cement Fuel CO2 Emissions Intensity\
		[Industrial Fuel!]))
	+ Cement Energy Intensity by Pathway and Fuel after Kiln Fuel Shift[Cement Production Pathway\
		,biomass if]
	  * Cement Alternative Fuel Fossil Fraction
	  * Cement Alternative Fuel Fossil CO2 Emissions Intensity
	~	g CO2/metric ton
	~	CO2 per ton of clinker that the generic fuel accounting would miss, booked \
		with the kiln's process CO2: the petcoke emission-factor correction on the \
		two petroleum elements that carry it (petcoke emits at 102 kg/MMBtu, its \
		carrier fuels at 74-75), and the fossil-derived share of the alternative \
		fuels (biomass-labelled, not added to industry 239). Fleet-intensity \
		basis; the new-build process-CO2 intensity scales it by CNBEIR. Added \
		2026-10-01.
	|

Cement Fuel CO2 Emissions Intensity[Industrial Fuel]=
	CO2 Emissions Intensity by Fuel for All Fuels[industrial fuel within all fuels]
	~	g CO2/BTU
	~	CO2 content of each industrial fuel, mapped from the All Fuels dimension \
		onto Industrial Fuel. Exists because a mapped subrange cannot co-range \
		with Industrial Fuel inside a SUM (they range independently, producing a \
		double sum) - the elementwise mapping here is the safe form (steel P2-A \
		lesson, in the KB gotchas).
	|

Cement Fuel Feedstock Share[Industrial Fuel]= INITIAL(
	ZIDZ(
	  BIFU BAU Industrial Fuel Use as Feedstock[cement and other nonmetallic minerals 239\
		,Industrial Fuel],
	  BIFU BAU Industrial Fuel Use for Energy[cement and other nonmetallic minerals 239,\
		Industrial Fuel]
	  * SUM(BAU Percentage Fuel Use by Industrial Process with Fallback Data[cement and other nonmetallic minerals 239\
		,Industrial Fuel,industrial process end use!])
	  + BIFU BAU Industrial Fuel Use as Feedstock[cement and other nonmetallic minerals 239\
		,Industrial Fuel]
	))
	~	Dmnl
	~	Start-year share of each fuel's total 239 use (process-end-use energy plus \
		feedstock) that is feedstock rather than combustion - in practice
	|

Cement Fuel Mix Renormalization Factor by Pathway[Cement Production Pathway]=
	ZIDZ(
	  SUM(CEIbPaF Cement Energy Intensity by Pathway and Fuel[Cement Production Pathway,\
		Industrial Fuel!])
	  - CEIbPaF Cement Energy Intensity by Pathway and Fuel[Cement Production Pathway,electricity if\
		],
	  SUM(
	    CEIbPaF Cement Energy Intensity by Pathway and Fuel[Cement Production Pathway,Industrial Fuel\
		!]
	    * BAU Cement Kiln Fuel Mix Index[Industrial Fuel!])
	  - CEIbPaF Cement Energy Intensity by Pathway and Fuel[Cement Production Pathway,electricity if\
		]
	    * BAU Cement Kiln Fuel Mix Index[electricity if]
	)
	~	Dmnl
	~	Start-year kiln thermal (non-electric) intensity over the same intensity \
		after applying the BAU fuel-mix index unnormalized: multiplying the \
		shifted fuels by this keeps each pathway's total thermal intensity \
		unchanged, so the AEO trend changes the mix, not the efficiency.
	|

Cement Generic Process Energy by Fuel and Process[Industrial Fuel,industrial process end use\
		] :EXCEPT: [electricity if,industrial process end use]=
	ZIDZ(
	  SUM(
	    Output from Industrial Equipment Installed During Model Run[cement and other nonmetallic minerals 239\
		,industrial process end use,Model Run Vintage!]
	    * Last Year Industrial Equipment Fuel Type Shares in Electricity Equivalent by Vintage\
		[cement and other nonmetallic minerals 239,industrial process end use
	,Industrial Fuel,Model Run Vintage!]
	    * Last Year Energy Intensity of Industrial Production by Fuel and Vintage in Electricity Equivalent\
		[cement and other nonmetallic minerals 239,Industrial Fuel,industrial process end use\
		,Model Run Vintage!]
	  )

	  +

	  Output from New Industrial Equipment[cement and other nonmetallic minerals 239,industrial process end use\
		]
	  * Share of New Industrial Equipment by Fuel Type and Process in Electricity Equivalent\
		[cement and other nonmetallic minerals 239,Industrial Fuel
	,industrial process end use]
	  * Energy Intensity of Industrial Production by Fuel and Process for New Equipment in Electricity Equivalent\
		[cement and other nonmetallic minerals 239,Industrial Fuel,industrial process end use\
		]

	  +

	  Output from Preexisting Industrial Equipment[cement and other nonmetallic minerals 239\
		,industrial process end use]
	  * Preexisting Industrial Equipment Fuel Type Shares in Electricity Equivalent Adjusted by Early Retirement Policy\
		[cement and other nonmetallic minerals 239
	,industrial process end use,Industrial Fuel]
	  * Start Year Energy Intensity of Industrial Production in Electricity Equivalent[cement and other nonmetallic minerals 239\
		]
	  ,
	  1 - PIFURfE Percentage Industry Fuel Use Reduction for Electricity[cement and other nonmetallic minerals 239\
		,industrial process end use]
	) ~~|
Cement Generic Process Energy by Fuel and Process[electricity if,industrial process end use\
		]=
	(
	SUM(
	  Output from Industrial Equipment Installed During Model Run[cement and other nonmetallic minerals 239\
		,industrial process end use,Model Run Vintage!]
	  * Last Year Industrial Equipment Fuel Type Shares in Electricity Equivalent by Vintage\
		[cement and other nonmetallic minerals 239,industrial process end use
	,electricity if,Model Run Vintage!]
	  * Last Year Energy Intensity of Industrial Production by Fuel and Vintage in Electricity Equivalent\
		[cement and other nonmetallic minerals 239,electricity if,industrial process end use\
		,Model Run Vintage!]
	)

	+

	Output from New Industrial Equipment[cement and other nonmetallic minerals 239,industrial process end use\
		]
	* Share of New Industrial Equipment by Fuel Type and Process in Electricity Equivalent\
		[cement and other nonmetallic minerals 239,electricity if
	,industrial process end use]
	* Energy Intensity of Industrial Production by Fuel and Process for New Equipment in Electricity Equivalent\
		[cement and other nonmetallic minerals 239,electricity if,industrial process end use\
		]

	+

	Output from Preexisting Industrial Equipment[cement and other nonmetallic minerals 239\
		,industrial process end use]
	* Preexisting Industrial Equipment Fuel Type Shares in Electricity Equivalent Adjusted by Early Retirement Policy\
		[cement and other nonmetallic minerals 239
	,industrial process end use,electricity if]
	* Start Year Energy Intensity of Industrial Production in Electricity Equivalent[cement and other nonmetallic minerals 239\
		]
	)
	~	BTU/Year
	~	What the generic equipment-stock structure computes for 239's \
		process-end-use energy. Since the Phase 3a restructure scaled the data \
		spine, this IS the non-cement residual (lime, gypsum, ceramics) natively - \
		no share factor is applied here any more; the before-WHR 239 branch adds \
		the cement module's energy on top so WHR and process-efficiency levers act \
		on the blend (Dan's disposition). SYNC NOTE: deliberately DUPLICATES both \
		branches of Industrial Equipment Energy Use by Fuel Type and Process \
		before Waste Heat Recovery, evaluated at cement and other nonmetallic \
		minerals 239 - any edit to that equation must be mirrored here and in the \
		BAU twin. (Do NOT anchor any INITIAL to this variable: the init pass \
		evaluates the new-equipment output term as zero.) Generic levers \
		(electrification via PIFURfE, intensity standards, etc.) act on this \
		residual natively and never reach the module's share.
	|

Cement Import Share Adjustment Time=
	3
	~	years
	~	Years over which the cement import share moves toward its new level after \
		a border fee changes the import price: terminals, shipping contracts and \
		supply chains adjust over a few years rather than at once.
	|

Cement Import Share Response Factor=
	SMOOTH(Cement Import Share Target Response Factor, Cement Import Share Adjustment Time\
		)
	~	Dmnl
	~	The import share's actual ratio to BAU, moving toward the target over the \
		adjustment time. Exactly one with no fee.
	|

Cement Import Share Target Response Factor=
	IF THEN ELSE(BFoCDMbNI BAU Fraction of Clinker Demand Met by Net Imports > 0,
	  ZIDZ(1, (1 + (1 - BFoCDMbNI BAU Fraction of Clinker Demand Met by Net Imports)
	              / BFoCDMbNI BAU Fraction of Clinker Demand Met by Net Imports
	              * ZIDZ(CIBP Cement Import Delivered Price + Cement Border Fee per Ton Imported\
		, CIBP Cement Import Delivered Price)
	                ^ CIBP Cement Import Price Elasticity)
	       * BFoCDMbNI BAU Fraction of Clinker Demand Met by Net Imports),
	  1)
	~	Dmnl
	~	Ratio of the import share the border fee would settle at to the BAU import \
		share, from an Armington share equation calibrated to the BAU share: the \
		import-to-domestic ratio falls with the relative import price to the power \
		of the elasticity. One when there is no fee.
	|

Cement Imports in Cement Tons=
	Total Cement Demand * Fraction of Clinker Demand Met by Net Imports
	~	metric tons/Year
	~	Cement imported this year (the clinker-basis import share applied to \
		cement tons, as elsewhere in the module).
	|

Cement Intensity Standard Gap=
	IF THEN ELSE(Cement CO2 Intensity Standard This Year > 0,
	  MAX(0, Last Year Cement Fleet Net CO2 Intensity per Ton of Cement - Cement CO2 Intensity Standard Level This Year\
		),
	  0)
	~	kg CO2/metric ton
	~	How far last year's fleet intensity sits above this year's cap; zero when \
		the standard is off or already met.
	|

Cement Kiln Combustion CO2 Emissions=
	SUM(
	  Clinker Production by Pathway[Cement Production Pathway!]
	  * Cement Energy Intensity Multiplier by Pathway[Cement Production Pathway!]
	  * Cement Kiln Combustion CO2 per Ton of Clinker by Pathway[Cement Production Pathway\
		!]
	)
	~	g CO2
	~	CO2 from burning kiln fuel in the module's kilns this year, before any \
		capture, as industry 239's fuel accounts book it (clinker production times \
		combustion CO2 per ton, with the module's energy intensity multiplier). \
		The CCS sector takes it out of 239's energy-related emissions before \
		applying the generic capture fraction, so cement kilns are captured only \
		through the module's CCS pathway, as with process CO2.
	|

Cement Kiln Combustion CO2 per Ton of Clinker by Pathway[Cement Production Pathway]=
	SUM(
	  Cement Energy Intensity by Pathway and Fuel after Kiln Fuel Shift[Cement Production Pathway\
		,Industrial Fuel!]
	  * (1 - Cement Fuel Feedstock Share[Industrial Fuel!])
	  * Boolean Cement Fuel Present in Industry 239 Input Data[Industrial Fuel!]
	  * Industrial Fuel Pollutant Emissions Intensities This Year[cement and other nonmetallic minerals 239\
		,CO2,Industrial Fuel!]
	)
	~	g CO2/metric ton
	~	CO2 per ton of clinker from burning kiln fuel, as industry 239's fuel \
		accounts book it: the module's energy-purpose fuel (not the feedstock \
		share, and only fuels present in the 239 data) times 239's fuel emission \
		factors. Fleet-intensity basis. The fuel carbon the module books as \
		process CO2 is in Cement Fuel Carbon Booked as Process CO2 per Ton of \
		Clinker by Pathway instead.
	|

Cement Kiln Fossil Fuel Flag[Industrial Fuel] :EXCEPT: [hard coal if],[natural gas if\
		],[petroleum diesel if],[heavy or residual fuel oil if]=
	0 ~~|
Cement Kiln Fossil Fuel Flag[hard coal if]=
	1 ~~|
Cement Kiln Fossil Fuel Flag[natural gas if]=
	1 ~~|
Cement Kiln Fossil Fuel Flag[petroleum diesel if]=
	1 ~~|
Cement Kiln Fossil Fuel Flag[heavy or residual fuel oil if]=
	1
	~	Dmnl
	~	One for the fossil thermal kiln fuels the kiln fuel shift lever can \
		displace - coal, natural gas and the two petroleum elements that carry \
		petroleum coke (CEIbPaF) - zero for electricity, the biomass element that \
		carries the alternative fuels themselves, and fuels the kilns do not use. \
		Entered in Vensim, 2026-10-12.
	|

Cement Marginal Fuel Carbon Tax Adjustment per Ton of Clinker by Pathway[Cement Production Pathway\
		]=
	- (1 - Marginal Carbon Price Factor for Cement)
	* SUM(
	    Cement Energy Intensity by Pathway and Fuel after Kiln Fuel Shift[Cement Production Pathway\
		,Industrial Fuel!]
	    * (1 - Cement Fuel Feedstock Share[Industrial Fuel!])
	    * Industrial Fuel Carbon Tax Amount per Unit Energy[cement and other nonmetallic minerals 239\
		,Industrial Fuel!]
	  )
	~	$/metric ton
	~	Correction to the fuel term of the pathway operating costs when only part \
		of the carbon price acts on the margin: removes the share (1 minus the \
		marginal factor) of the carbon tax embedded in kiln fuel prices. Zero at \
		the default factor of one. Fleet-intensity basis; the new-build operating \
		cost scales it by CNBEIR.
	|

Cement Marginal Process Emissions Carbon Tax Rate=
	Cement Process Emissions Carbon Tax Rate * Marginal Carbon Price Factor for Cement
	~	$/metric ton CO2e
	~	Carbon price per tonne of process CO2 that cement producers weigh when \
		choosing a pathway: the effective process-emissions rate times the \
		marginal carbon price factor.
	|

Cement Module Change in CO2 Captured by Emissions Type[Emissions Type]=
	Cement Module CO2 Captured by Emissions Type[Emissions Type]
	- Last Year Cement Module CO2 Captured by Emissions Type[Emissions Type]
	~	g CO2
	~	Change since last year in the CO2 captured by the cement module's CCS \
		pathway, by emissions type. The CCS sector takes it out of industry 239's \
		growth in sequestered CO2 before sizing new capture equipment spending, \
		because the module pays for its capture equipment in its own capital costs.
	|

Cement Module CO2 Captured by Emissions Type[process emissions]=
	Cement Process CO2 Captured by Pathway Capture ~~|
Cement Module CO2 Captured by Emissions Type[energy related emissions]=
	Cement Combustion CO2 Captured by Pathway Capture
	~	g CO2
	~	CO2 captured this year by the cement module's CCS pathway, by emissions \
		type. The CCS sector subtracts it when working out industry 239's new \
		capture capacity, because the module already pays for this capture \
		equipment in its own capital costs.
	|

Cement Module Energy as Share of Generic Energy by Fuel[Industrial Fuel]=
	ZIDZ(
	  Cement Module Energy Use by Fuel Type[Industrial Fuel],
	  Industrial Fuel Use for Energy Purposes[Industrial Fuel,cement and other nonmetallic minerals 239\
		]
	)
	~	Dmnl
	~	Monitoring diagnostic (post-1b semantics): pathway-module energy by fuel \
		over 239's TOTAL energy-purpose fuel use, whose 239 process cells now \
		CONTAIN the module via the carve-out blend - so this reads module / \
		(module + residual + nonprocess), i.e. the module's realized share of the \
		blended total, not a pre-splice calibration gap. Heavy or residual fuel \
		oil still reads far above 1 by design (the module books petcoke there; the \
		model consumes it on the feedstock chain, outside this denominator). \
		Biomass reads 0 by ZIDZ (module-internal alternative fuels; see Boolean \
		Cement Fuel Present in Industry 239 Input Data).
	|

Cement Module Energy Use by Fuel Type[Industrial Fuel]=
	SUM(
	  Clinker Production by Pathway[Cement Production Pathway!]
	  * Cement Energy Intensity by Pathway and Fuel after Kiln Fuel Shift[Cement Production Pathway\
		!,Industrial Fuel]
	  * Cement Energy Intensity Multiplier by Pathway[Cement Production Pathway!]
	)
	~	BTU/Year
	~	Cement fuel use by fuel implied by the pathway module: clinker production \
		by pathway times energy intensity by pathway and fuel. It is split three \
		ways: the part added to industry 239's energy use (Cement Module Fuel \
		Added to Industry 239 Energy Use by Fuel Type), the part added to its \
		feedstock use (Cement Module Fuel Added to Industry 239 Feedstock Use by \
		Fuel Type), and alternative kiln fuels that stay inside the module (Cement \
		Module Fuel Not Added to Industry 239 Fuel Use This Year).
	|

Cement Module Fuel Added to Industry 239 Energy Use by Fuel Type[Industrial Fuel] :EXCEPT:\
		 [natural gas if],[hydrogen if]=
	Cement Module Energy Use by Fuel Type[Industrial Fuel]
	* (1 - Cement Fuel Feedstock Share[Industrial Fuel])
	* Boolean Cement Fuel Present in Industry 239 Input Data[Industrial Fuel] ~~|
Cement Module Fuel Added to Industry 239 Energy Use by Fuel Type[natural gas if]=
	Cement Module Energy Use by Fuel Type[natural gas if]
	* (1 - Cement Fuel Feedstock Share[natural gas if])
	* Boolean Cement Fuel Present in Industry 239 Input Data[natural gas if]
	+ Cement SCM Production Energy This Year ~~|
Cement Module Fuel Added to Industry 239 Energy Use by Fuel Type[hydrogen if]=
	Cement Module Energy Use by Fuel Type[hydrogen if]
	~	BTU/Year
	~	The part of the cement module's fuel use that is added to industry 239's \
		energy use (energy-purpose fuel, in the same accounts as the rest of \
		industry): the module's fuel use minus the feedstock share, for fuels \
		present in the 239 input data only (alternative kiln fuels stay inside the \
		module - see Boolean Cement Fuel Present in Industry 239 Input Data). The \
		natural gas branch also adds the energy to produce SCMs for policy-driven \
		clinker substitution (calcined-clay calcination), so substitution-heavy \
		scenarios pay their heat bill; zero at defaults and in BAU (the lever is \
		policy-only, so this is a policy-case difference, not a twin asymmetry). \
		The hydrogen branch adds the module's hydrogen kiln fuel in full, although \
		239's input data has none: it only exists when the kiln fuel shift lever \
		creates it, so start-year neutrality is not at stake, and adding it is \
		what prices it and lets the hydrogen sector see the demand.
	|

Cement Module Fuel Added to Industry 239 Feedstock Use by Fuel Type[Industrial Fuel]=
	Cement Module Energy Use by Fuel Type[Industrial Fuel]
	* Cement Fuel Feedstock Share[Industrial Fuel]
	~	BTU/Year
	~	The part of the cement module's fuel use that is added to industry 239's \
		feedstock (non-energy) fuel use - in practice the petcoke stream, which \
		the model carries on the heavy or residual fuel oil element and which the \
		239 input data books as feedstock. Its carbon is counted in cement process \
		CO2 (see Cement Process CO2 Intensity per Ton of Clinker by Pathway).
	|

Cement Module Fuel Not Added to Industry 239 Fuel Use This Year=
	SUM(Cement Module Energy Use by Fuel Type[Industrial Fuel!])
	- SUM(Cement Module Fuel Added to Industry 239 Energy Use by Fuel Type[Industrial Fuel\
		!])
	- SUM(Cement Module Fuel Added to Industry 239 Feedstock Use by Fuel Type[Industrial Fuel\
		!])
	+ Cement SCM Production Energy This Year
	~	BTU/Year
	~	Diagnostic (adversarial review, 2026-08-24): cement module fuel use that \
		is added to neither industry 239's energy use nor its feedstock use - the \
		fuels that Boolean Cement Fuel Present in Industry 239 Input Data leaves \
		out. At draft data this equals exactly the alternative-fuels (biomass) \
		energy, which stays inside the module by design pending the team's \
		alternative-fuels booking decision. Any OTHER nonzero amount means a \
		module fuel missing from the 239 input data was dropped by accident. Check \
		alongside Unserved Clinker Demand This Year in every validation run.
	|

Cement New Build Operating Cost per Ton of Clinker[Cement Production Pathway]=
	SUM(
	  Cement Energy Intensity by Pathway and Fuel after Kiln Fuel Shift[Cement Production Pathway\
		,Industrial Fuel!]
	  * CNBEIR Cement New Build Energy Intensity Ratio[Cement Production Pathway]
	  * (
	    Industrial Fuel Cost per Unit Energy[cement and other nonmetallic minerals 239,Industrial Fuel\
		!]
	    - Cement Fuel Feedstock Share[Industrial Fuel!]
	      * Industrial Fuel Carbon Tax Amount per Unit Energy[cement and other nonmetallic minerals 239\
		,Industrial Fuel!]
	  )
	)
	+ Cement New Build Process CO2 Intensity per Ton of Clinker by Pathway[Cement Production Pathway\
		]
	  / Grams per Metric Ton CO2e
	  * Cement Marginal Process Emissions Carbon Tax Rate
	+ CNBEIR Cement New Build Energy Intensity Ratio[Cement Production Pathway]
	  * Cement Marginal Fuel Carbon Tax Adjustment per Ton of Clinker by Pathway[Cement Production Pathway\
		]
	+ Cement CCS Capture Operating Cost per Ton of Clinker[Cement Production Pathway]
	~	$/metric ton
	~	Operating cost per ton for a NEW-BUILD plant: fuel at the new-build \
		(best-available-technology) intensity, CNBEIR x CEIbPaF, with the same \
		feedstock carbon-tax netting and process-CO2 term as the fleet operating \
		cost. Used in the new-build levelized cost, so the overhaul-vs-replace \
		decision correctly sees that a replacement plant is more efficient than \
		the kiln it replaces (the overhaul continuation cost keeps the \
		fleet-typical intensity - an overhaul does not rebuild the kiln to BAT). \
		SYNC NOTE: mirrors Cement Pathway Operating Cost per Ton of Clinker with \
		the CNBEIR factor - mirror edits.
	|

Cement New Build Process CO2 Intensity per Ton of Clinker by Pathway[Cement Production Pathway\
		]=
	CCF Calcination CO2 Factor by Pathway[Cement Production Pathway]
	+ CNBEIR Cement New Build Energy Intensity Ratio[Cement Production Pathway]
	  * (1 - Cement Pathway CO2 Capture Fraction[Cement Production Pathway])
	  * Cement Fuel Carbon Booked as Process CO2 per Ton of Clinker by Pathway[Cement Production Pathway\
		]
	~	g CO2/metric ton
	~	Process CO2 per ton of clinker for a new-build plant after any pathway \
		capture: calcination (CCF, which does not depend on kiln efficiency) plus \
		the uncaptured share of the kiln fuel carbon booked as process CO2, scaled \
		by the new-build energy intensity ratio because a \
		best-available-technology kiln burns proportionally less fuel. Used in the \
		new-build operating cost, so its carbon tax term matches the CNBEIR-scaled \
		fuel term.
	|

Cement Overhaul Capital Cost per Unit Annual Capacity after RnD[Cement Production Pathway\
		]=
	CPCaOC Overhaul Capital Cost per Unit Annual Capacity[Cement Production Pathway]
	* (1 - RnD Cement Pathway Capital Cost Perc Reduction This Year[Cement Production Pathway\
		])
	~	$/(metric tons/Year)
	~	Kiln overhaul capital cost per unit of annual capacity after the cement \
		pathway R&D capital-cost lever (policy case). Feeds the overhaul \
		continuation cost, the capital booking and the financing ladders; the BAU \
		twins keep CPCaOC.
	|

Cement Overhaul Capital Recovery Factor[Cement Production Pathway]= INITIAL(
	ZIDZ(
	  BCoISC Weighted Average Cost of Capital by Industry[cement and other nonmetallic minerals 239\
		]
	  * (1 + BCoISC Weighted Average Cost of Capital by Industry[cement and other nonmetallic minerals 239\
		])
	    ^ (CPPL Cement Pathway Campaign Length[Cement Production Pathway] / One Year),
	  (1 + BCoISC Weighted Average Cost of Capital by Industry[cement and other nonmetallic minerals 239\
		])
	    ^ (CPPL Cement Pathway Campaign Length[Cement Production Pathway] / One Year)
	  - 1
	))
	~	1/Year
	~	Capital recovery factor for kiln overhaul capital: the industry WACC \
		annuitized over one campaign length (an overhaul buys one more campaign, \
		so its amortization window is CPPL, not the site asset life).
	|

Cement Overhaul Continuation Cost per Ton of Clinker[Cement Production Pathway]=
	Cement Overhaul Capital Cost per Unit Annual Capacity after RnD[Cement Production Pathway\
		]
	* Cement Overhaul Capital Recovery Factor[Cement Production Pathway]
	/ MAX(0.05, MIN(CUUB Cement Utilization Upper Bound, Cement Expected Utilization for Investment Decisions\
		))
	+ Cement Pathway Operating Cost per Ton of Clinker[Cement Production Pathway]
	~	$/metric ton
	~	Cost per ton of overhauling and running one more campaign: overhaul \
		capital annuitized at the industry WACC over the campaign (Phase 3b) per \
		ton produced at the fleet's EXPECTED utilization (recent utilization \
		smoothed over Cement Utilization Smoothing Time, clamped to [0.05, CUUB]; \
		2026-10-01, replaces this year's utilization): a slack fleet spreads the \
		overhaul annuity over fewer tons, raising per-ton continuation cost and \
		retirement pressure as a slump persists, without scrapping kilns in a \
		one-year dip. Plus operating cost including carbon exposure. Compared \
		against the cost of replacement capacity in the overhaul-vs-replace \
		choice, which uses the same expected utilization.
	|

Cement Overhaul Share at Decision[Cement Production Pathway]=
	ZIDZ(
	  CRSW Cement Overhaul Shareweight[Cement Production Pathway]
	  * (
	      MAX(Cement Overhaul Continuation Cost per Ton of Clinker[Cement Production Pathway\
		], 0.01)
	      / MAX(Cement Replacement Alternative Cost per Ton of Clinker, 0.01)
	    ) ^ IELC Industrial Equipment Logit Coefficient,
	  CRSW Cement Overhaul Shareweight[Cement Production Pathway]
	  * (
	      MAX(Cement Overhaul Continuation Cost per Ton of Clinker[Cement Production Pathway\
		], 0.01)
	      / MAX(Cement Replacement Alternative Cost per Ton of Clinker, 0.01)
	    ) ^ IELC Industrial Equipment Logit Coefficient
	  + 1
	)
	~	Dmnl
	~	Share of decision-facing capacity that overhauls rather than retiring: a \
		two-option relative-cost logit (overhaul continuation cost vs the \
		replacement alternative - a spare-capacity-weighted blend of fleet \
		marginal cost and greenfield cost since 2026-08-27, see Cement Replacement \
		Alternative Cost per Ton - at the same exponent as the pathway choice), \
		with the CRSW calibration weight on the overhaul branch so default costs \
		reproduce observed kiln longevity. A deterministic threshold rule is the \
		logged alternative form, pending team preference (steel open question 3).
	|

Cement Pathway Capital Cost per Unit Annual Capacity after Subsidy[Cement Production Pathway\
		]=
	Cement Pathway Capital Cost per Unit Annual Capacity Including Capture Equipment[Cement Production Pathway\
		]
	* (1 - Cement Pathway Capital Subsidy Rate[Cement Production Pathway])
	~	$/(metric tons/Year)
	~	Greenfield capital cost per unit of annual clinker capacity net of the BAU \
		and policy pathway capital subsidies: the capital cost the investor sees \
		when choosing a pathway. Used only in the levelized cost for the pathway \
		choice; the capital booking and the financing ladders carry the gross \
		cost, and the subsidized share is paid by government through Cement \
		Pathway Investment Subsidy Amount Paid.
	|

Cement Pathway Capital Cost per Unit Annual Capacity Including Capture Equipment[Cement Production Pathway\
		] :EXCEPT: [electric kiln with CCS]=
	CPCaOC New Build Capital Cost per Unit Annual Capacity[Cement Production Pathway]
	* (1 - RnD Cement Pathway Capital Cost Perc Reduction This Year[Cement Production Pathway\
		])
	+ SUM(Cement CCS Captured CO2 per Ton of Clinker by Emissions Type[Cement Production Pathway\
		,Emissions Type!])
	  / Grams per Metric Ton CO2e
	  * CC Capital Cost of Eqpt to Sequester One Ton of Industry CO2 per Year[cement and other nonmetallic minerals 239\
		,process emissions] ~~|
Cement Pathway Capital Cost per Unit Annual Capacity Including Capture Equipment[electric kiln with CCS\
		]=
	CPCaOC New Build Capital Cost per Unit Annual Capacity[electric kiln with CCS]
	* (1 - RnD Cement Pathway Capital Cost Perc Reduction This Year[electric kiln with CCS\
		])
	+ CDSC Direct Separation Capture Unit Capital Cost
	~	$/(metric tons/Year)
	~	Greenfield capital per ton per year of clinker capacity including, for the \
		CCS pathway, the capture equipment, priced from the CCS sector's capital \
		cost per ton of CO2 captured per year for industry 239's process \
		emissions. That figure is the cement-plant capture cost, which covers the \
		kiln's whole flue gas, so it applies to both the captured process CO2 and \
		the captured kiln-fuel CO2 (one capture unit on one stack). Used by the \
		levelized pathway cost and by the module's capital and financing cash \
		flows; the CCS sector leaves these tons out of its own capture-equipment \
		spending so the equipment is paid for once. Kiln capital is reduced by the \
		cement pathway R&D capital-cost lever (policy case only); capture \
		equipment carries the CCS sector's own R&D lever.
	|

Cement Pathway Capital Recovery Factor[Cement Production Pathway]=
	ZIDZ(
	  Cement Pathway Cost of Capital[Cement Production Pathway]
	  * (1 + Cement Pathway Cost of Capital[Cement Production Pathway])
	    ^ (CPAL Cement Pathway Asset Life[Cement Production Pathway] / One Year),
	  (1 + Cement Pathway Cost of Capital[Cement Production Pathway])
	    ^ (CPAL Cement Pathway Asset Life[Cement Production Pathway] / One Year)
	  - 1
	)
	~	1/Year
	~	Capital recovery factor for greenfield cement pathway capital: the \
		industry WACC annuitized over the pathway asset life. The hurdle rate \
		premium (HRPfIEP) is deliberately excluded: it is an equipment-adoption \
		behavioral parameter calibrated on small purchases against a 20 percent \
		adoption target, not a plant-scale investment criterion - including it \
		would triple the effective discount rate on billion-dollar facilities \
		(steel P24 convention, flagged for team review alongside the CRSW \
		calibration). Since 2026-10-12 the policy case uses Cement Pathway Cost of \
		Capital, which carries the clean-equipment cost-of-capital lever for \
		qualifying pathways, so this is no longer an initial-time constant; the \
		BAU twin keeps BCoISC.
	|

Cement Pathway Capital Subsidy Rate[Cement Production Pathway]=
	MIN(1,
	  BCPCS BAU Cement Pathway Capital Subsidy[Cement Production Pathway]
	  + Perc Subsidy for Cement Pathway Capital Cost This Year[Cement Production Pathway\
		]
	  + Cement Pathway Clean Heat Investment Subsidy Rate[Cement Production Pathway]
	)
	~	Dmnl
	~	Fraction of the greenfield capital cost of new clinker capacity that \
		government pays in the policy case: the BAU subsidy plus the cement \
		pathway capital subsidy lever plus the clean industrial heat investment \
		credit for electric kilns, capped at the whole cost.
	|

Cement Pathway Choice Weight[Cement Production Pathway]=
	10000
	* CPSW Cement Pathway Shareweight[Cement Production Pathway]
	* (
	  MAX(Cement Pathway Levelized Cost per Ton of Clinker[Cement Production Pathway], 0.01\
		)
	  / MAX(Minimum Eligible Cement Pathway Cost per Ton of Clinker, 0.01)
	) ^ IELC Industrial Equipment Logit Coefficient
	~	Dmnl
	~	Relative-cost (power function) logit weight for new clinker capacity by \
		pathway, cloning the Industrial Equipment Fuel Choice Weight form (and \
		steel's pathway-logit clone of it) and reusing its exponent (IELC). Cost \
		floors are rescaled to per-ton magnitudes (0.01 $/ton versus 1e-7 $/BTU in \
		the fuel-choice version).
	|

Cement Pathway Clean Heat Investment Subsidy Rate[Cement Production Pathway]=
	Cement Pathway Qualifies for Clean Heat Subsidies[Cement Production Pathway]
	* IFTQfS Industrial Fuel Types Qualifying for Clean Heat Investment Subsidy[electricity if\
		]
	* (BCHSR BAU Clean Heat Investment Subsidy Rate[heat above 1000 C]
	   + Perc Subsidy for Clean Industrial Heat Equipment This Year[heat above 1000 C])
	~	Dmnl
	~	Share of an electric kiln's capital cost paid by the clean industrial heat \
		investment tax credit (BAU rate plus the ITC lever, heat above 1000 C \
		band), when electricity qualifies. Zero for fuel-burning kilns. Stacked \
		with the pathway capital subsidies in Cement Pathway Capital Subsidy Rate.
	|

Cement Pathway Clean Heat Production Subsidy per Ton of Clinker[Cement Production Pathway\
		]=
	Cement Pathway Qualifies for Clean Heat Subsidies[Cement Production Pathway]
	* SUM(
	    Cement Energy Intensity by Pathway and Fuel after Kiln Fuel Shift[Cement Production Pathway\
		,Industrial Fuel!]
	    * Clean Industrial Heat Subsidy Rate by Fuel Type[Industrial Fuel!,heat above 1000 C\
		]
	  )
	~	$/metric ton
	~	Clean industrial heat production tax credit an electric kiln earns per \
		tonne of clinker (BAU rate plus the PTC lever, heat above 1000 C band, on \
		the qualifying fuels it uses), on the fleet-intensity basis. Zero for \
		fuel-burning kilns. Paid for the PTC duration (BCHPD) after a kiln is \
		built.
	|

Cement Pathway CO2 Capture Fraction[Cement Production Pathway] :EXCEPT: [Cement Capture Pathway\
		]=
	0 ~~|
Cement Pathway CO2 Capture Fraction[Cement Capture Pathway]=
	ZIDZ(
	  CCF Calcination CO2 Factor by Pathway[dry kiln with precalciner]
	  - CCF Calcination CO2 Factor by Pathway[Cement Capture Pathway],
	  CCF Calcination CO2 Factor by Pathway[dry kiln with precalciner]
	)
	~	Dmnl
	~	Share of the kiln's CO2 that each capture pathway catches, read from CCF: \
		the gap between the uncaptured calcination factor (dry kiln with \
		precalciner; the electric kiln calcines the same raw meal) and the \
		pathway's net factor, as a share of the uncaptured factor. The same share \
		applies to the kiln-fuel CO2 (a post-combustion unit treats the whole flue \
		gas; the electric kiln burns no fuel). Zero for pathways without capture.
	|

Cement Pathway Cost of Capital[Cement Production Pathway]=
	BCoISC Weighted Average Cost of Capital by Industry[cement and other nonmetallic minerals 239\
		]
	* (1 - Perc Decrease in Cost of Capital for Clean Industrial Equipment This Year[heat above 1000 C\
		,cement and other nonmetallic minerals 239]
	  * Cement Pathway Qualifies for Clean Equipment Financing[Cement Production Pathway\
		])
	~	Dmnl
	~	Cost of capital for greenfield cement pathway capital in the policy case: \
		the industry WACC (BCoISC) reduced by the clean industrial equipment \
		cost-of-capital lever (the heat above 1000 C process band of industry 239) \
		for the pathways that qualify as clean equipment. Mirrors Cost of Capital \
		by Industry Fuel and Process. Added 2026-10-12.
	|

Cement Pathway Investment Subsidy Amount Paid=
	SUM(
	  Cement Pathway Capital Subsidy Rate[Cement Production Pathway!]
	  * Cement Pathway Capital Cost per Unit Annual Capacity Including Capture Equipment\
		[Cement Production Pathway!]
	  * New Clinker Production Capacity by Pathway[Cement Production Pathway!]
	)
	~	$
	~	Government payments covering the subsidized fraction of this year's new \
		clinker capacity on each pathway (BAU subsidy plus the cement pathway \
		capital subsidy lever), at the gross capital cost including capture \
		equipment. The capital booking in New Industrial Equipment Capital Cost \
		stays gross; this subsidy is booked separately through Industry Sector \
		Change in Government Subsidy Amount Paid, on the clean industrial heat \
		subsidy pattern.
	|

Cement Pathway Levelized Cost per Ton of Clinker[Cement Production Pathway]=
	Cement Pathway Capital Cost per Unit Annual Capacity after Subsidy[Cement Production Pathway\
		]
	* Cement Pathway Capital Recovery Factor[Cement Production Pathway]
	/ MAX(0.05, MIN(CUUB Cement Utilization Upper Bound, Cement Expected Utilization for Investment Decisions\
		))
	+ Cement New Build Operating Cost per Ton of Clinker[Cement Production Pathway]
	- Cement Expected Production Support per Ton of Clinker by Pathway[Cement Production Pathway\
		]
	~	$/metric ton
	~	New-build pathway choice cost: greenfield capital annuitized at the \
		industry WACC over the pathway asset life, per ton produced at the fleet's \
		EXPECTED utilization (same smoothed, clamped utilization the overhaul \
		continuation cost uses; 2026-10-01, replaces the fixed SYCCU norm so both \
		sides of the overhaul-or-replace comparison stand on one basis), plus the \
		NEW-BUILD operating cost (fuel at best-available-technology intensity via \
		CNBEIR, with feedstock carbon-tax netting and the process-CO2 tax - the \
		CCS pathway's net CCF gives it the capture credit). The overhaul \
		continuation cost keeps the fleet-typical operating cost, so replacement \
		correctly looks more efficient than rebuilding the old kiln. Non-energy \
		variable costs (raw meal, labor) omitted: shared components cancel in the \
		cost-ratio logit. Capital is net of the cement pathway capital subsidy, \
		and the capital recovery factor carries the clean-equipment \
		cost-of-capital lever for qualifying pathways.
	|

Cement Pathway Net CO2 Intensity per Ton of Cement[Cement Production Pathway]=
	Cement Pathway Net CO2 Intensity per Ton of Clinker[Cement Production Pathway]
	* Clinker to Cement Ratio
	/ Grams per Metric Ton CO2e * Kilograms per Metric Ton
	~	kg CO2/metric ton
	~	Direct CO2 per tonne of cement made from each pathway's clinker at this \
		year's clinker-to-cement ratio, in kilograms: the basis the low-carbon \
		procurement threshold and the production tax credit threshold are written \
		in (Buy Clean limits and near-zero definitions are per tonne of cement).
	|

Cement Pathway Net CO2 Intensity per Ton of Clinker[Cement Production Pathway]=
	Cement Process CO2 Intensity per Ton of Clinker by Pathway[Cement Production Pathway\
		]
	+ Cement Kiln Combustion CO2 per Ton of Clinker by Pathway[Cement Production Pathway\
		]
	  * (1 - Cement Pathway CO2 Capture Fraction[Cement Production Pathway])
	~	g CO2/metric ton
	~	Direct CO2 a kiln on each pathway releases per tonne of clinker after any \
		capture: post-capture process CO2 plus kiln-fuel combustion CO2 net of the \
		pathway's capture share. Scope-1 basis (electricity used by electric kilns \
		is counted in the power sector), the convention emissions trading systems, \
		product standards and contracts for difference use. Fleet-intensity basis.
	|

Cement Pathway Operating Cost per Ton of Clinker[Cement Production Pathway]=
	SUM(
	  Cement Energy Intensity by Pathway and Fuel after Kiln Fuel Shift[Cement Production Pathway\
		,Industrial Fuel!]
	  * (
	    Industrial Fuel Cost per Unit Energy[cement and other nonmetallic minerals 239,Industrial Fuel\
		!]
	    - Cement Fuel Feedstock Share[Industrial Fuel!]
	      * Industrial Fuel Carbon Tax Amount per Unit Energy[cement and other nonmetallic minerals 239\
		,Industrial Fuel!]
	  )
	)
	+ Cement Process CO2 Intensity per Ton of Clinker by Pathway[Cement Production Pathway\
		]
	  / Grams per Metric Ton CO2e
	  * Cement Marginal Process Emissions Carbon Tax Rate
	+ Cement Marginal Fuel Carbon Tax Adjustment per Ton of Clinker by Pathway[Cement Production Pathway\
		]
	+ Cement CCS Capture Operating Cost per Ton of Clinker[Cement Production Pathway]
	~	$/metric ton
	~	Non-capital cost per ton of clinker by pathway: fuel costs (with the \
		fuel-price carbon tax netted off feedstock quantities - petcoke is priced \
		through the process-CO2 term instead) plus the carbon tax on process CO2. \
		Shared by the new-build levelized cost and the overhaul continuation cost. \
		With the kiln fuel shift lever on, the fuel term uses the post-shift mix; \
		alternative fuels ride the biomass element and are priced at its price \
		(their fossil-derived CO2 is in the process-CO2 term).
	|

Cement Pathway Qualifies as Low Carbon[Cement Production Pathway]=
	IF THEN ELSE(Cement Pathway Net CO2 Intensity per Ton of Cement[Cement Production Pathway\
		]
	  <= LCPT Low Carbon Procurement Threshold as Share of Fleet CO2 Intensity * Last Year Cement Fleet Net CO2 Intensity per Ton of Cement\
		, 1, 0)
	~	Dmnl
	~	One for the pathways whose cement counts as low-carbon this year: at or \
		below the LCPT share of last year's fleet average intensity. These are the \
		kilns low-carbon procurement buys from and the kilns the procurement lever \
		and the intensity standard require new capacity to be built as.
	|

Cement Pathway Qualifies for Clean Equipment Financing[Cement Production Pathway]=
	0,	{dry kiln with precalciner}
	1,	{dry kiln with CCS}
	1,	{electric kiln}
	1,	{electric kiln with CCS}
	1	{alternative chemistry cement}
	~	Dmnl
	~	One for the clinker production pathways that count as clean industrial \
		equipment for the cost-of-capital lever (Perc Decrease in Cost of Capital \
		for Clean Industrial Equipment): the kiln with carbon capture, the \
		electric kiln and alternative chemistry cement. Zero for the conventional \
		dry kiln. Mirrors the generic rule, where electricity and clean hydrogen \
		equipment qualify (IFTQfS), with capture added as the cement-specific \
		clean option. Entered in Vensim, 2026-10-12.
	|

Cement Pathway Qualifies for Clean Heat Subsidies[Cement Production Pathway]=
	0,	{dry kiln with precalciner}
	0,	{dry kiln with CCS}
	1,	{electric kiln}
	1,	{electric kiln with CCS}
	1	{alternative chemistry cement}
	~	Dmnl
	~	One for the clinker production pathways whose kiln is clean industrial \
		heat equipment (electrically heated kilns and calciners), so the clean \
		industrial heat investment and production subsidies (ITC and PTC, BAU \
		rates and levers, for the heat above 1000 C band and qualifying fuels) \
		reach them. The fuel-burning kilns do not qualify. Entered in Vensim.
	|

Cement Pathway Qualifies for Production Tax Credit[Cement Production Pathway]=
	IF THEN ELSE(Cement Pathway Net CO2 Intensity per Ton of Cement[Cement Production Pathway\
		]
	  <= LCCT Low Carbon Cement Credit Threshold as Share of Fleet CO2 Intensity * Last Year Cement Fleet Net CO2 Intensity per Ton of Cement\
		, 1, 0)
	~	Dmnl
	~	One for the pathways whose cement meets the production credit threshold \
		this year: at or below the threshold share of last year's fleet average \
		intensity.
	|

Cement Petroleum Coke CO2 Emissions Intensity=
	0.10241
	~	g CO2/BTU
	~	Petroleum coke combustion CO2 intensity (EPA Emission Factors Hub 2025: \
		102.41 kg CO2 per MMBtu). The module's petcoke rides EPS's petroleum \
		diesel and residual-oil elements (matching how BIFU files AEO's petroleum \
		coke and 'other petroleum' for industry 239 - deliberate, by Btu \
		intensity), whose factors are 74-75 kg/MMBtu; the difference is added to \
		the kiln's process CO2 so petcoke emits at its own factor. Entered in \
		Vensim, 2026-10-01.
	|

Cement Petroleum Fuel Flag[Industrial Fuel] :EXCEPT: [petroleum diesel if],[heavy or residual fuel oil if\
		]=
	0 ~~|
Cement Petroleum Fuel Flag[petroleum diesel if]=
	1 ~~|
Cement Petroleum Fuel Flag[heavy or residual fuel oil if]=
	1
	~	Dmnl
	~	One for the two EPS fuel elements that carry the cement module's petroleum \
		coke (petroleum diesel as energy, residual oil as feedstock, following \
		BIFU's labelling), zero otherwise. Selects the fuels that get the petcoke \
		emission-factor correction.
	|

Cement Process CO2 Captured by Pathway Capture=
	SUM(
	  Clinker Production by Pathway[Cement Production Pathway!]
	  * Cement CCS Captured Process CO2 per Ton of Clinker[Cement Production Pathway!])
	~	g CO2
	~	Process CO2 captured this year by the module's CCS pathway. Added to \
		industry 239's sequestered process CO2 in the CCS sector, so \
		transport-and-storage costs, 45Q-type subsidies, capture O&M and capture \
		energy are booked once by the generic CCS machinery (the module books the \
		capture capital itself).
	|

Cement Process CO2 Emissions=
	SUM(
	  Clinker Production by Pathway[Cement Production Pathway!]
	  * (Cement Process CO2 Intensity per Ton of Clinker by Pathway[Cement Production Pathway\
		!]
	     + Cement CCS Captured Process CO2 per Ton of Clinker[Cement Production Pathway!\
		])
	)
	~	g CO2
	~	GROSS process CO2 from the module's kilns this year: calcination plus the \
		petcoke and fossil-alternative-fuel corrections, BEFORE pathway capture \
		(the CCS pathway's captured tons are added back here and removed again in \
		the CCS sector's sequestered-tons chain, so Process Emissions after CCS \
		nets them once). The per-ton intensity used in the cost logits stays net \
		of capture. Feeds Process Emissions before CCS for industry 239 alongside \
		the lime and other-minerals residual.
	|

Cement Process CO2 Intensity per Ton of Clinker by Pathway[Cement Production Pathway]\
		=
	CCF Calcination CO2 Factor by Pathway[Cement Production Pathway]
	+ (1 - Cement Pathway CO2 Capture Fraction[Cement Production Pathway])
	  * Cement Fuel Carbon Booked as Process CO2 per Ton of Clinker by Pathway[Cement Production Pathway\
		]
	~	g CO2/metric ton
	~	Process CO2 per ton of clinker that the kiln still emits after any pathway \
		capture: calcination (CCF, already net of capture for the CCS pathway) \
		plus the uncaptured share of the kiln fuel carbon booked as process CO2 \
		(petcoke feedstock carbon, petcoke emission-factor correction, fossil \
		alternative fuels). Used in the operating-cost carbon tax term and, with \
		captured tons added back, in the gross process emissions. Fleet-intensity \
		basis.
	|

Cement Process Emissions Carbon Tax Rate=
	IF THEN ELSE(
	  Does Carbon Tax Exempt Process Emissions This Year[cement and other nonmetallic minerals 239\
		] > 0,
	  0,
	  Carbon Tax Rate[industry sector]
	  * Fraction of Industry Category Subject to Carbon Tax[cement and other nonmetallic minerals 239\
		]
	  * Industry Pollutants Taxed by Carbon Pricing[cement and other nonmetallic minerals 239\
		,CO2]
	)
	~	$/metric ton CO2e
	~	Effective carbon tax rate on cement process CO2 for the pathway choice, \
		mirroring Carbon Tax on Process Emissions by Industry's gating (BEPEfCT \
		exemption, coverage fraction, pollutant coverage).
	|

Cement Production Pathway:
	dry kiln with precalciner,
	dry kiln with CCS,
	electric kiln,
	electric kiln with CCS,
	alternative chemistry cement
	~	
	~	Clinker/cement production pathways. Pre-commercial routes (CCS, electric \
		kiln, electric kiln with CCS, alternative chemistry) are gated by the CPSW \
		shareweight time series, not by structure.
	|

Cement Production Support Offered per Ton of Clinker by Program and Pathway[clean heat production credit\
		,Cement Production Pathway]=
	CNBEIR Cement New Build Energy Intensity Ratio[Cement Production Pathway]
	* Cement Pathway Clean Heat Production Subsidy per Ton of Clinker[Cement Production Pathway\
		] ~~|
Cement Production Support Offered per Ton of Clinker by Program and Pathway[low carbon production credit\
		,Cement Production Pathway]=
	Low Carbon Cement Credit per Ton of Clinker[Cement Production Pathway] ~~|
Cement Production Support Offered per Ton of Clinker by Program and Pathway[carbon contract for difference\
		,Cement Production Pathway]=
	Cement CCfD Payment per Ton CO2 Abated by Pathway[Cement Production Pathway]
	* Cement CCfD Abatement per Ton of Clinker by Pathway[Cement Production Pathway]
	~	$/metric ton
	~	What each program pays per ton of clinker to a kiln built this year: the \
		clean heat production credit at new-build energy intensity, the low-carbon \
		production credit, and the CCfD payment at this year's strike times the \
		tons of CO2 a ton of clinker avoids. A kiln enters a program when this is \
		nonzero.
	|

Cement Production Support Paid by Program[Cement Production Support Program]=
	SUM(
	  MIN(Last Year Clinker Capacity Receiving Production Support by Program and Pathway\
		[Cement Production Support Program,Cement Production Pathway!]
	      + Clinker Capacity Entering Production Support by Program and Pathway[Cement Production Support Program\
		,Cement Production Pathway!],
	      Clinker Production Capacity Added During Model Run by Pathway[Cement Production Pathway\
		!])
	  * ZIDZ(Clinker Production by Pathway[Cement Production Pathway!], Clinker Production Capacity by Pathway\
		[Cement Production Pathway!])
	  * Cement Production Support Paid per Ton of Clinker by Program and Pathway[Cement Production Support Program\
		,Cement Production Pathway!]
	)
	~	$
	~	Payments to the cement industry under each production support program this \
		year: capacity inside the term (capped by the surviving model-run stock) \
		run at the fleet utilization, times the per-ton payment. Government to \
		industry 239.
	|

Cement Production Support Paid per Ton of Clinker by Program and Pathway[Cement Production Support Program\
		,Cement Production Pathway] :EXCEPT: [carbon contract for difference,Cement Production Pathway\
		]=
	Cement Production Support Offered per Ton of Clinker by Program and Pathway[Cement Production Support Program\
		,Cement Production Pathway] ~~|
Cement Production Support Paid per Ton of Clinker by Program and Pathway[carbon contract for difference\
		,Cement Production Pathway]=
	Cement CCfD Payment per Ton CO2 Abated on Contracted Kilns by Pathway[Cement Production Pathway\
		]
	* Cement CCfD Abatement per Ton of Clinker by Pathway[Cement Production Pathway]
	~	$/metric ton
	~	What each program pays this year per ton of clinker from kilns inside its \
		term. The tax credits pay the rate in force this year; contracts for \
		difference pay the strike price locked in when they were signed (averaged \
		over the contracted kilns), less this year's carbon price.
	|

Cement Production Support Program:
	clean heat production credit,
	low carbon production credit,
	carbon contract for difference
	~	
	~	Per-ton payments to new low-carbon kilns for a fixed term after they are \
		built: the clean industrial heat production tax credit, the low-carbon \
		cement production tax credit and the carbon contract for difference. They \
		share one structure: qualifying new capacity enters the program, stays for \
		the program term, and is paid each year on what it produces.
	|

Cement Production Support Term by Program[clean heat production credit]=
	BCHPD BAU Clean Heat PTC Duration ~~|
Cement Production Support Term by Program[low carbon production credit]=
	Low Carbon Cement Credit Duration ~~|
Cement Production Support Term by Program[carbon contract for difference]=
	Cement CCfD Contract Length
	~	years
	~	Years a new kiln is paid under each production support program after it is \
		built: the clean heat production tax credit duration, the low-carbon \
		cement credit duration and the CCfD contract length.
	|

Cement Replacement Alternative Cost per Ton of Clinker=
	(1 - Cement Replacement Blend Weight)
	* MIN(
	    Cement Fleet Marginal Operating Cost per Ton of Clinker,
	    Average New Clinker Capacity Cost per Ton of Clinker)
	+ Cement Replacement Blend Weight
	  * Average New Clinker Capacity Cost per Ton of Clinker
	~	$/metric ton
	~	The cost of the RETIRE alternative in the overhaul-or-replace decision: a \
		tightness-weighted blend of the fleet's marginal operating cost (a slack \
		market absorbs the retiring kiln's output with sunk capital) and the \
		new-build levelized cost (a tight market must build). The blend weight \
		comes from the CRBWvT curve, which responds smoothly as the fleet \
		approaches either edge of its historical utilization range. The MIN keeps \
		a slack market from ever pricing absorption above new-build cost.
	|

Cement Replacement Blend Weight=
	CRBWvT Cement Replacement Blend Weight vs Tightness[average summer day](
	  Cement Fleet Tightness)
	~	Dmnl
	~	Greenfield weight in the retire-alternative cost blend, read off the \
		tightness curve: 0 at the bottom of the utilization envelope (a slack \
		market absorbs a retiring kiln's output with sunk capital), 1 at the top \
		(a tight market must build).
	|

Cement Residual Share of Process Energy[industrial process end use]= INITIAL(
	ZIDZ(
	  SUM(
	    BIFU BAU Industrial Fuel Use for Energy[cement and other nonmetallic minerals 239\
		,Industrial Fuel!]
	    * BAU Percentage Fuel Use by Industrial Process with Fallback Data[cement and other nonmetallic minerals 239\
		,Industrial Fuel!,industrial process end use]
	    * (1 - Cement Carveout Share of Energy by Fuel[Industrial Fuel!])
	  ),
	  SUM(
	    BIFU BAU Industrial Fuel Use for Energy[cement and other nonmetallic minerals 239\
		,Industrial Fuel!]
	    * BAU Percentage Fuel Use by Industrial Process with Fallback Data[cement and other nonmetallic minerals 239\
		,Industrial Fuel!,industrial process end use]
	  )
	))
	~	Dmnl
	~	The non-cement residual's share of each 239 process end use's start-year \
		energy (the energy-weighted mean of one minus the per-fuel carveout \
		shares). Used to NORMALIZE the residual fuel- shareweight rescale to mean \
		one within each process: dividing the (1 - share) fuel-weight scaling by \
		this leaves fuel-vs-fuel shares identical (any positive per-process scalar \
		cancels in the logit) while keeping the thermal battery weight - an \
		unscaled thirteenth competitor in the same denominator - at its calibrated \
		relative position (3a adversarial-review finding: without normalization \
		TB's 239 share inflated up to
	|

Cement SCM Production Energy This Year=
	Fraction of Additional Clinker Substitution This Year
	* Domestic Cement Production
	* SCMEI SCM Production Energy per Ton of Substitution
	~	BTU/Year
	~	Thermal energy to produce the extra SCM tonnage that displaces clinker \
		under the substitution lever (calcined-clay calcination), added to the \
		module's natural gas use in industry 239's energy accounts so \
		substitution-heavy scenarios pay their heat bill. SCM tonnage = \
		substitution fraction x cement demand on the domestic-production basis \
		(consumption x (1 - net-import share), the clinker-basis share applied to \
		cement tons as an approximation), i.e. a 1:1 mass substitution of SCM for \
		clinker per ton of cement blended in-region. Named once so the \
		energy-added branch and the Cement Module Fuel Not Added to Industry 239 \
		Fuel Use This Year diagnostic stay consistent (the diagnostic adds this \
		back; otherwise the lever would push that check negative). Zero at \
		defaults and in BAU.
	|

Cement Spare Capacity Headroom=
	QUANTUM(
	  MAX(0,
	    SUM(Last Year Clinker Production Capacity by Pathway[Cement Production Pathway!]\
		)
	    * SYCCU Start Year Cement Capacity Utilization
	    - Domestic Clinker Demand),
	  10000)
	~	metric tons/Year
	~	DIAGNOSTIC ONLY since the tightness activation (2026-08-28): retired from \
		the decision chain (the absorbable share it fed was replaced by the smooth \
		Cement Replacement Blend Weight), kept because slack over the SYCCU norm \
		remains a useful output. Production the surviving fleet could deliver \
		beyond this year's clinker demand (nameplate at target utilization minus \
		demand, floored at zero): the slack a retiring kiln's output can be \
		absorbed into. Beginning-of-year capacity stocks; demand's only cost \
		responses are Last Year passthrough chains, so no same-year loop back from \
		the overhaul decision. QUANTUM (10 kt/yr bins, 0.014 percent of demand - \
		far below any economics, far above float noise): headroom is a small \
		difference of two
	~	:SUPPLEMENTARY 
	|

Cement Start Year Fleet CO2 Intensity per Ton of Cement= INITIAL(
	ZIDZ(BAU Cement Taxable CO2 after Capture * Kilograms per Metric Ton,
	     ZIDZ(SUM(BAU Clinker Production by Pathway[Cement Production Pathway!]), BCtCR BAU Clinker to Cement Ratio\
		)))
	~	kg CO2/metric ton
	~	Start-year direct CO2 per tonne of cement produced in-region: the baseline \
		a cement carbon-intensity standard is written against (California's SB 596 \
		sets its targets relative to the 2019 intensity of cement used in the \
		state).
	|

Cement Start Year Fleet CO2 Intensity per Ton of Clinker= INITIAL(
	ZIDZ(BAU Cement Taxable CO2 after Capture * Kilograms per Metric Ton, SUM(BAU Clinker Production by Pathway\
		[Cement Production Pathway!])))
	~	kg CO2/metric ton
	~	Start-year direct CO2 per tonne of clinker produced in-region.
	|

Cement Start Year Module Energy Use by Fuel Type[Industrial Fuel]= INITIAL(
	SUM(
	  SYCPbP Start Year Clinker Production by Pathway[Cement Production Pathway!]
	  * CEIbPaF Cement Energy Intensity by Pathway and Fuel[Cement Production Pathway!,Industrial Fuel\
		]
	))
	~	BTU/Year
	~	The cement module's start-year energy by fuel computed from INPUT DATA \
		ONLY (start-year production x intensities; SYCPbP is a production \
		quantity, so no utilization factor applies). Used as the numerator of the \
		carveout shares instead of INITIAL of the running module chain: the shares \
		now sit inside the generic structure's data spine (the Phase 3a residual \
		restructure) and must not create an initialization loop through pathway \
		costs and electricity prices. Differs from the running module's start-year \
		energy only by the sub-0.01 percent gap between start-year demand and \
		SYCPbP.
	|

Cement Taxable CO2 after Capture=
	(Cement Process CO2 Emissions
	 - Cement Process CO2 Captured by Pathway Capture
	 + Cement Kiln Combustion CO2 Emissions
	 - Cement Combustion CO2 Captured by Pathway Capture)
	/ Grams per Metric Ton CO2e
	~	metric tons CO2e
	~	CO2 the cement module's kilns release this year after any pathway capture: \
		process CO2 plus kiln-fuel combustion CO2, both net of the CCS pathway's \
		capture. The base on which the carbon price is actually levied (process \
		CO2 through the industry process-emissions tax, kiln fuel through the fuel \
		carbon tax), used to cap the output-based allocation rebate at what is \
		paid.
	|

Cement Utilization Smoothing Time=
	3
	~	Year
	~	Averaging window for the utilization that kiln investment decisions assume \
		(both the overhaul continuation cost and the greenfield levelized cost \
		spread their capital over this utilization). Three years: long enough that \
		a one- or two-year demand dip does not scrap and rebuild capacity (the \
		2026-09-25 review found a 3-year -20% shock churned
	|

CES Costs for ACP Compliance[RPS]=
	MAX(0,
	    Elec Portfolio Percentage to Seek This Year[RPS]-SUM(Expected Achievable Annual RPS by Optimization Pass\
		[last pass!])
	  )
	
	* Optimized RPS Credit Price
	* Total Electricity Demand Including TnD Losses ~~|
CES Costs for ACP Compliance[CES]=
	MAX(0,
	    Elec Portfolio Percentage to Seek This Year[CES]-SUM(Expected Achievable Annual CES by Optimization Pass\
		[last pass!])
	  )
	
	* Optimized CES Credit Price
	* Total Electricity Demand Including TnD Losses
	~	$
	~		|

CES Costs for Incremental Capacity Additions=
	SUM(CES Costs for Market Based CES Mechanism[Clean Elec Requirement!])
	~	$
	~		|

CES Costs for Incremental Capacity Additions after Financial Lifetime= DELAY FIXED (
	CES Costs for Incremental Capacity Additions, RPfFESCC Repayment Period for Financed Electricity Sector Capital Costs\
		, 0)
	~	$
	~		|

CES Costs for Market Based CES Mechanism[RPS]=
	(SUM(New Capacity Added or Retrofitted due to Elec Portfolio Stds excluding Hybrid Power Plants\
		[Electricity Source!,RPS
	]
	    *Marginal Capacity Factor by Hour[Electricity Source!,Electricity Timeslice!,Hour\
		!,RPS]
	    *SHELF Days per Electricity Timeslice[Electricity Timeslice!]
	    *Optimized RPS Credit Price
	     )* One Hour
	  )
	
	
	+(SUM(New Capacity Added due to Elec Portfolio Stds for Hybrid Power Plants[Electricity Source\
		!,RPS]
	    *Marginal Capacity Factor by Hour[Electricity Source!,Electricity Timeslice!,Hour\
		!,RPS]
	    *SHELF Days per Electricity Timeslice[Electricity Timeslice!]
	    *Optimized RPS Credit Price
	    *(1-Share of Annual Expected Output that Can be Shifted[Electricity Source!])
	     )* One Hour
	
	  +SUM(New Capacity Added due to Elec Portfolio Stds for Hybrid Power Plants[Electricity Source\
		!,RPS]
	    *Expected Capacity Factors by Hour for Plants Built This Year[Electricity Source\
		!,Electricity Timeslice!,Hour!]
	    *SHELF Days per Electricity Timeslice[Electricity Timeslice!]
	    *Optimized RPS Credit Price
	    *Share of Annual Expected Output that Can be Shifted[Electricity Source!]
	     )* One Hour
	  ) ~~|
CES Costs for Market Based CES Mechanism[CES]=
	(SUM(New Capacity Added or Retrofitted due to Elec Portfolio Stds excluding Hybrid Power Plants\
		[Electricity Source!,CES
	]
	    *Marginal Capacity Factor by Hour[Electricity Source!,Electricity Timeslice!,Hour\
		!,CES]
	    *SHELF Days per Electricity Timeslice[Electricity Timeslice!]
	    *Summed CES and RPS Credit Price
	     )* One Hour
	  )
	
	
	+(SUM(New Capacity Added due to Elec Portfolio Stds for Hybrid Power Plants[Electricity Source\
		!,CES]
	    *Marginal Capacity Factor by Hour[Electricity Source!,Electricity Timeslice!,Hour\
		!,CES]
	    *SHELF Days per Electricity Timeslice[Electricity Timeslice!]
	    *Summed CES and RPS Credit Price
	    *(1-Share of Annual Expected Output that Can be Shifted[Electricity Source!])
	     )* One Hour
	
	  +SUM(New Capacity Added due to Elec Portfolio Stds for Hybrid Power Plants[Electricity Source\
		!,CES]
	    *Expected Capacity Factors by Hour for Plants Built This Year[Electricity Source\
		!,Electricity Timeslice!,Hour!]
	    *SHELF Days per Electricity Timeslice[Electricity Timeslice!]
	    *Summed CES and RPS Credit Price
	    *Share of Annual Expected Output that Can be Shifted[Electricity Source!]
	     )* One Hour
	  )
	~	$
	~		|

CES Credit Price by Optimization Pass[pass1]=
	Initial Guess at CES Price ~~|
CES Credit Price by Optimization Pass[current pass] :EXCEPT:  [last pass]=
	MIN(IF THEN ELSE(
	  CES Credit Price by Optimization Pass[preceeding pass] = 0,
	
	  0,
	
	  MAX(
	    0,
	
	    IF THEN ELSE(
	      Expected Achievable Annual CES by Optimization Pass[preceeding pass]
	      < Elec Portfolio Percentage to Seek This Year[CES],
	  
	      CES Credit Price by Optimization Pass[preceeding pass]
	      + CES Optimization Step Size[current pass],
	
	      CES Credit Price by Optimization Pass[preceeding pass]
	      - CES Optimization Step Size[current pass]
	    )
	  )
	), Elec Portfolio Alternative Compliance Payment) ~~|
CES Credit Price by Optimization Pass[last pass]=
	MIN(IF THEN ELSE(
	  CES Credit Price by Optimization Pass[penultimate pass] = 0,
	
	  0,
	
	  MAX(
	    0,
	    
	    IF THEN ELSE(
	      Final CES Pass Gate = 1,
	      Final CES Pass Value,
	      IF THEN ELSE(
	        Expected Achievable Annual CES by Optimization Pass[penultimate pass]
	        < Elec Portfolio Percentage to Seek This Year[CES],
	  
	        CES Credit Price by Optimization Pass[penultimate pass]
	        + CES Optimization Step Size[last pass],
	
	        CES Credit Price by Optimization Pass[penultimate pass]
	        - CES Optimization Step Size[last pass]
	      )
	    )
	  )
	), Elec Portfolio Alternative Compliance Payment)
	~	$/(MW*hr)
	~		|

CES Optimization Step Size[pass1]=
	ABS(CES Credit Price by Optimization Pass[pass1])
	/ 2 ~~|
CES Optimization Step Size[current pass]=
	IF THEN ELSE(
	  CES Steps in Same Direction[preceeding pass]
	  > Steps Allowed in Same Direction Without Increasing Step Size,
	
	  CES Optimization Step Size[preceeding pass] * 1.5,
	
	  IF THEN ELSE(
	    CES Steps in Same Direction[preceeding pass]
	    > Steps Allowed in Same Direction With Declining Step Size,
	
	    CES Optimization Step Size[preceeding pass],
	
	    CES Optimization Step Size[preceeding pass]
	    / 2
	  )
	)
	~	$/(MW*hour)
	~		|

CES Revenue at Expected Capacity Factor by Optimization Pass[Hybrid Electricity Source\
		,Optimization Pass]=
	CES Credit Price by Optimization Pass[Optimization Pass]
	* Marginal Capacity Factor x Hours per Year for Hybrid Power Plants[Hybrid Electricity Source\
		,CES]
	* Elec Portfolio Qualifying Sources[Hybrid Electricity Source,CES]
	~	$/MW
	~		|

CES Revenue at Marginal Capacity Factor by Optimization Pass[Optimization Pass, Electricity Source\
		]=
	CES Credit Price by Optimization Pass[Optimization Pass]
	* Marginal Capacity Factor x Hours per Year[Electricity Source,CES]
	* Elec Portfolio Qualifying Sources[Electricity Source,CES]
	~	$/MW
	~		|

CES Steps in Negative Direction[pass1]=
	0 ~~|
CES Steps in Negative Direction[current pass]=
	IF THEN ELSE(
	  Expected Achievable Annual CES by Optimization Pass[preceeding pass]
	  > Elec Portfolio Percentage to Seek This Year[CES],
	
	  CES Steps in Negative Direction[preceeding pass] + 1,
	
	  0
	)
	~	Dmnl
	~		|

CES Steps in Positive Direction[pass1]=
	0 ~~|
CES Steps in Positive Direction[current pass]=
	IF THEN ELSE(
	  Expected Achievable Annual CES by Optimization Pass[preceeding pass]
	  < Elec Portfolio Percentage to Seek This Year[CES],
	
	  CES Steps in Positive Direction[preceeding pass] + 1,
	
	  0
	)
	~	Dmnl
	~		|

CES Steps in Same Direction[Optimization Pass]=
	MAX(
	  CES Steps in Positive Direction[Optimization Pass],
	  CES Steps in Negative Direction[Optimization Pass]
	)
	~	Dmnl
	~		|

CF Scaling Revenue per Unit Capacity for Cost Driven Retirement[Electricity Source]=
	Last Year Annual Net Energy Market Revenue per MW by Plant Type[Electricity Source]
	+ Last Year Revenue from CES Credits per Unit Electricity Output by Plant Type[Electricity Source\
		]
	+ Variable Subsidies per Unit Capacity[Electricity Source]
	~	$/MW
	~		|

CGSCR Credit Guarantee Subsidy Cost Rate=
	GET DIRECT CONSTANTS(
	   'InputData/indst/CGSCR/CGSCR.csv',
	   ',',
	   'B2'
	)
	~	Dmnl
	~	One-time budgetary cost to the government of guaranteeing one dollar of \
		qualifying industrial equipment loans: the expected default payouts net of \
		fees, per the US Federal Credit Reform Act (FCRA) budget scoring \
		convention.  NOT an annual interest rate - guaranteeing $100M of loans \
		books a cost of $100M times this value, once, at origination.  The \
		fair-value convention (market price of the same risk) is often several \
		times larger; the budget basis is used deliberately because Government \
		Revenue Accounting allocates actual government cash flow.  Regional \
		adapters should substitute their own credit-program expected-loss rate.  \
		See the About tab of the source workbook in InputData/indst/CGSCR.
	|

