	~	$
	~		|

Change in Government Transfer Payments Included in Nonenergy Industry Revenue by ISIC Code\
		[ISIC Code] :EXCEPT:  [Industry Category ISIC Code],[ISIC 64T66]=
	0 ~~|
Change in Government Transfer Payments Included in Nonenergy Industry Revenue by ISIC Code\
		[Industry Category ISIC Code] :EXCEPT:  [ISIC 27]=
	Industry Sector Change in Government Subsidy Payments by ISIC Code[Industry Category ISIC Code\
		]
	+ Change in Carbon Tax Rebates to Nonenergy Industries[Industry Category] ~~|
Change in Government Transfer Payments Included in Nonenergy Industry Revenue by ISIC Code\
		[ISIC 27 {Manufacture of Electrical Equipment}]=
	Industry Sector Change in Government Subsidy Payments by ISIC Code[ISIC 27]
	+ Change in Carbon Tax Rebates[appliances and electrical equipment 27]
	+ Change in Subsidies Paid for Grid Battery Production Retained by Manufacturers ~~|
Change in Government Transfer Payments Included in Nonenergy Industry Revenue by ISIC Code\
		[ISIC 64T66 {Financial and insurance activities}]=
	Industry Sector Change in Government Subsidy Payments by ISIC Code[ISIC 64T66]
	~	$
	~	Government transfer payments that sit inside the nonenergy industry \
		revenue variables: industry sector subsidies (production, performance, \
		process emissions incentives, and CCS subsidies on each industry's own \
		row; investment and capital subsidies on ISIC 64T66), carbon tax rebates, \
		and the grid battery production subsidy retained by manufacturers (ISIC \
		27).  A transfer changes who holds cash without any production behind it, \
		and the real spending a transfer enables (equipment purchases, OM, measure \
		installations) already enters the IO demand vector through the capital and \
		OM recipient-ISIC terms, so this amount is subtracted from industry \
		revenue at the IO handoff (Industry Contribution to Change in Global \
		Output by ISIC Code before Passthrough Costs).  Entity cash flow \
		accounting and the accounting equality checks continue to use \
		subsidy-inclusive revenue.  This mirrors the fuel-side convention, where \
		the IO chain uses Change in Fuel Industry Revenue by Fuel while only a \
		supplementary check uses the Including Subsidies and Rebates variant.
	|

