Duration Multiplier for Subsidies for Carbon Sequestration[Electricity Source]=
	ZIDZ(
	  (1/(1-ACTR Average Corporate Tax Rate))
	  *Capital Recovery Factor for Power Plants[Electricity Source],
	  Capital Recovery Factor for Subisides per Unit Electricity Output[Electricity Source\
		]
	)
	~	Dmnl
	~		|

Duration Multiplier for Subsidies per Unit Electricity Output[Electricity Source]=
	ZIDZ(
	  (1/(1-ACTR Average Corporate Tax Rate))
	  *Capital Recovery Factor for Power Plants[Electricity Source],
	  Capital Recovery Factor for Subsidies per Unit Electricity Output[Electricity Source\
		]
	)
	~	Dmnl
	~		|

DUVT Deep Unviability Threshold=
	GET DIRECT CONSTANTS(
	   'InputData/elec/DUVT/DUVT.csv',
	   ',',
	   'B2'
	)
	~	Dmnl
	~		|

Economic Benefit from Avoided Climate Damages=
	- Change in Total CO2e Emissions
	* SCoC Social Cost of Carbon
	~	$
	~		|

Economic Capacity Retirements by Optimization Pass[Electricity Source,Optimization Pass\
		]=
	IF THEN ELSE(
	  Desired Cost Driven Capacity Retirements[Electricity Source,Optimization Pass] > 0
	  :AND: Max Capacity that Can be Retired Economically by Plant Type[Electricity Source\
		]
	        < 2 * MPPC Minimum Power Plant Capacity[Electricity Source],
	  Max Capacity that Can be Retired Economically by Plant Type[Electricity Source],
	  QUANTUM(
	    MIN(IF THEN ELSE(Average Three Year Prior Net Loss per Unit Output[Electricity Source\
		,Optimization Pass] > 0, 1, 0)
	        * Pending Cost Driven Retirements[Electricity Source]
	        + Desired Cost Driven Capacity Retirements[Electricity Source,Optimization Pass\
		],
	      Max Capacity that Can be Retired Economically by Plant Type[Electricity Source\
		]),
	    MPPC Minimum Power Plant Capacity[Electricity Source]))
	~	MW
	~	If the same power plant is scheduled to retire and is also uneconomic to \
		operate, that plant retires only once, not twice.  Therefore, we must use \
		a MAX() function here instead of adding the capacity that meets each \
		retirement condition.
	|

Economic Capacity Retirements Excluding Capacity Payment[Electricity Source]=
	IF THEN ELSE(
	  Desired Cost Driven Capacity Retirements Excluding Capacity Payment[Electricity Source\
		] > 0
	  :AND: Max Capacity that Can be Retired Economically by Plant Type[Electricity Source\
		]
	        < 2 * MPPC Minimum Power Plant Capacity[Electricity Source],
	  Max Capacity that Can be Retired Economically by Plant Type[Electricity Source],
	  QUANTUM(
	    MIN(IF THEN ELSE(Average Three Year Prior Net Loss per Unit Output Excluding Capacity Payment\
		[Electricity Source] > 0, 1, 0)
	        * Pending Cost Driven Retirements Excluding Capacity Payment[Electricity Source\
		]
	        + Desired Cost Driven Capacity Retirements Excluding Capacity Payment[Electricity Source\
		],
	      Max Capacity that Can be Retired Economically by Plant Type[Electricity Source\
		]),
	    MPPC Minimum Power Plant Capacity[Electricity Source]))
	~	MW
	~		|

Economically Driven Fraction of CO2 Captured by Industry Category[Industry Category,Emissions Type\
		]=
	Start Year BAU Fraction of CCS Potential Achieved by Industry Sector[Industry Category\
		,Emissions Type]
	+ Cumulative CCS Retrofit Capacity Built During Model Run[Industry Category,Emissions Type\
		]
	~	Dmnl
	~		|

Effective Industrial Equipment Shareweights[Industry Category,Industrial Fuel,Industrial Process\
		] :EXCEPT: [cement and other nonmetallic minerals 239,Industrial Fuel,industrial process end use\
		]=
	MAX(
	  IES Industrial Equipment Shareweights[Industry Category,Industrial Fuel,Industrial Process\
		],
	  MSfFIEF Minimum Shareweight for Feasible Industrial Equipment Fuels
	  * FIEF Feasible Industrial Equipment Fuels[Industrial Fuel,Industrial Process]
	) ~~|
Effective Industrial Equipment Shareweights[cement and other nonmetallic minerals 239\
		,Industrial Fuel,industrial process end use]=
	MAX(
	  IES Industrial Equipment Shareweights[cement and other nonmetallic minerals 239,Industrial Fuel\
		,industrial process end use],
	  MSfFIEF Minimum Shareweight for Feasible Industrial Equipment Fuels
	  * FIEF Feasible Industrial Equipment Fuels[Industrial Fuel,industrial process end use\
		]
	)
	* ZIDZ(
	    1 - Cement Carveout Share of Energy by Fuel[Industrial Fuel],
	    Cement Residual Share of Process Energy[industrial process end use]
	  )
	~	Dmnl
	~	Calibrated shareweights (IES) floored at a small epsilon for technically \
		feasible fuel and process pairs. The calibration writes a zero shareweight \
		wherever a fuel has zero observed share, which makes those fuels \
		permanently undeployable through the fuel choice - even a policy that made \
		them free could not create adoption, though a mandate could. The floor \
		separates infeasibility from non-observation: fuels the feasibility mask \
		(FIEF) marks as technically capable of serving a process keep at least \
		MSfFIEF (0.001) of shareweight. The cement and other nonmetallic minerals \
		239 process-end-use branch scales the IES weights by (1 - cement carveout \
		share) normalized by the residual's share of each process's energy (Phase \
		3a residual restructure): the IES calibration reproduces the FULL-239 fuel \
		mix, but 239's equipment structure now models only the non-cement residual \
		- at equal costs a logit's shares transform exactly this way, so this is \
		the residual's mix calibration, not a policy. Without it the residual's \
		turnover drifts back toward cement's coal-heavy mix (verified: 239 coal \
		+25 percent by 2050 at defaults). The per-process normalization (dividing \
		by the residual's share of the process's energy) leaves fuel-vs-fuel \
		shares identical while keeping the thermal battery weight - an unscaled \
		thirteenth competitor in the same denominator - at its calibrated relative \
		position (3a adversarial-review finding). The scaling multiplies the whole \
		MAX: under normalization no floor-bound fuel is diluted (fuels with zero \
		observed share have zero cement share, so their scale factor is one over \
		the residual share, at least one); the latent hazard that a fuel with \
		carveout share exactly one would drop out of the eligibility set is \
		documented (only the petcoke/resid row, which the residual should not \
		adopt anyway). With the relative-cost logit at exponent -6 a floored fuel \
		is dormant at BAU prices (share of roughly MSfFIEF) and reaches parity \
		with an incumbent when its cost per unit of energy service falls to about \
		one third of the incumbent's; a policy that makes it nearly free lets it \
		take the market. Setting MSfFIEF to zero reproduces the unfloored model \
		exactly.
	|

Effective Maximum Hourly Electricity Imports[Electricity Timeslice,Hour]=
	MIN(MaMHEIaE Maximum Hourly Imports,
	  Total Electricity Demand by Hour before Net Imports[Electricity Timeslice,Hour])
	~	MW*hr
	~	Hourly imports are capped at the lesser of the exogenous interchange limit \
		and that hour's demand before net imports. Without the demand term, the \
		net-load allocation can assign an hour more imports than it consumes (the \
		exogenous limit is not scaled to small-state demand), clamping residual \
		demand to zero and creating degenerate dispatch hours. The allocation loop \
		redistributes the excess to other hours via Share of Max Hourly Imports \
		Met.
	|

Efficiency Measure Energy Use Multiplier[Industry Category,Industrial Fuel] :EXCEPT: \
		[Industry Category,electricity if]=
	1 - Fraction of Industrial Combustion Fuel Use Avoided by Efficiency Measures[Industry Category\
		] ~~|
Efficiency Measure Energy Use Multiplier[Industry Category,electricity if]=
	1 - Fraction of Industrial Electricity Use Avoided by Efficiency Measures[Industry Category\
		]
	~	Dmnl
	~	Multiplier on industrial equipment energy use from deployed process \
		efficiency measures.  Applies to all processes; fuel savings reduce \
		combustion fuels and electricity savings reduce electricity use.
	|

