	Est Start Year Transportation Sector Fuel Used[Vehicle Type,Cargo Type,Vehicle Technology\
		,Transportation Fuel])
	~	BTU
	~		|

Last Year Vehicles by Vintage[Vehicle Type,Cargo Type,Vehicle Technology,Vehicle Vintage\
		]= INTEG (
	New Vehicles[Vehicle Type,Cargo Type,Vehicle Technology] * This Year Vintage Flag[Vehicle Vintage\
		]
	- Retiring Vehicles[Vehicle Type,Cargo Type,Vehicle Technology]
	  * ZIDZ(Last Year Vehicles by Vintage[Vehicle Type,Cargo Type,Vehicle Technology,Vehicle Vintage\
		] * Vehicle Survival Rate by Vehicle Type Cargo Type Engine and Vintage
	[Cargo Type,Vehicle Type,Vehicle Vintage],
	    SUM(Last Year Vehicles by Vintage[Vehicle Type,Cargo Type,Vehicle Technology,Vehicle Vintage\
		!] * Vehicle Survival Rate by Vehicle Type Cargo Type Engine and Vintage
	[Cargo Type,Vehicle Type,Vehicle Vintage!])),
		SYVbT Start Year Vehicles by Technology[Vehicle Type,Cargo Type,Vehicle Technology]\
		 * SYVSbV Start Year Vehicle Share by Vintage
		[Vehicle Type,Cargo Type,Vehicle Technology,Vehicle Vintage])
	~	vehicles
	~		|

Last Year Vehicles over Fuel Economy by Vintage[Vehicle Type,Cargo Type,Vehicle Technology\
		,Vehicle Vintage]= INTEG (
	New Vehicles over Fuel Economy[Vehicle Type,Cargo Type,Vehicle Technology] * This Year Vintage Flag\
		[Vehicle Vintage]
	- Retiring Vehicles over Fuel Economy[Vehicle Type,Cargo Type,Vehicle Technology]
	  * ZIDZ(Last Year Vehicles over Fuel Economy by Vintage[Vehicle Type,Cargo Type,Vehicle Technology\
		,Vehicle Vintage] * Vehicle Survival Rate by Vehicle Type Cargo Type Engine and Vintage\
		[Cargo Type,Vehicle Type,Vehicle Vintage],
	    SUM(Last Year Vehicles over Fuel Economy by Vintage[Vehicle Type,Cargo Type,Vehicle Technology\
		,Vehicle Vintage!] * Vehicle Survival Rate by Vehicle Type Cargo Type Engine and Vintage\
		[Cargo Type,Vehicle Type,Vehicle Vintage!])),
		IF THEN ELSE(NVFEbV New Vehicle Fuel Economy by Vintage[Vehicle Type,Cargo Type,Vehicle Technology\
		,Vehicle Vintage] > 0, (SYVbT Start Year Vehicles by Technology[Vehicle Type,Cargo Type\
		,Vehicle Technology] * SYVSbV Start Year Vehicle Share by Vintage[Vehicle Type,Cargo Type\
		,Vehicle Technology,Vehicle Vintage]) / (NVFEbV New Vehicle Fuel Economy by Vintage\
		[Vehicle Type,Cargo Type,Vehicle Technology,Vehicle Vintage]), 0))
	~	vehicles/(miles/BTU)
	~		|

Last Year Weighted Average Annual Capital Cost per Unit Capacity[Electricity Source]=
	DELAY FIXED(Weighted Average Annual Capital Costs per Unit Capacity[Electricity Source\
		],
	  1,
	  0
	)
	~	$/MW
	~		|

Last Year Weighted Average Annual Fixed OM Costs per Unit Capacity[Electricity Source\
		]=
	DELAY FIXED(Weighted Average Annual Fixed OM Cost per Unit Capacity[Electricity Source\
		],
	  1,
	  0
	)
	~	$/MW
	~		|

Last Year Weighted Average Expected Capacity Factors by Hour Without Hypothetical Adjustments\
		[Electricity Source,Electricity Timeslice,Hour]= DELAY FIXED (
	  Weighted Average Expected Capacity Factors by Hour Without Hypothetical Adjustments\
		[Electricity Source,Electricity Timeslice,Hour],
	  1,
	  Start Year Seasonal Expected Hourly Electricity Capacity Factors after RAF[Electricity Source\
		,Electricity Timeslice,Hour])
	~	Dmnl
	~		|

LCCT Low Carbon Cement Credit Threshold as Share of Fleet CO2 Intensity=
	GET DIRECT CONSTANTS(
	   'InputData/indst/_cement/LCCT/LCCT.csv',
	   ',',
	   'B2'
	)
	~	Dmnl
	~	Cement earns the production tax credit when its direct CO2 per tonne of \
		cement is at or below this share of last year's fleet average. US default \
		0.6: 40 percent below the industry benchmark, the eligibility rule of New \
		Mexico's 2026 Industrial Carbon Reduction Act proposal. With the US inputs \
		the CCS and alternative-chemistry pathways qualify; the electric kiln, \
		which keeps the process CO2, sits at about two thirds of the fleet average \
		and does not. See the About tab of the workbook in its folder.
	|

LCFS Credit Price=
	MIN(
	  LCPC LCFS Credit Price Cap,
	
	  ZIDZ(
	    Incremental Costs from Substitute Fuel Production due to LCFS,
	    LCFS Credits Generated
	  )
	)
	~	$/LCFS credit
	~	Although some substitute fuels have no incremental cost to produce, they \
		still create LCFS credits and thus reduce the market price for LCFS \
		credits, so we divide total costs by total credits, rather than \
		subscripting by fuel type.
	~	:SUPPLEMENTARY 
	|

LCFS Credits Generated=
	SUM(Reduction in CO2 Emissions due to LCFS[Vehicle Type!,Cargo Type!,Vehicle Technology\
		!,Transportation Fuel!])
	/ GCApLC Grams CO2e Avoided per LCFS Credit
	~	LCFS credits
	~		|

LCPC LCFS Credit Price Cap=
	GET DIRECT CONSTANTS(
	   'InputData/trans/LCPC/LCPC.csv',
	   ',',
	   'B2*'
	)
	~	$/LCFS credit
	~		|

LCPT Low Carbon Procurement Threshold as Share of Fleet CO2 Intensity=
	GET DIRECT CONSTANTS(
	   'InputData/indst/_cement/LCPT/LCPT.csv',
	   ',',
	   'B2'
	)
	~	Dmnl
	~	Cement counts as low-carbon for the procurement lever when its direct CO2 \
		per tonne of cement is at or below this share of last year's fleet \
		average. US default 0.817: the GSA 2023 low-embodied-carbon limit for the \
		cleanest 20 percent of cement (751 kg CO2e per tonne) over the \
		industry-average portland cement declaration such limits are set against \
		(919 kg per tonne, PCA), a relative rule that carries over to the model's \
		direct-CO2 basis. A near-zero advance market commitment (First Movers \
		Coalition, 184 kg per tonne) is about 0.2. See the About tab of the \
		workbook in its folder.
	|

LDVs Feebate Rate=
	0
	~	Dmnl [0,1]
	~	The feebate is set as a fraction of the global best practice feebate rate.  The \
		global best practice feebate rate is $2000 per (hundredth gal/mile), which \
		equates to a $2000 fee on an LDV that gets 20 mpg if the pivot point is 25 \
		mpg.  (To see this: 20 mpg is 0.04 gpm.  25 mpg is 0.05 gpm.  The \
		difference is -.01 gpm.  So in order to levy a $2000 fee on the 20 mpg car \
		when the pivot point is 25 mpg, we need a rate of $2000/.01 gpm.)  The \
		feebate rate is designated in gpm rather than mpg because this provides \
		the same monetary incentive to save a gallon of fuel for all LDVs, despite \
		their different efficiencies.  That is, given a fixed number of miles \
		driven, reducing gpm by 0.01 will save the same quantity of fuel for any \
		car.  Given a fixed number of miles driven, increasing mpg by 0.01 will \
		save different amounts of fuel in different cars, depending on their \
		starting efficiencies.
		
		We're assuming the pivot point is adjusted to always maintain revenue \
		neutrality (no net cost to government or consumers).  This policy lever \
		only applies to passenger LDVs due to a lack of data on feebate effects on \
		other vehicle types.  The policy does not increase the efficiency of \
		battery electric LDVs, as it is assumed that they are already sufficiently \
		fuel-efficient to receive any potential benefits of the feebate, so the \
		feebate would not incentivize further fuel efficiency improvements in this \
		type of LDV.
	|

LDVs Feebate Rate This Year=
	LDVs Feebate Rate
	* Selected Policy Implementation Schedule[trans LDVs feebate X]
	~	Dmnl
	~		|

Legally Required Elec Portfolio Percentage[Clean Elec Requirement]=
	SUM(
	  Weighted Average Elec Portfolio Percentage by Year[Future Year!,Clean Elec Requirement\
		]
	  * This Year Flag[Future Year!]
	)
	~	Dmnl
	~		|

Legally Required Electricity Dispatch to Satisfy Elec Portfolio Stds[Clean Elec Requirement\
		]=
	SUM(
	  Electricity Dispatch Required to Satisfy Elec Portfolio Stds[Electricity Timeslice\
		!,Hour!,Clean Elec Requirement]
	  * SHELF Days per Electricity Timeslice[Electricity Timeslice!]
	)
	~	MW*hour
	~		~	:SUPPLEMENTARY 
	|

Levelized BAU Subsidy per Unit Grid Battery Capacity Deployed=
	(BAU Subsidy per Unit Grid Battery Capacity Deployed + BAU Subsidy for Grid Battery Production Passed Through to Consumers\
		)
	/ RPfFESCC Repayment Period for Financed Electricity Sector Capital Costs
	*ESTRTE Grid Battery Round Trip Efficiency
	*One Year
	~	$/(MW*hr)
	~		|

Levelized Subsidy per Unit Grid Battery Capacity Deployed=
	(Subsidy per Unit Grid Battery Capacity Deployed + Subsidy for Grid Battery Production Passed Through to Consumers\
		)
	/ RPfFESCC Repayment Period for Financed Electricity Sector Capital Costs
	*ESTRTE Grid Battery Round Trip Efficiency
	*One Year
	~	$/(MW*hr)
	~		|

LFHVM Load Factor Hourly Variance Multiplier[Electricity Timeslice,Sectors]=
	GET DIRECT CONSTANTS(
	   'InputData/elec/LFHVM/LFHVM.csv',
	   ',',
	   'B2'
	)
	~	Dmnl
	~		|

Loading Multiplier from Absorbed Travel[Vehicle Type,Cargo Type]=
	IF THEN ELSE(
	  VAUL Vehicle Absorbed Use as Loading[Vehicle Type,Cargo Type] > 0,
	  Use per Vehicle Multiplier at BAU Fleet Mix[Vehicle Type,Cargo Type],
	  1
	)
	~	Dmnl
	~		|

Low Carbon Cement Credit Duration=
	10
	~	years [1,20,1]
	~	Years a new kiln earns the low-carbon cement production tax credit after \
		it is built (US production credits typically run 10 years, 45Q 12). Policy \
		design setting entered in Vensim.
	|

Low Carbon Cement Credit per Ton of Clinker[Cement Production Pathway]=
	ZIDZ(Low Carbon Cement Production Tax Credit This Year, Clinker to Cement Ratio)
	* Cement Pathway Qualifies for Production Tax Credit[Cement Production Pathway]
	~	$/metric ton
	~	Credit per tonne of clinker on each qualifying pathway: the credit per \
		tonne of cement times the cement made from a tonne of clinker.
	|

Low Carbon Cement Premium per Ton of Clinker=
	MAX(0,
	  ZIDZ(SUM(Cement Pathway Qualifies as Low Carbon[Cement Production Pathway!] * Clinker Production by Pathway\
		[Cement Production Pathway!]
	           * Cement Pathway Levelized Cost per Ton of Clinker[Cement Production Pathway\
		!]),
	       Low Carbon Clinker Production)
	  - Cement Pathway Levelized Cost per Ton of Clinker[dry kiln with precalciner])
	~	$/metric ton
	~	Cost premium of low-carbon clinker over conventional clinker this year: \
		the production-weighted levelized cost of the qualifying pathways less the \
		precalciner kiln's, floored at zero.
	|

Low Carbon Cement Procurement Premium Paid=
	Low Carbon Cement Premium per Ton of Clinker
	* MIN(Low Carbon Procurement Clinker Volume, Low Carbon Clinker Production)
	~	$
	~	Premium committed buyers pay this year for the low-carbon cement they \
		procure (on the volume actually available).
	|

Low Carbon Cement Procurement Premium Paid by Government=
	Low Carbon Cement Procurement Premium Paid * Share of Low Carbon Cement Premium Paid by Government
	~	$
	~	Government share of the procurement premium, paid to industry 239 through \
		the clean materials support cash flow.
	|

Low Carbon Cement Production Tax Credit=
	0
	~	output currency unit/metric ton [0,100,1]
	~	Policy lever (low-carbon cement production tax credit): a payment per \
		tonne of cement produced on pathways whose direct CO2 per tonne is at or \
		below the LCCT share of last year's fleet average, paid for the credit \
		duration after a kiln is built, at full implementation. The template is a \
		per-tonne production credit with an intensity threshold (New Mexico's 2026 \
		proposal paid 85 dollars per tonne of CO2 avoided to plants 40 percent \
		below the industry benchmark; federal 45V/45Z credits use intensity \
		tiers). Enters the new-kiln choice as a reduction of the qualifying \
		pathways' operating cost. Rides the indst cement production credit \
		implementation schedule.
	|

Low Carbon Cement Production Tax Credit in Dollars=
	Low Carbon Cement Production Tax Credit
	* OCCF Dollars per Small Output Currency Unit
	~	$/metric ton
	~	Low Carbon Cement Production Tax Credit converted to the model dollar year.
	|

Low Carbon Cement Production Tax Credit This Year=
	Low Carbon Cement Production Tax Credit in Dollars
	* Selected Policy Implementation Schedule[indst cement production credit X]
	~	$/metric ton
	~	Low Carbon Cement Production Tax Credit as implemented this year, on the \
		indst cement production credit implementation schedule.
	|

Low Carbon Clinker Production=
	SUM(Cement Pathway Qualifies as Low Carbon[Cement Production Pathway!] * Clinker Production by Pathway\
		[Cement Production Pathway!])
	~	metric tons/Year
	~	Clinker produced this year on pathways meeting the procurement threshold.
	|

Low Carbon Hydrogen Demand=
	SUM(Low Carbon Hydrogen Demand by Sector[Sectors!])
	~	BTU
	~		|

Low Carbon Hydrogen Demand by Sector[Sectors] :EXCEPT:    [industry sector],[geoengineering sector\
		],[district heat and hydrogen sector]=
	Hydrogen Demand by Sector[Sectors]
	* SoHDTiGaLCbS Share of Hydrogen Demand That is Low Carbon by Sector[Sectors] ~~|
Low Carbon Hydrogen Demand by Sector[industry sector]=
	SUM(Industrial Fuel Use[low carbon hydrogen if,Industry Category!]) ~~|
Low Carbon Hydrogen Demand by Sector[geoengineering sector]=
	Geoengineering Sector Energy Use[low carbon hydrogen if] ~~|
Low Carbon Hydrogen Demand by Sector[district heat and hydrogen sector]=
	Fuel Used to Generate Heat[low carbon hydrogen if]
	~	BTU
	~		|

Low Carbon Hydrogen Production by Pathway[Hydrogen Production Pathway]=
	ZIDZ(
	  New Low Carbon Hydrogen Production Capacity This Year[Hydrogen Production Pathway]
	  + Surviving Capacity Used for Low Carbon Hydrogen Production[Hydrogen Production Pathway\
		],
	
	  SUM(
	    New Low Carbon Hydrogen Production Capacity This Year[Hydrogen Production Pathway\
		!]
	    + Surviving Capacity Used for Low Carbon Hydrogen Production[Hydrogen Production Pathway\
		!]
	  )
	)
	~	Dmnl
	~		|

Low Carbon Hydrogen Production Shares with Fallback[Hydrogen Production Pathway]=
	IF THEN ELSE(
	  SUM(Low Carbon Hydrogen Production by Pathway[Hydrogen Production Pathway!]) = 0,
	
	  IF THEN ELSE(
	    SUM(New Low Carbon Hydrogen Production Capacity Shares This Year[Hydrogen Production Pathway\
		!]) = 0,
	
	    Start Year Low Carbon Hydrogen Production Shares by Pathway[Hydrogen Production Pathway\
		],
	
	    New Low Carbon Hydrogen Production Capacity Shares This Year[Hydrogen Production Pathway\
		]
	  ),
	
	  ZIDZ( 
	    Low Carbon Hydrogen Production by Pathway[Hydrogen Production Pathway],
	    SUM(Low Carbon Hydrogen Production by Pathway[Hydrogen Production Pathway!])
	  )
	)
	~	Dmnl
	~		|

Low Carbon Pathway Allocation of Required New Clinker Capacity[Cement Production Pathway\
		]=
	ZIDZ(Cement Pathway Qualifies as Low Carbon[Cement Production Pathway] * (MAX(Cement Pathway Levelized Cost per Ton of Clinker\
		[Cement Production Pathway], 0.01) / MAX(Cheapest Low Carbon Pathway Levelized Cost\
		, 0.01)) ^ IELC Industrial Equipment Logit Coefficient,
	     SUM(Cement Pathway Qualifies as Low Carbon[Cement Production Pathway!] * (MAX(Cement Pathway Levelized Cost per Ton of Clinker\
		[Cement Production Pathway!], 0.01) / MAX(Cheapest Low Carbon Pathway Levelized Cost\
		, 0.01)) ^ IELC Industrial Equipment Logit Coefficient))
	~	Dmnl
	~	How the new capacity that procurement or the intensity standard requires \
		is split among the low-carbon pathways: the same relative-cost logit as \
		the ordinary kiln choice (IELC), over the low-carbon pathways only and \
		without the commercial-readiness shareweights, since a requirement opens \
		the market to pathways not yet chosen on their own.
	|

Low Carbon Procurement Clinker Volume=
	Fraction of Cement Demand Purchased under Low Carbon Procurement This Year
	* (1 - Share of Procured Cement Supplied as Blended Cement)
	* Total Cement Demand * Clinker to Cement Ratio
	~	metric tons/Year
	~	Clinker in the procured cement that must come from low-carbon kilns this \
		year: the committed share of demand not supplied as blended cement.
	|

Low Carbon Procurement Required Share of New Clinker Capacity=
	ZIDZ(MAX(0, Low Carbon Procurement Clinker Volume - Last Year Low Carbon Clinker Production\
		),
	     Last Year Total New Clinker Production Capacity)
	~	Dmnl
	~	Share of new clinker capacity the procurement lever needs as low-carbon \
		kilns: the committed volume not met by blended cement or by last year's \
		low-carbon production, over last year's total new capacity.
	|

