New Process Efficiency Measure Capital Expenditures[Industry Category]=
	SUM(
	  New Process Efficiency Measure Deployment This Year[Industry Category,Process Efficiency Measure\
		!,Measure Cost Level!]
	  * MCLS Measure Cost Level Share[Measure Cost Level!]
	  * (PEMFSP Process Efficiency Measure Fuel Savings Potential[Industry Category,Process Efficiency Measure\
		!]
	     * Industrial Combustion Fuel Base for Measures[Industry Category]
	     + PEMESP Process Efficiency Measure Elec Savings Potential[Industry Category,Process Efficiency Measure\
		!]
	     * Industrial Electricity Base for Measures[Industry Category])
	  * PEMCC Process Efficiency Measure Capital Cost per Unit Annual Energy Saved[Industry Category\
		,Process Efficiency Measure!]
	)
	~	$
	~	Capital cost of process efficiency measure savings capacity newly deployed \
		this year, priced against the current-year energy base at unadjusted input \
		costs.
	|

New Process Efficiency Measure Deployment This Year[Industry Category,Process Efficiency Measure\
		,Measure Cost Level]=
	IF THEN ELSE(
	  PEMFSP Process Efficiency Measure Fuel Savings Potential[Industry Category,Process Efficiency Measure\
		]
	  + PEMESP Process Efficiency Measure Elec Savings Potential[Industry Category,Process Efficiency Measure\
		] <= 0,
	
	  0,
	
	  MIN(
	    MAX(
	      0,
	      MIN(
	        Process Efficiency Measure Potential Remaining After BAU Deployment[Industry Category\
		,Process Efficiency Measure],
	      MAX(
	        Process Efficiency Measure Tranche Economically Deployable[Industry Category\
		,Process Efficiency Measure,Measure Cost Level
	],
	        Min Fraction of Process Efficiency Measure Potential Deployed This Year[Industry Category\
		]
	      ))
	      - MAX(
	          0,
	          Last Year Deployed Fraction of Process Efficiency Measure Potential[Industry Category\
		,Process Efficiency Measure,Measure Cost Level
	]
	          - Process Efficiency Measure Deployment Retired This Year[Industry Category\
		,Process Efficiency Measure,Measure Cost Level]
	        )
	    ),

	    Process Efficiency Measure Deployment Retired This Year[Industry Category,Process Efficiency Measure\
		,Measure Cost Level]
	    + MAX(
	        SoCEMDSiaY Share of Cost Effective Measure Deployment Started in a Year
	        * MAX(
	            0,
	            MIN(
	              Process Efficiency Measure Tranche Economically Deployable[Industry Category\
		,Process Efficiency Measure,Measure Cost Level],
	              Process Efficiency Measure Potential Remaining After BAU Deployment[Industry Category\
		,Process Efficiency Measure]
	            )
	            - Last Year Deployed Fraction of Process Efficiency Measure Potential[Industry Category\
		,Process Efficiency Measure,Measure Cost Level]
	          ),

	        MAX(
	          0,
	          MIN(
	            Min Fraction of Process Efficiency Measure Potential Deployed This Year[\
		Industry Category],
	            Process Efficiency Measure Potential Remaining After BAU Deployment[Industry Category\
		,Process Efficiency Measure]
	          )
	          - Last Year Deployed Fraction of Process Efficiency Measure Potential[Industry Category\
		,Process Efficiency Measure,Measure Cost Level]
	        )
	      )
	  )
	)
	~	Dmnl
	~	Deployment this year, as a fraction of tranche potential.  Retiring \
		capacity is replaced in full (replacing worn-out equipment that is still \
		cost effective is routine maintenance, not a fresh adoption decision, so \
		it is not held back by the annual start-rate).  Genuinely new deployment \
		is rate limited: the economic channel starts SoCEMDSiaY of the remaining \
		gap to its ceiling each year, while a binding standard deploys \
		immediately.  The outer MIN caps the level at whichever target is higher, \
		so a tranche that stops being cost effective decays by retirement instead \
		of being replaced.  Both channels are capped at the potential remaining \
		after BAU-case deployment (no cap in the US, where that share is zero).
	|

