Normalized Change in Amount Spent on Industry Fuels[Industry Category ISIC Code] :EXCEPT:\
		 [ISIC 239]=
	ZIDZ(
	      SUM(Amount Spent on Industry Fuels[Industry Category, Industrial Fuel!])
	      + Amount Spent on Thermal Battery Grid Charging Electricity[Industry Category]\
		,
	      BAU Output by ISIC Code by Year[Industry Category ISIC Code]
	      * (1 + Percent Change in Production due to Policies[Industry Category])
	    )

	    - ZIDZ(
	      SUM(BAU Amount Spent on Industry Fuels[Industry Category, Industrial Fuel!])
	      + BAU Amount Spent on Thermal Battery Grid Charging Electricity[Industry Category\
		],
	      BAU Output by ISIC Code by Year[Industry Category ISIC Code]
	    ) ~~|
Normalized Change in Amount Spent on Industry Fuels[ISIC 239]=
	ZIDZ(
	      SUM(Amount Spent on Industry Fuels[cement and other nonmetallic minerals 239, \
		Industrial Fuel!])
	      + Amount Spent on Thermal Battery Grid Charging Electricity[cement and other nonmetallic minerals 239\
		],
	      BAU Output by ISIC Code by Year[ISIC 239]
	      * (1 + Percent Change in Production due to Policies[cement and other nonmetallic minerals 239\
		])
	      * (1 - Cement Content Reduction in 239 Product Requirements)
	    )

	    - ZIDZ(
	      SUM(BAU Amount Spent on Industry Fuels[cement and other nonmetallic minerals 239\
		, Industrial Fuel!])
	      + BAU Amount Spent on Thermal Battery Grid Charging Electricity[cement and other nonmetallic minerals 239\
		],
	      BAU Output by ISIC Code by Year[ISIC 239]
	    )
	~	Dmnl
	~	Includes thermal battery grid charging purchases on both sides: they are \
		energy purchases affecting industrial unit costs exactly as fuel purchases \
		do, even though thermal battery charging is outside Industrial Fuel Use. \
		The 239 branch scales the policy-case output denominator by the \
		cement-content lever's VALUE cut (1 - lever x CSoIO): the lever's physical \
		cut is deliberately excluded from Percent Change in Production due to \
		Policies (see Cement Content Lever Component of 239 Output Change), so \
		without this factor the numerator's fuel spending falls with cement \
		tonnage while the output denominator does not, and the whole tonnage cut \
		reads as a spurious per-unit fuel-cost saving (adversarial review \
		2026-08-27, finding B4). The remaining decline in fuel cost per dollar of \
		239 output under the lever is the genuine composition effect (cement is \
		energy-intensive relative to its value share).
	|

Normalized Change in Industrial Nonfuel Expenditures[Industry Category ISIC Code]=
	ZIDZ(
	      Change in Miscellaneous Expenditures by Industry[Industry Category]
	      - Change in Industry Sector Carbon Tax Rebates[Industry Category]
	      - Industry Sector Change in Government Subsidy Amount Paid[Industry Category]
	      + Industry Sector Change in New CCS Capital Eqpt Costs That Are Not Financed[Industry Category\
		]
	      + New Measure Capital Expenditures That Are Not Financed[Industry Category]
	      + Change in New Industrial Equipment Capital Costs due to Change in Cost per Unit Output\
		[Industry Category]
	        * (1 - SoICETAF Share of Industry Capital Expenditures That Are Financed[Industry Category\
		]),
	      BAU Output by ISIC Code by Year[Industry Category ISIC Code]
	    )
	~	Dmnl
	~	Change in the industry's nonfuel costs per unit of BAU output, feeding the \
		unit-cost price signal that adjusts product demand and production.  \
		Government transfers that cover industry costs are netted out here \
		(mirroring the CCS carbon tax rebate term): a subsidized equipment \
		purchase or abatement action does not raise the industry's production \
		costs to the extent the government pays, so it should not reduce \
		competitiveness.  Every component of the subsidy aggregate has its gross \
		cost counterpart in this expression or in the fuel-spend term (verified \
		2026-08-04): CCS subsidies vs CCS capital and OM costs, clean heat \
		investment and production subsidies vs gross equipment capital costs \
		(equipment cost here uses the Before Clean Heat Subsidy price), measure \
		capital and performance subsidies vs gross measure capex and OM, and \
		process emissions incentives vs Cost of Process Emissions Policies.  Cash \
		flow and GRA accounting are unaffected; this term changes only the \
		competitiveness signal.
	|

Normalized Standard Deviation of Dispatch Costs Mapped to Hydrogen Production Pathways\
		[electrolysis]=
	SUM(
	  NSDoDC Normalized Standard Deviation of Dispatch Costs[Electricity Source!]
	  * Last Year Electricity Generation by Type[Electricity Source!]
	)
	/
	SUM(Last Year Electricity Generation by Type[Electricity Source!]) ~~|
Normalized Standard Deviation of Dispatch Costs Mapped to Hydrogen Production Pathways\
		[natural gas reforming]=
