Overhauled Preexisting Clinker Capacity Retiring by Pathway[Cement Production Pathway\
		]=
	Preexisting Clinker Capacity Retirements by Pathway[Cement Production Pathway]
	* Overhauled Share of Preexisting Clinker Capacity by Pathway[Cement Production Pathway\
		]
	~	metric tons/Year
	~	Overhauled start-year capacity leaving the fleet this year: start-year \
		retirements taken pro rata to the overhauled share.
	|

Overhauled Share of Preexisting Clinker Capacity by Pathway[Cement Production Pathway\
		]=
	MIN(1, ZIDZ(
	  Last Year Overhauled Preexisting Clinker Capacity by Pathway[Cement Production Pathway\
		],
	  Last Year Preexisting Clinker Production Capacity by Pathway[Cement Production Pathway\
		]))
	~	Dmnl
	~	Share of the surviving start-year fleet on each pathway that has been \
		overhauled since the start year.
	|

PAC Payback Acceptance Curve[Measure Set](
	GET DIRECT LOOKUPS(
	   'InputData/indst/PAC/PAC.csv',
	   ',',
	   '1',
	   'B2'
	))
	~	Dmnl
	~	Share of firms whose investment threshold accepts a given simple payback \
		(x axis in years, dimensionless for the lookup).  Anchored on \
		audited-plant adoption data: Anderson and Newell 2004 and Muthulingam et \
		al. 2013 independently find about half of recommendations adopted at \
		roughly one-year paybacks, with adoption falling steeply toward three \
		years; see PEaWHRP_PaybackAcceptance_Plan.md.  Same construct as the NEMS \
		Industrial Demand Module AcceptFrac payback acceptance curve.  Values \
		beyond both ends of the grid hold the endpoint.
	|

Payment Period for Decommissioning Costs=
	3
	~	Year
	~		|

PCFURfE Percentage Components Fuel Use Reduction for Electricity[Building Component]=
	GET DIRECT CONSTANTS(
	   'InputData/bldgs/PCFURfE/PCFURfE.csv',
	   ',',
	   'B2*'
	)
	~	Dmnl
	~	For building components that can use either electricity or other fuels \
		(such as "heating"), this variable specifies the percentage reduction in \
		fuel use (on a BTU basis) for electric components relative to natural \
		gas-burning components due to the fact that electricity can be converted \
		into work more efficiently than other fuel types.
	|

