Percent Change in Nonenergy Industry Domestic Production due to Change in Passthrough Expenses\
		[Industry Category]=
	-Percent of Purchases of Domestically Produced Goods Shifted to Purchases of Imports\
		[Industry Category]
	
	+ (1 - Percent of Purchases of Domestically Produced Goods Shifted to Purchases of Imports\
		[Industry Category])
	  * Percent Change in Nonenergy Industry Product Demand due to Change in Passthrough Expenses\
		[Industry Category]
	~	Dmnl
	~	First, we substract out the share of domestic production that was shifted \
		to imports.  Then, we assume that reductions in demand affect domestic \
		production and imports equally (meaning each is reduced by the same \
		percentage), so we reduce the remaining share of domestic production by \
		the percentage change in demand.
	|

Percent Change in Nonenergy Industry Product Demand due to Change in Passthrough Expenses\
		[Industry Category]=
	MAX(0.01, 1 + Weighted Average Percent Change in Cost of Goods by Industry Category[\
		Industry Category])
	^ EoDfIP Elasticities of Demand for Industrial Products[Industry Category]
	- 1
	~	Dmnl
	~	Constant-elasticity (power) form rather than the linear approximation \
		gap*elasticity: identical for small cost changes, bounded with diminishing \
		returns for large ones, and the functional form the source estimates (Ho, \
		Morgenstern and Shih 2008 derive the demand elasticities from a CES \
		multisector model).  The MAX guards the base of the power against zero or \
		negative cost ratios.
	|

Percent Change in Nonfuel Production due to Policies[Industry Category] :EXCEPT: [energy industries within industry category\
		],[agriculture and forestry 01T03]=
	Percent Change in Production after Macroeconomic Feedbacks[Industry Category]
	- (Percent Reduction in Nonenergy Nonagriculture Industry Product Demand This Year[Industry Category\
		]
	  * (1 + Percent Change in Production after Macroeconomic Feedbacks[Industry Category\
		])
	  )
	+ Percent Change in Production due to Material Efficiency by Industry Category[Industry Category\
		]
	  * (1 + Percent Change in Production after Macroeconomic Feedbacks[Industry Category\
		]) ~~|
Percent Change in Nonfuel Production due to Policies[energy industries within industry category\
		]=
	0 ~~|
Percent Change in Nonfuel Production due to Policies[agriculture and forestry 01T03]=
	Percent Change in Production after Macroeconomic Feedbacks[agriculture and forestry 01T03\
		]
	+ Percent Change in Agriculture Industry Production due to Nonanimal Product Shifting
	+ Percent Change in Production due to Material Efficiency by Industry Category[agriculture and forestry 01T03\
		]
	  * (1 + Percent Change in Production after Macroeconomic Feedbacks[agriculture and forestry 01T03\
		])
	~	Dmnl
	~	The material efficiency term books the reduction in each supplying \
		industry's production caused by material efficiency adopted in the \
		industries it sells to, using the same form as the product demand \
		reduction term (a percent change applied to post-feedback production).  It \
		is negative for supplying industries.
	|

Percent Change in Production after Macroeconomic Feedbacks[Industry Category]=
	(1 + Percent Change in Nonenergy Industry Domestic Production due to Change in Passthrough Expenses\
		[Industry Category])
	* (1 + Percent Change in Production Due to Macroeconomic Feedbacks[Industry Category\
		])
	
	- 1
	~	Dmnl
	~		|

Percent Change in Production Due to Macroeconomic Feedbacks[Industry Category]=
	IF THEN ELSE(
	  BDMFL Boolean Disable Macroeconomic Feedback Loops > 0,
	
	  0,
	
	  MAX(
	    -1,
	
	    Last Year Percent Change in Contribution to Gross Domestic Product Excluding Most Direct Impacts by ISIC Code\
		[Industry Category ISIC Code]
	  )
	)
	~	Dmnl
	~		|

Percent Change in Production due to Material Efficiency by Industry Category[Industry Category\
		] :EXCEPT: [cement and other nonmetallic minerals 239]=
	ZIDZ(
	  Change in Output due to Material Efficiency by ISIC Code[Industry Category ISIC Code\
		],
	  BAU Output by ISIC Code by Year[Industry Category ISIC Code]
	) ~~|
Percent Change in Production due to Material Efficiency by Industry Category[cement and other nonmetallic minerals 239\
		]=
	ZIDZ(
	  Change in Output due to Material Efficiency by ISIC Code[ISIC 239]
	  - Cement Content Lever Component of 239 Output Change,
	  BAU Output by ISIC Code by Year[ISIC 239]
	)
	~	Dmnl
	~	The percent reduction in a supplying industry's production caused by \
		material efficiency in the industries it sells to (negative for supplying \
		industries).  Folded into Percent Change in Nonfuel Production due to \
		Policies so that Percent Change in Production due to Policies (and its \
		physical consumers: process emissions and non-energy fuel use; energy fuel \
		use follows via output levels and equipment utilization in this model \
		version) reflects material efficiency.  Also used to divide the material \
		efficiency component back OUT of Change in Nonenergy Industry Revenue due \
		to Production Changes, because the economic accounting is booked directly \
		via Change in Output due to Material Efficiency (supplier side) and Direct \
		Production Cost Savings (buyer side).  The 239 branch subtracts the \
		cement-content lever's component (injected into the Material Efficiency \
		Improvement matrix for the DOLLAR booking only): the lever's physical cut \
		is carried at full strength, cement-only, by Total Cement Demand's (1 - \
		lever) factor, so folding the diluted 239-wide percent into the physical \
		chain would double-count cement and wrongly shrink the lime/ceramics \
		residual (2026-08-27).
	|

Percent Change in Production due to Policies[Industry Category] :EXCEPT:         [energy industries within industry category
]=
	Percent Change in Nonfuel Production due to Policies[Industry Category] ~~|
Percent Change in Production due to Policies[energy industries within industry category\
		] :EXCEPT:         [energy pipelines and gas processing 352T353
]=
	Last Year Percent Change in Domestic Fuel Industry Production[energy industries within industry category\
		] ~~|
Percent Change in Production due to Policies[energy pipelines and gas processing 352T353\
		]=
	Last Year Percent Change in Domestic Plus Exported Natural Gas Demand
	~	Dmnl
	~	For the "energy pipelines and gas processing 352T353" industry, we base \
		change in production on the change in demand for natural gas, not the \
		change in production of natural gas, because there are EPS regions (such \
		as U.S. states) which produce little or no gas, but can still have methane \
		leaks from natural gas transmission systems.  Changes in NG demand better \
		reflects changes in leakage from these transmission systems.
	|

Percent Change in Transport Demand due to Macroeconomic Feedbacks by Cargo Type[passenger\
		]=
	0 ~~|
Percent Change in Transport Demand due to Macroeconomic Feedbacks by Cargo Type[freight\
		]=
	Weighted Average Last Year Percent Change in Industry Contribution to Gross Domestic Product Excluding Most Direct Impacts
	~	Dmnl
	~	Research on the relationship between income and VMT have shown negligible \
		impacts, so we set passenger modes to 0 here.
	|

Percent Improvement in Overhauled Kiln Energy Intensity=
	0
	~	Dmnl [0,0.3,0.01]
	~	Policy lever (cement kiln performance standard, companion): the reduction \
		in thermal energy per tonne of clinker that an overhauled start-year kiln \
		achieves relative to the fleet-typical intensity it ran at before, at full \
		implementation. By default an overhaul rebuilds the kiln at its old \
		efficiency; a performance standard that requires modernisation (preheater \
		and precalciner upgrades, grate coolers, process controls) can deliver 10 \
		to 20 percent. Applied to the overhauled share of the start-year fleet \
		through the module's energy-intensity multiplier. Rides the indst cement \
		overhaul efficiency implementation schedule.
	|

Percent Improvement in Overhauled Kiln Energy Intensity This Year=
	Percent Improvement in Overhauled Kiln Energy Intensity
	* Selected Policy Implementation Schedule[indst cement overhaul efficiency X]
	~	Dmnl
	~	The overhauled-kiln energy intensity improvement as implemented this year.
	|

