	  BAU Output by ISIC Code by Year[Nonenergy Industry ISIC Code],
	
	  SUM(BAU Output by ISIC Code by Year[Nonenergy Industry ISIC Code!])
	)
	~	Dmnl
	~		|

Percent of Payroll Tax Allocation by ISIC Code[ISIC Code] :EXCEPT:  [Private Business ISIC Code\
		]=
	0 ~~|
Percent of Payroll Tax Allocation by ISIC Code[Private Business ISIC Code]=
	BAU Employee Compensation by ISIC Code by Year[Private Business ISIC Code]
	/ SUM(BAU Employee Compensation by ISIC Code by Year[Private Business ISIC Code!])
	~	Dmnl
	~		|

Percent of Purchases of Domestically Produced Goods Shifted to Purchases of Imports[Industry Category\
		]=
	MIN(
	
	  1,
	
	  MAX(
	
	    ZIDZ(
	
	      -(1 - Domestic Content Share of Consumption Before Import Substitution by ISIC Code\
		[Industry Category ISIC Code]),
	
	      Domestic Content Share of Consumption Before Import Substitution by ISIC Code[\
		Industry Category ISIC Code]
	
	    ),
	
	    1 - ZIDZ(
	      Domestic Product Attractiveness Ratio due to Passthrough Cost Changes[Industry Category\
		],

	      Domestic Content Share of Consumption Before Import Substitution by ISIC Code[\
		Industry Category ISIC Code]
	      * Domestic Product Attractiveness Ratio due to Passthrough Cost Changes[Industry Category\
		]
	      + 1 - Domestic Content Share of Consumption Before Import Substitution by ISIC Code\
		[Industry Category ISIC Code]
	    )
	  )
	)
	~	Dmnl
	~	CES (Armington) share equation: buyers reallocate between domestic goods \
		and imports as relative costs change, so the fraction of domestic \
		purchases lost depends on how large the import alternative is.  This \
		replaces the earlier direct use of (cost ratio)^elasticity - 1, which is \
		the percent change in the import-to-domestic RATIO, not the share of \
		domestic purchases shifted - using it directly overstated import \
		substitution by roughly the domestic-to-import odds (about 6x for iron and \
		steel at 83 percent domestic content).  The share form asymptotes \
		naturally to the two bounds the MIN() and MAX() clamps enforce (no more \
		than 100 percent of domestic purchases replaced; domestic content cannot \
		exceed 100 percent), so those clamps are retained only as redundant safety \
		bounds.
	|

Percent of Travel Demand Shifted to Other Modes or Eliminated[Cargo Type,Vehicle Type\
		]=
	0,	0,	0,	0,	0,	0;	{passenger}
	0,	0,	0,	0,	0,	0;	{freight}
	{LDVs}	{HDVs}	{airc}	{rail}	{ships}	{mtrbks}
