Preexisting Clinker Capacity Facing Overhaul Decision by Pathway[Cement Production Pathway\
		]=
	MIN(
	  Last Year Preexisting Clinker Production Capacity by Pathway[Cement Production Pathway\
		],
	  CPRS Cement Preexisting Capacity Overhaul Decision Schedule[Cement Production Pathway\
		]
	  + Preexisting Clinker Capacity Reentering Decision Pool by Pathway[Cement Production Pathway\
		]
	  + Below Standard Clinker Capacity Forced to Decide by Pathway[Cement Production Pathway\
		]
	)
	~	metric tons/Year
	~	Preexisting nameplate capacity whose campaign ends this year: the \
		age-based first-decision schedule (fleet age cohorts; kiln-level campaign \
		data does not exist publicly - see CPRS) plus overhauled capacity \
		returning to the pool as a distributed curve (Q4), plus below-standard \
		kilns forced to an early decision by the kiln performance standard lever. \
		Capped by the surviving preexisting stock. Model-run capacity faces its \
		own decisions via Cement Model Run Capacity Facing Overhaul Decision by \
		Pathway.
	|

Preexisting Clinker Capacity Overhauled by Pathway[Cement Production Pathway]=
	Preexisting Clinker Capacity Facing Overhaul Decision by Pathway[Cement Production Pathway\
		]
	* Cement Overhaul Share at Decision[Cement Production Pathway]
	~	metric tons/Year
	~	Capacity that overhauls (kiln shell/drive rebuild) for another campaign. \
		Stays in the preexisting stock and re-enters the decision pool as a \
		distributed curve around the mean campaign length (Q4).
	|

Preexisting Clinker Capacity Reentering Decision Pool by Pathway[Cement Production Pathway\
		]= DELAY3I(
	Preexisting Clinker Capacity Overhauled by Pathway[Cement Production Pathway],
	CPPL Cement Pathway Campaign Length[Cement Production Pathway],
	0)
	~	metric tons/Year
	~	Overhauled preexisting capacity returning to the overhaul-or-replace \
		decision pool, distributed around the mean campaign length CPPL \
		(third-order delay) rather than as a pulse exactly CPPL years later (Q4 \
		decision-curve change, 2026-08-26): different kilns hit their big-capex \
		moments at different ages, so the recurrence is a curve - this \
		approximates an age-hazard process without tracking vintages and keeps \
		decisions flowing through the whole model horizon (the DELAY FIXED pulse \
		left a dead zone after the CPRS schedule ends). Renamed from Cement \
		Capacity Overhauled One Campaign Ago by Pathway. Standalone because delay \
		functions should not be nested inside other functions (KB gotcha).
	|

Preexisting Clinker Capacity Retirements by Pathway[Cement Production Pathway]=
	Preexisting Clinker Capacity Facing Overhaul Decision by Pathway[Cement Production Pathway\
		]
	- Preexisting Clinker Capacity Overhauled by Pathway[Cement Production Pathway]
	+ Clinker Capacity Retired under Replacement Rule by Pathway[Cement Production Pathway\
		]
	~	metric tons/Year
	~	Start-year fleet capacity that retires at its campaign-end decision \
		instead of overhauling: capacity facing a decision less the overhauled \
		part (Dan's 2026-09-29 form; equals facing x (1 - overhaul share)). It \
		leaves production in the decision year and is replaced the same year \
		through the new-build pathway logit. The counterpart for capacity built \
		during the model run is Model Run Clinker Capacity Retirements by Pathway. \
		The capacity replacement lever adds its forced retirements to this flow.
	|

Preexisting Clinker Production Capacity by Pathway[Cement Production Pathway]=
	Last Year Preexisting Clinker Production Capacity by Pathway[Cement Production Pathway\
		]
	- Preexisting Clinker Capacity Retirements by Pathway[Cement Production Pathway]
	~	metric tons/Year
	~	Start-year fleet nameplate available THIS year: the beginning-of-year \
		level less the capacity retiring at this year's campaign-end decisions \
		(the standard EPS level-plus-flows convention, aligned 2026-09-29). Feeds \
		this year's production and the total available capacity; the decision \
		chain reads the Last Year level so this year's retirements cannot feed \
		back into the decision that sets them.
	|

Preexisting Industrial Equipment Fuel Type Shares in Electricity Equivalent Adjusted by Early Retirement Policy\
		[Industry Category
,Industrial Process,Industrial Fuel]=
	IF THEN ELSE(
	  BIISRP Boolean Use Individual Industrial Site Retirement Profile[Industry Category\
		] = 0,
	
	  Start Year BAU Fuel Type Shares in Electricity Equivalent[Industry Category,Industrial Process\
		,Industrial Fuel]
	  *
	  IF THEN ELSE(
	    IFTQfEERP Industrial Fuel Types Qualifying for Early Equipment Retirement Policy\
		[Industrial Fuel] = 1,
	    ZIDZ(
	      1 - 
	      MAX(Minimum Share of Start Year Industrial Equipment Retired To Date[Industry Category\
		,Industrial Process],
	        IESD Fraction of Preexisting Equipment Retired Naturally by Age[Industrial Process\
		]),
	      1 - Fraction of All Preexisting Industrial Equipment Retired[Industry Category\
		,Industrial Process]
	    ),
	    ZIDZ(
	      1 - IESD Fraction of Preexisting Equipment Retired Naturally by Age[Industrial Process\
		],
	      1 - Fraction of All Preexisting Industrial Equipment Retired[Industry Category\
		,Industrial Process]
	    )
	  ),
	
	  Start Year BAU Fuel Type Shares in Electricity Equivalent[Industry Category,Industrial Process\
		,Industrial Fuel]
	  *
	  IF THEN ELSE(
	    IFTQfEERP Industrial Fuel Types Qualifying for Early Equipment Retirement Policy\
		[Industrial Fuel] = 1,
	    ZIDZ(
	      1 - 
	      MAX(Minimum Share of Start Year Industrial Equipment Retired To Date[Industry Category\
		,Industrial Process],
	        BIISRP BAU Individual Industrial Site Retirement Profile[Industry Category])\
		,
	      1 - Fraction of All Preexisting Industrial Equipment Retired[Industry Category\
		,Industrial Process]
	    ),
	    ZIDZ(
	      1 - BIISRP BAU Individual Industrial Site Retirement Profile[Industry Category\
		],
	      1 - Fraction of All Preexisting Industrial Equipment Retired[Industry Category\
		,Industrial Process]
	    )
	  )
	)
	~	Dmnl
	~		|

Present Value Discount and Lifetime Window Denominator[Industry Category,Industrial Process\
		,Future Year]=
	(1 + BCoISC Weighted Average Cost of Capital by Industry[Industry Category]
	 + HRPfIEP Hurdle Rate Premium for Industrial Equipment Purchases)
	^ ((Numerical Future Year Definitions[Future Year] - Time) / One Year)
	* IF THEN ELSE(
	    Numerical Future Year Definitions[Future Year] - Time >= IESD Average Age at Which Equipment Retires\
		[Industrial Process],
	    0,
	    IF THEN ELSE(Numerical Future Year Definitions[Future Year] < Time, 0, 1)
	  )
	~	Dmnl
	~	Discount factor at the decision discount rate (BCoISC plus the purchase \
		hurdle premium), masked to the equipment lifetime window.  All consumers \
		are future-flow valuations inside the fuel choice (fuel prices, fuel \
		taxes, carbon tax, and the hydrogen-branch lifetime annuity), so the \
		behavioral premium applies to every one of them; financing repayment math \
		uses the WACC-only unmasked discount factor instead.
	|

Present Value of Clean Industrial Heat Subsidy[Industry Category,Industrial Fuel,industrial heating process\
		]=
	Clean Industrial Heat Subsidy Rate by Fuel Type[Industrial Fuel,industrial heating process\
		]
	* Clean Industrial Heat Subsidy Annuity Factor[Industry Category]
	~	$/BTU
	~		|

Present Value of Industrial Equipment Annualized Capital Costs[Industry Category,Industrial Fuel\
		,Industrial Process]=
	Industrial Equipment Annualized Capital Costs per Unit Energy[Industry Category,Industrial Fuel\
		,Industrial Process]
	* Industrial Equipment Capital Annuity Factor[Industry Category,Industrial Process]
	~	$/BTU
	~		|

Present Value of Industrial Equipment Costs per Unit Energy[Industry Category,Industrial Fuel\
		,Industrial Process]=
	Present Value of Industrial Equipment Annualized Capital Costs[Industry Category,Industrial Fuel\
		,Industrial Process]
	+ Present Value of Industrial Equipment Costs per Unit Energy including Clean Heat Subsidy\
		[Industry Category,Industrial Fuel,Industrial Process]
	~	$/BTU
	~		|

Present Value of Industrial Equipment Costs per Unit Energy including Clean Heat Subsidy\
		[Industry Category,Industrial Fuel,Industrial Process] :EXCEPT:                 [Industry Category\
		,Industrial Fuel,industrial heating process]=
	(Present Value of Pretax Industrial Fuel Price in Future Years[Industry Category,Industrial Fuel\
		,Industrial Process]
	 + Present Value of Industrial Fuel Tax or Subsidy Rate by Fuel in Future Years[Industry Category\
		,Industrial Fuel,Industrial Process])
	
	* (1 - SoFCTiBoSFbI Share of Fuel Consumption That is Byproduct or Secondary Fuels by Industry\
		[Industry Category,Industrial Fuel
	])
	
	+ Present Value of Industrial Fuel Carbon Tax per Unit Energy in Future Years[Industry Category\
		,Industrial Fuel,Industrial Process] ~~|
Present Value of Industrial Equipment Costs per Unit Energy including Clean Heat Subsidy\
		[Industry Category,Industrial Fuel,industrial heating process]=
	(Present Value of Pretax Industrial Fuel Price in Future Years[Industry Category,Industrial Fuel\
		,industrial heating process]
	 + Present Value of Industrial Fuel Tax or Subsidy Rate by Fuel in Future Years[Industry Category\
		,Industrial Fuel,industrial heating process])
	
	* (1 - SoFCTiBoSFbI Share of Fuel Consumption That is Byproduct or Secondary Fuels by Industry\
		[Industry Category,Industrial Fuel
	])
	
	+ Present Value of Industrial Fuel Carbon Tax per Unit Energy in Future Years[Industry Category\
		,Industrial Fuel,industrial heating process]
	
	- Present Value of Clean Industrial Heat Subsidy[Industry Category,Industrial Fuel,industrial heating process
	]
	~	$/BTU
	~		|

Present Value of Industrial Fuel Carbon Tax per Unit Energy in Future Years[Industry Category\
		,Industrial Fuel,Industrial Process
]=
	Industrial Fuel Taxable CO2e Emissions Intensity[Industry Category,Industrial Fuel]
	* Fraction of Industry Category Subject to Carbon Tax[Industry Category]
	* Carbon Tax Present Value Factor[Industry Category,Industrial Process]
	~	$/BTU
	~		|

Present Value of Industrial Fuel Tax or Subsidy Rate by Fuel in Future Years[Industry Category\
		,Industrial Fuel,Industrial Process]=
	SUM( 
	  ZIDZ(
	    Fuel Tax or Subsidy Rate by Fuel in Future Years[industrial fuel within all fuels\
		 ,industry sector,Future Year!], 
	    Present Value Discount and Lifetime Window Denominator[Industry Category,Industrial Process\
		,Future Year!]
	  ) 
	)
	~	$/BTU
	~		|

Present Value of Pretax Industrial Fuel Price in Future Years[Industry Category,Industrial Fuel\
		,Industrial Process] :EXCEPT:     [Industry Category,green hydrogen if,Industrial Process\
		],[Industry Category,low carbon hydrogen if,Industrial Process
],[Industry Category,hydrogen if,Industrial Process]=
	SUM( 
	  ZIDZ( 
	    Pretax Fuel Price by Sector in Future Years after Fuel Price Deregulation[industrial fuel within all fuels\
		 ,industry sector ,Future Year!], 
	    Present Value Discount and Lifetime Window Denominator[Industry Category,Industrial Process\
		,Future Year!]
	  ) 
	) ~~|
Present Value of Pretax Industrial Fuel Price in Future Years[Industry Category,green hydrogen if\
		,Industrial Process]=
	Smoothed Green Hydrogen Cost
	* Industrial Equipment Lifetime Annuity Factor[Industry Category,Industrial Process]\
		 ~~|
Present Value of Pretax Industrial Fuel Price in Future Years[Industry Category,low carbon hydrogen if\
		,Industrial Process]=
	Smoothed Low Carbon Hydrogen Cost
	* Industrial Equipment Lifetime Annuity Factor[Industry Category,Industrial Process]\
		 ~~|
Present Value of Pretax Industrial Fuel Price in Future Years[Industry Category,hydrogen if\
		,Industrial Process]=
	Smoothed Other Hydrogen Cost
	* Industrial Equipment Lifetime Annuity Factor[Industry Category,Industrial Process]
	~	$/BTU
	~		|

Present Value Unmasked Decision Discount Factor[Industry Category,Future Year]=
	(1 + BCoISC Weighted Average Cost of Capital by Industry[Industry Category]
	 + HRPfIEP Hurdle Rate Premium for Industrial Equipment Purchases)
	^ ((Numerical Future Year Definitions[Future Year] - Time) / One Year)
	~	Dmnl
	~	Twin of Present Value Unmasked Discount Factor at the decision discount \
		rate (WACC plus the purchase hurdle premium).  Used by the clean heat \
		subsidy annuity factor, a future-flow valuation inside the fuel choice; \
		the capital annuity factor keeps the WACC-only twin because financing \
		repayments are real cash flows, not a behavioral valuation.
	|

Present Value Unmasked Discount Factor[Industry Category,Future Year]=
	(1 + BCoISC Weighted Average Cost of Capital by Industry[Industry Category])
	^ ((Numerical Future Year Definitions[Future Year] - Time) / One Year)
	~	Dmnl
	~	WACC-only discount factor; used by the capital annuity factor (financing \
		repayment window).  The fuel-choice future-flow valuations use the \
		decision-rate twin above.
	|

Pretax Fuel Cost per Unit Energy by Sector after Fuel Price Deregulation[All Fuels,Sectors\
		]=
	(Pretax Fuel Cost per Unit Energy by Sector After Reduction of BAU Subsidies[All Fuels\
		,Sectors]
	* (1 - Fuel Price Deregulation This Year[All Fuels])
	+
	IMFPbFT International Market Fuel Price by Fuel Type[All Fuels]
	* Fuel Price Deregulation This Year[All Fuels])
	
	* (1 + Policy Driven Change in Fuel Price This Year[All Fuels,Sectors])
	~	$/BTU
	~		|

Pretax Fuel Cost per Unit Energy by Sector After Modeled Price Adjustments[All Fuels,\
		Sectors]=
	IF THEN ELSE(
	  BAU Pretax Fuel Cost per Unit Energy by Sector with Sensitivity Analysis Adjustment\
		[All Fuels,Sectors] > 0,
	
	  MAX(
	    0,
	
	    BAU Pretax Fuel Cost per Unit Energy by Sector with Sensitivity Analysis Adjustment\
		[All Fuels,Sectors]
	    + Change in Baseline Energy Costs[All Fuels]
	  ),
	
	  0
	)
	~	$/BTU
	~	A price of zero typically means a fuel isn't used in a given sector, so we \
		only add the value of the change in baseline fuel cost to sectors where \
		the fuel has a non-zero price, for clarity of output in the model.  (The \
		price of a fuel that is not used makes no difference to the numerical \
		results.)
	|

Pretax Fuel Cost per Unit Energy by Sector After Reduction of BAU Subsidies[All Fuels\
		,Sectors]=
	IF THEN ELSE(
	  Pretax Fuel Cost per Unit Energy by Sector After Modeled Price Adjustments[All Fuels\
		,Sectors] > 0,
	
	  Pretax Fuel Cost per Unit Energy by Sector After Modeled Price Adjustments[All Fuels\
		,Sectors]
	  + Added Thermal Fuel Cost per Unit Energy from Ending Subsidies[All Fuels,Sectors]\
		,
	
	  0
	)
	~	$/BTU
	~	A price of zero typically means a fuel isn't used in a given sector, so we \
		only add the value of the removed subsidies to sectors where the fuel has \
		a non-zero price, for clarity of output in the model.  (The price of a \
		fuel that is not used makes no difference to the numerical results.)
	|

Pretax Fuel Price by Sector in Future Years after Fuel Price Deregulation[All Fuels,Sectors\
		,Future Year] :EXCEPT:       [
electricity
,Sectors,Future Year]=
	IF THEN ELSE(
	  Dimensionless Time < FYIbFP First Year Impacted by Future Policies,
	  Pretax Fuel Price by Sector in Future Years After Reduction of BAU Subsidies[All Fuels\
		,Sectors,Future Year], 
	  
	  Pretax Fuel Price by Sector in Future Years After Reduction of BAU Subsidies[All Fuels\
		,Sectors,Future Year]
	  * (1 - Fuel Price Deregulation This Year[All Fuels])
	  +
	  IMFPbFT International Market Fuel Price by Fuel Type[All Fuels]
	  * Fuel Price Deregulation This Year[All Fuels]
	) ~~|
Pretax Fuel Price by Sector in Future Years after Fuel Price Deregulation[electricity\
		,Sectors,Future Year]=
	Average Five Year Prior Pretax Electricity Price by Sector[Sectors]
	~	$/BTU
	~		|

Pretax Fuel Price by Sector in Future Years After Modeled Price Adjustments[All Fuels\
		,Sectors,Future Year]=
	IF THEN ELSE(
	  Dimensionless Time < FYIbFP First Year Impacted by Future Policies,
	  BFPAT BAU Pretax Fuel Price by Sector[All Fuels,Sectors,Future Year],
	
	  IF THEN ELSE(
	    BFPAT BAU Pretax Fuel Price by Sector[All Fuels,Sectors,Future Year] > 0,
	 
	    MAX(
	      0,
	
	      BFPAT BAU Pretax Fuel Price by Sector[All Fuels,Sectors,Future Year]
	      + Change in Baseline Energy Costs[All Fuels]
	    ),
	
	    0
	  )
	)
	~	$/BTU
	~		|

Pretax Fuel Price by Sector in Future Years After Reduction of BAU Subsidies[All Fuels\
		,Sectors,Future Year]=
	IF THEN ELSE(
	  Dimensionless Time < FYIbFP First Year Impacted by Future Policies,
	  Pretax Fuel Price by Sector in Future Years After Modeled Price Adjustments[All Fuels\
		,Sectors,Future Year],
	
	  IF THEN ELSE(
	    Pretax Fuel Price by Sector in Future Years After Modeled Price Adjustments[All Fuels\
		,Sectors,Future Year] > 0,
	
	    Pretax Fuel Price by Sector in Future Years After Modeled Price Adjustments[All Fuels\
		,Sectors,Future Year]
	    + Added Thermal Fuel Cost per Unit Energy from Ending Subsidies[All Fuels,Sectors\
		],
	  
	    0
	  )
	)
	~	$/BTU
	~		|

Price Increase for New Indst Eqpt Due to Energy Intensity Improvement[Industry Category\
		,Industrial Fuel,Industrial Process]=
	- Improvement in New Industrial Eqpt Energy Intensity vs BAU[Industry Category,Industrial Fuel\
		,Industrial Process]
	* EoIEPwEEI Elasticity of Industrial Eqpt Price wrt Energy Eff Improvement[Industrial Process\
		,Industrial Fuel]
	~	Dmnl
	~	Capital price premium on new equipment whose energy intensity improves \
		relative to BAU, from any cause: efficiency standards, or \
		fuel-cost-induced efficiency via EoIEECwEC (e.g. carbon taxes, fuel taxes, \
		subsidies).  Negative when intensity worsens vs BAU (e.g. under fuel \
		subsidies).
	|

primary fuel:
	hard coal,
	natural gas,
	nuclear,
	biomass,
	lignite,
	crude oil,
	municipal solid waste
	~	
	~		|

