Process Efficiency Measure:
	GET DIRECT SUBSCRIPT(
	   'InputData/indst/PEaWHRP/PEaWHRP-PM.csv',
	   ',',
	   'A2',
	   'A',
	   ''
	)
	~	
	~	Generic slots for industry process efficiency measures.  Unused slots \
		carry zero savings potential and are inert.
	|

Process Efficiency Measure Capital Cost per Unit Annual Energy Saved after Capital Subsidy\
		[Industry Category,Process Efficiency Measure]=
	PEMCC Process Efficiency Measure Capital Cost per Unit Annual Energy Saved[Industry Category\
		,Process Efficiency Measure]
	* (1 - Perc Subsidy for Process Efficiency Measure Capital Cost This Year[Industry Category\
		])
	~	$/BTU
	~	Capital cost of each process efficiency measure per BTU of annual energy \
		savings capacity, net of the capital cost subsidy.  Used only in the \
		payback-based adoption decision; financing and cash flows are computed on \
		the gross capital cost.
	|

Process Efficiency Measure Capital Subsidy Amount Paid[Industry Category]=
	New Process Efficiency Measure Capital Expenditures[Industry Category]
	* Perc Subsidy for Process Efficiency Measure Capital Cost This Year[Industry Category\
		]
	~	$
	~	Government payments covering the subsidized share of the capital cost of \
		process efficiency measure equipment installed this year.  Firms still pay \
		and finance the gross capital cost; the subsidy arrives as a government \
		transfer, mirroring the clean industrial heat investment subsidy \
		convention.
	|

Process Efficiency Measure Deployment Retired This Year[Industry Category,Process Efficiency Measure\
		,Measure Cost Level]= DELAY FIXED (
	New Process Efficiency Measure Deployment This Year[Industry Category,Process Efficiency Measure\
		,Measure Cost Level],
	PEMEL Process Efficiency Measure Equipment Lifetime[Industry Category,Process Efficiency Measure\
		],
	0)
	~	Dmnl
	~	Deployed savings retire at the end of each measure's lifetime, re-opening \
		potential for redeployment.
	|

Process Efficiency Measure Energy Savings This Year[Industry Category]=
	SUM(
	  Deployed Fraction of Process Efficiency Measure Potential[Industry Category,Process Efficiency Measure\
		!,Measure Cost Level!]
	  * MCLS Measure Cost Level Share[Measure Cost Level!]
	  * (PEMFSP Process Efficiency Measure Fuel Savings Potential[Industry Category,Process Efficiency Measure\
		!]
	     * Industrial Combustion Fuel Base for Measures[Industry Category]
	     + PEMESP Process Efficiency Measure Elec Savings Potential[Industry Category,Process Efficiency Measure\
		!]
	     * Industrial Electricity Base for Measures[Industry Category])
	)
	~	BTU
	~	Energy saved this year by deployed process efficiency measures.  Basis for \
		OM costs and performance subsidy payments.
	|

Process Efficiency Measure OM Expenditures[Industry Category]=
	SUM(
	  Deployed Fraction of Process Efficiency Measure Potential[Industry Category,Process Efficiency Measure\
		!,Measure Cost Level!]
	  * MCLS Measure Cost Level Share[Measure Cost Level!]
	  * (PEMFSP Process Efficiency Measure Fuel Savings Potential[Industry Category,Process Efficiency Measure\
		!]
	     * Industrial Combustion Fuel Base for Measures[Industry Category]
	     + PEMESP Process Efficiency Measure Elec Savings Potential[Industry Category,Process Efficiency Measure\
		!]
	     * Industrial Electricity Base for Measures[Industry Category])
	  * PEMOC Process Efficiency Measure OM Cost per Unit Energy Saved[Industry Category\
		,Process Efficiency Measure!]
	)
	~	$
	~	Annual OM cost of deployed process efficiency measures, at unadjusted \
		input costs.  May be negative for measures that reduce nonenergy operating \
		costs.
	|

Process Efficiency Measure Performance Subsidy[Industry Category]=
	0,	{agriculture and forestry 01T03}
	0,	{coal mining 05}
	0,	{oil and gas extraction 06}
	0,	{other mining and quarrying 07T08}
	0,	{food beverage and tobacco 10T12}
	0,	{textiles apparel and leather 13T15}
	0,	{wood products 16}
	0,	{pulp paper and printing 17T18}
	0,	{refined petroleum and coke 19}
	0,	{chemicals 20}
	0,	{rubber and plastic products 22}
	0,	{glass and glass products 231}
	0,	{cement and other nonmetallic minerals 239}
	0,	{iron and steel 241}
	0,	{other metals 242}
	0,	{metal products except machinery and vehicles 25}
	0,	{computers and electronics 26}
	0,	{appliances and electrical equipment 27}
	0,	{other machinery 28}
	0,	{road vehicles 29}
	0,	{nonroad vehicles 30}
	0,	{other manufacturing 31T33}
	0,	{energy pipelines and gas processing 352T353}
	0,	{water and waste 36T39}
	0	{construction 41T43}
	~	output currency unit/output TPE unit [0,20,0.25]
	~	Subsidy paid per MMBtu of annual energy savings delivered by deployed \
		process efficiency measures.  Adds to the value of measures in the \
		cost-effectiveness screen and is paid out as a government cash flow.
	|

Process Efficiency Measure Performance Subsidy Amount Paid[Industry Category]=
	Process Efficiency Measure Energy Savings This Year[Industry Category]
	* Process Efficiency Measure Performance Subsidy This Year[Industry Category]
	~	$
	~		|

Process Efficiency Measure Performance Subsidy in Dollars[Industry Category]=
	Process Efficiency Measure Performance Subsidy[Industry Category]
	* OCCF Dollars per Small Output Currency Unit
	~	$/output TPE unit
	~		|

Process Efficiency Measure Performance Subsidy This Year[Industry Category]=
	Process Efficiency Measure Performance Subsidy in Dollars[Industry Category]
	* VECTOR ELM MAP(
	    Selected Policy Implementation Schedule[indst efficiency measure performance subsidy X agriculture and forestry 01T03\
		],
	
	    (Industry Category-1)
	  )
	~	$/output TPE unit
	~		|

Process Efficiency Measure Potential Remaining After BAU Deployment[Industry Category\
		,Process Efficiency Measure]=
	1 - PEMBDS Process Efficiency Measure BAU Deployed Share by End Year[Industry Category\
		,Process Efficiency Measure]
	* (Time - INITIAL TIME) / (FINAL TIME - INITIAL TIME)
	~	Dmnl
	~	Fraction of each measure's loaded potential still available to policy this \
		year, after netting out deployment the BAU case achieves on its own.  BAU \
		deployment ramps linearly from zero at the start year to PEMBDS at the \
		final year, so the ramp adapts to each geography's run period.  Caps both \
		the economic and standards deployment channels.  In the US PEMBDS is zero \
		and this equals 1 in all years.
	|

Process Efficiency Measure Simple Payback Period[Industry Category,Process Efficiency Measure\
		,Measure Cost Level]=
	IF THEN ELSE(
	  Value of Process Efficiency Measure Energy Savings[Industry Category,Process Efficiency Measure\
		]
	  - MCLM Measure Cost Level Multiplier[Measure Cost Level]
	    * PEMOC Process Efficiency Measure OM Cost per Unit Energy Saved[Industry Category\
		,Process Efficiency Measure] <= 0,

	  10,

	  MIN(10,
	    ZIDZ(
	      MCLM Measure Cost Level Multiplier[Measure Cost Level]
	      * Process Efficiency Measure Capital Cost per Unit Annual Energy Saved after Capital Subsidy\
		[Industry Category,Process Efficiency Measure],

	      Value of Process Efficiency Measure Energy Savings[Industry Category,Process Efficiency Measure\
		]
	      - MCLM Measure Cost Level Multiplier[Measure Cost Level]
	        * PEMOC Process Efficiency Measure OM Cost per Unit Energy Saved[Industry Category\
		,Process Efficiency Measure]
	    )
	  )
	)
	~	Dmnl
	~	Simple payback in years at the policy-inclusive value of saved energy (a \
		performance subsidy shortens the payback by raising the value, and a \
		capital subsidy shortens it by cutting the upfront cost).  Capped at the \
		acceptance curve's ten-year endpoint, where acceptance is approximately \
		zero; measures whose operating cost exceeds their value are assigned the \
		cap.
	|

Process Efficiency Measure Simple Payback Period at BAU Prices[Industry Category,Process Efficiency Measure\
		,Measure Cost Level]=
	IF THEN ELSE(
	  Process Efficiency Measure Value at BAU Prices[Industry Category,Process Efficiency Measure\
		]
	  - MCLM Measure Cost Level Multiplier[Measure Cost Level]
	    * PEMOC Process Efficiency Measure OM Cost per Unit Energy Saved[Industry Category\
		,Process Efficiency Measure] <= 0,

	  10,

	  MIN(10,
	    ZIDZ(
	      MCLM Measure Cost Level Multiplier[Measure Cost Level]
	      * PEMCC Process Efficiency Measure Capital Cost per Unit Annual Energy Saved[Industry Category\
		,Process Efficiency Measure],

	      Process Efficiency Measure Value at BAU Prices[Industry Category,Process Efficiency Measure\
		]
	      - MCLM Measure Cost Level Multiplier[Measure Cost Level]
	        * PEMOC Process Efficiency Measure OM Cost per Unit Energy Saved[Industry Category\
		,Process Efficiency Measure]
	    )
	  )
	)
	~	Dmnl
	~	Simple payback in years at BAU prices with no subsidy.  Firms whose \
		acceptance threshold exceeds this payback are treated as having already \
		adopted (embedded in the calibrated base year and the diffusion netting of \
		the potentials).
	|

Process Efficiency Measure Tranche Economically Deployable[Industry Category,Process Efficiency Measure\
		,Measure Cost Level]=
	IF THEN ELSE(
	  PEMFSP Process Efficiency Measure Fuel Savings Potential[Industry Category,Process Efficiency Measure\
		]
	  + PEMESP Process Efficiency Measure Elec Savings Potential[Industry Category,Process Efficiency Measure\
		] <= 0,

	  0,

	  MAX(0,
	    ( PAC Payback Acceptance Curve[process efficiency measures](
	        Process Efficiency Measure Simple Payback Period[Industry Category,Process Efficiency Measure\
		,Measure Cost Level] )
	      - PAC Payback Acceptance Curve[process efficiency measures](
	          Process Efficiency Measure Simple Payback Period at BAU Prices[Industry Category\
		,Process Efficiency Measure,Measure Cost Level] )
	      - 1e-06 )
	    / MAX( 1e-06,
	        PAC Payback Acceptance Curve[process efficiency measures](0)
	        - PAC Payback Acceptance Curve[process efficiency measures](
	              Process Efficiency Measure Simple Payback Period at BAU Prices[Industry Category\
		,Process Efficiency Measure,Measure Cost Level] ) )
	  )
	)
	~	Dmnl
	~	Deployment ceiling, as a fraction of tranche potential.  Firms are \
		heterogeneous in the simple payback they will accept (the PAC curve, \
		anchored on audited-plant adoption data).  Policy that shortens the \
		payback wins over the firms whose thresholds lie between the BAU and \
		policy paybacks; the denominator scales that gain against the most any \
		policy could win, so driving the payback to zero unlocks the full loaded \
		potential.  At zero policy the two paybacks are equal and the ceiling is \
		exactly zero.  Baseline-policy deployment, where a geography has it, is \
		handled separately by the BAU deployed share input rather than assumed \
		here.  Same mechanism as the NEMS Industrial Demand Module payback \
		acceptance curve and the FORECAST payback-time-expectation distribution.  \
		Empty measure slots are never deployable.
	|

Process Efficiency Measure Value at BAU Prices[Industry Category,Process Efficiency Measure\
		]=
	ZIDZ(
	  PEMFSP Process Efficiency Measure Fuel Savings Potential[Industry Category,Process Efficiency Measure\
		]
	  * BAU Industrial Combustion Fuel Base for Measures[Industry Category]
	  * BAU Blended Industrial Combustion Fuel Cost per Unit Energy[Industry Category]
	  + PEMESP Process Efficiency Measure Elec Savings Potential[Industry Category,Process Efficiency Measure\
		]
	  * BAU Industrial Electricity Base for Measures[Industry Category]
	  * BAU Industrial Fuel Cost per Unit Energy[Industry Category,electricity if],

	  PEMFSP Process Efficiency Measure Fuel Savings Potential[Industry Category,Process Efficiency Measure\
		]
	  * BAU Industrial Combustion Fuel Base for Measures[Industry Category]
	  + PEMESP Process Efficiency Measure Elec Savings Potential[Industry Category,Process Efficiency Measure\
		]
	  * BAU Industrial Electricity Base for Measures[Industry Category]
	)
	~	$/BTU
	~	Value of each measure's savings at BAU energy prices with BAU quantity \
		weights and no subsidy.  A pure BAU benchmark for the counterfactual \
		screen.
	|

Process Emissions Abated due to Carbon Tax by Marginal Cost[Industry by Process Emissions Policy\
		,Marginal Abatement Cost] :EXCEPT:           [cement industry measures,Marginal Abatement Cost\
		],[ngp production methane capture,Marginal Abatement Cost
],[ngp production methane destruction,Marginal Abatement Cost],[ngp processing TnD methane capture\
		,Marginal Abatement Cost
],[ngp processing TnD methane destruction,Marginal Abatement Cost],[chemicals f gas substitution\
		,Marginal Abatement Cost
],[chemicals f gas destruction,Marginal Abatement Cost],[chemicals f gas recovery,Marginal Abatement Cost\
		],[chemicals inspct maint retrofit
,Marginal Abatement Cost],[coal mining methane capture,Marginal Abatement Cost],[coal mining methane destruction\
		,Marginal Abatement Cost
],[waste management methane capture,Marginal Abatement Cost],[waste management methane destruction\
		,Marginal Abatement Cost
],[agriculture tillage,Marginal Abatement Cost],[chemicals N2O abatement,Marginal Abatement Cost\
		],[other metals inspct maint retrofit
,Marginal Abatement Cost]=
	IF THEN ELSE(
	
	  Does Carbon Tax Exempt Process Emissions This Year[agriculture and forestry 01T03]
	  = 0
	
	  :AND:
	
	  Industry Pollutants Taxed by Carbon Pricing[agriculture and forestry 01T03,CH4]
	  = 1
	
	  :AND:
	
	  Industry Pollutants Taxed by Carbon Pricing[agriculture and forestry 01T03,N2O]
	  = 1
	
	  :AND:
	
	  PERAC Marginal Cost Definitions[Marginal Abatement Cost]
	  > 0
	
	  :AND:
	
	  Incremental Carbon Tax from Policy[agriculture and forestry 01T03]
	  > PERAC Marginal Cost Definitions[Marginal Abatement Cost],
	
	  PERAC Mass CO2e Avoidable by Marginal Cost[Industry by Process Emissions Policy,Marginal Abatement Cost\
		],
	
	  0
	) ~~|
Process Emissions Abated due to Carbon Tax by Marginal Cost[cement industry measures,\
		Marginal Abatement Cost]=
	0
	{RETIRED WITH THE CEMENT BREAKOUT (Phase 2a adversarial-review finding,
	2026-08-24): this MAC element represents cement measures (clinker
	substitution etc.), and cement's process CO2 is now owned by the cement
	pathway module - under any industry carbon tax above the PERAC bin
	(~$0.1-10/t) the old branch fired its FULL 17-23 MMT potential against
	the non-cement RESIDUAL of 239's process emissions (lime and other
	carbonates), annihilating it, while the cost chain booked spending for
	abatement the MAX(0) floor discarded. Cement abatement economics now
	live in the module (pathway choice, calcination pricing; the SCM /
	clinker-substitution lever is the planned successor to this element).
	Restore only if a lime-appropriate curve is authored for the residual.} ~~|
