Taxes Paid on Geoengineering Fuels[Industrial Fuel]=
	QUANTUM(
	  Geoengineering Sector Energy Use[Industrial Fuel]
	  * Geoengineering Fuel Total Tax or Subsidy Amount per Unit Energy[Industrial Fuel]\
		,
	
	  QSfCF Quantization Size for Cash Flows
	)
	~	$
	~	We use the tax amount per unit fuel from the cement industry as a model \
		for tax amounts per unit fuel from industry more generally, for use to \
		represent tax amount per unit fuel used by geoengineering.
	|

Taxes Paid on Industry Fuels[Industry Category,Industrial Fuel]=
	Industrial Fuel Use for Energy Purposes[Industrial Fuel,Industry Category]
	  * Industrial Fuel Total Tax or Subsidy Amount per Unit Energy[Industry Category,Industrial Fuel\
		]
	  * (1 - SoFCTiBoSFbI Share of Fuel Consumption That is Byproduct or Secondary Fuels by Industry\
		[Industry Category,Industrial Fuel])
	
	+ Industrial Fuel Use for Non Energy Purposes[Industry Category,Industrial Fuel]
	  * (
	      Industrial Fuel Total Tax or Subsidy Amount per Unit Energy[Industry Category,\
		Industrial Fuel]
	      - Industrial Fuel Carbon Tax Amount per Unit Energy[Industry Category,Industrial Fuel\
		]
	    )
	~	$
	~		|

TBCC Thermal Battery Capital Cost per Unit Annual Energy Served[industrial heating process\
		]=
	GET DIRECT CONSTANTS(
	   'InputData/indst/TBCC/TBCC.csv',
	   ',',
	   'B2*'
	)
	~	$/BTU
	~	Thermal battery system capital cost (charging equipment, storage medium, \
		and heat delivery equipment) per BTU of annual heat service delivered, in \
		electricity-equivalent service units, matching the IECCpUAEU convention. \
		By temperature band: the heat-delivery component blends a steam-delivery \
		basis (Rissman and Gimon 2023 heat exchangers) with a direct hot-air basis \
		(Stack, Curtis and Forsberg 2019 blower, CPI adjusted), weighted by each \
		band's steam-served share of heat demand derived from EIA MECS 2022 \
		end-use data. Derivation and assumptions are in the TBCC input workbook - \
		verify against primary sources before release.
	|

TBCH Thermal Battery Daily Charging Hours=
	GET DIRECT CONSTANTS(
	   'InputData/elec/TBCH/TBCH.csv',
	   ',',
	   'B2*'
	)
	~	hours/day
	~	Number of hours per representative day in which grid-charging thermal \
		batteries buy electricity (the cheapest six hours of each 24-hour period, \
		per the issue 387 simplification). An equipment design parameter, not a \
		market outcome: it is coupled to the TBCC capital cost derivation, whose \
		charging equipment is sized at 24/TBCH times the discharge rate. WHICH \
		hours are cheapest is computed endogenously each year in Thermal Battery \
		Charging Window.
	|

TBEM Thermal Battery Eligibility Mask[industrial heating process]=
	GET DIRECT CONSTANTS(
	   'InputData/indst/TBEM/TBEM.csv',
	   ',',
	   'B2*'
	)
	~	Dmnl
	~	Fraction of this process's heat demand thermal batteries can technically \
		serve. Nonzero only for the five heat temperature bands. Bands at or below \
		1000 C are fully eligible (delivery capability is 1500 to 1700 C; Rissman \
		and Gimon 2023). The heat above 1000 C element is 0.76: the share of that \
		band's demand not chemistry-limited (primary steelmaking and precision \
		welding are excluded per Rissman and Gimon 2023, cross-checked against the \
		DOE Iron and Steel Bandwidth Study). Applied as a market-segmentation \
		factor in the share equations, not a choice-weight multiplier: a weight \
		multiplier cannot cap the share when the battery weight dominates the \
		logit. Derivation in the TBEM input workbook.
	|

TBMLE Thermal Battery Charging Mode Logit Exponent=
	GET DIRECT CONSTANTS(
	   'InputData/indst/TBMLE/TBMLE.csv',
	   ',',
	   'B2*'
	)
	~	Dmnl
	~	Exponent of the relative-cost logit that splits thermal battery charging \
		between grid (price-hunting) and dedicated off-grid (generation-following) \
		supply. Default -6 matches IELC; more negative values make the cheaper \
		mode dominate more completely.
	|

TBMS Thermal Battery Charging Mode Shareweights[Thermal Battery Charging Mode]=
	GET DIRECT CONSTANTS(
	   'InputData/indst/TBMS/TBMS.csv',
	   ',',
	   'B2*'
	)
	~	Dmnl
	~	Shareweights on the thermal battery charging mode logit. Both 1 by default \
		(pure cost competition); set a mode to 0 to disable it entirely - this \
		replaces the former SoTBCfG exogenous mode-share control setting.
	|

TBPD Thermal Battery PTC Duration=
	GET DIRECT CONSTANTS(
	   'InputData/indst/TBPD/TBPD.csv',
	   ',',
	   'B2*'
	)
	~	years
	~	Number of years each thermal battery equipment vintage receives the \
		production tax credit rate that was in effect when it was installed, \
		mirroring BCHPD BAU Clean Heat PTC Duration. Default 10 years \
		(placed-in-service convention of recent federal PTCs).
	|

TBRTE Thermal Battery Round Trip Efficiency[industrial heating process]=
	GET DIRECT CONSTANTS(
	   'InputData/indst/TBRTE/TBRTE.csv',
	   ',',
	   'B2*'
	)
	~	Dmnl
	~	Electricity-in to heat-out round trip efficiency of industrial thermal \
		batteries.
	|

TBS Thermal Battery Shareweight:INTERPOLATE::=
	GET DIRECT DATA(
	   'InputData/indst/TBS/TBS.csv',
	   ',',
	   '1',
	   'B2'
	)
	~	Dmnl
	~	Entry-gate shareweight time series for thermal batteries in the industrial \
		new equipment fuel choice, analogous to the FIEF feasibility floor role \
		for clean entrant fuels. A time series (not a constant) so \
		commercialization timing can be represented as a ramp: it must be
	|

TCCpUNEC Transmission Construction Cost per Unit New Electricity Capacity=
	GET DIRECT CONSTANTS(
	   'InputData/elec/TCCpUNEC/TCCpUNEC.csv',
	   ',',
	   'B2*'
	)
	~	$/MW
	~		|

Thermal Battery Annual Costs and Annual Financing Repayment per Unit Energy in Electricity Equivalent\
		[Industry Category,
industrial heating process]=
	(
	  Thermal Battery Annual Financing Repayment per Unit Energy in Electricity Equivalent\
		[Industry Category,industrial heating process]
	
	  + Thermal Battery Blended Charging Electricity Cost per Unit Energy
	    * Start Year Electric Heating Equipment Effective COP[industrial heating process\
		]
	    / TBRTE Thermal Battery Round Trip Efficiency[industrial heating process]
	    * Industrial Equipment Lifetime Annuity Factor[Industry Category,industrial heating process\
		]
	
	  - Subsidy for Thermal Battery Heat Production This Year[industrial heating process\
		]
	    / BpTPEU BTU per Medium Primary Energy Unit
	    * Thermal Battery Production Subsidy Annuity Factor[Industry Category]
	)
	~	$/BTU
	~	Thermal battery levelized cost per unit of energy service, built to be \
		directly comparable with Industrial Equipment Annual Costs and Annual \
		Financing Repayment per Unit Energy in Electricity Equivalent: battery \
		capital annualized with the electric-equipment capital recovery factor and \
		capital annuity factor, plus the present value of charging electricity \
		grossed up by round trip efficiency, plus the present value of O and M. \
		Mirrors the electricity fuel branch's basis (no PIFURfE divisor; \
		electricity is the efficiency reference) but deliberately does NOT apply \
		the electric-equipment efficiency-improvement intensity ratio: that \
		trajectory belongs to process-specific electric equipment, not thermal \
		batteries. Thermal battery efficiency is held constant over time until a \
		dedicated efficiency-forecast time series input exists (flagged for team \
		review). Charging is priced by the mode logit; no fuel taxes apply to the \
		off-grid mode and the carbon tax term is zero. The clean industrial heat \
		subsidy is deliberately omitted in this first version (conservative; \
		parity item flagged for team review).
	|

Thermal Battery Annual Financing Repayment per Unit Energy in Electricity Equivalent[\
		Industry Category,industrial heating process]=
	TBCC Thermal Battery Capital Cost per Unit Annual Energy Served[industrial heating process\
		]
	  * (1 - Perc Subsidy for Thermal Battery Equipment This Year[industrial heating process\
		])
	  * Capital Recovery Factor by Industry Fuel and Process[Industry Category,electricity if\
		,industrial heating process]
	  * Industrial Equipment Capital Annuity Factor[Industry Category,industrial heating process\
		]
	~	$/BTU
	~		|

Thermal Battery Blended Charging Electricity Cost per Unit Energy=
	SUM(
	  Thermal Battery Charging Mode Share[Thermal Battery Charging Mode!]
	  * Thermal Battery Electricity Cost by Mode[Thermal Battery Charging Mode!]
	)
	~	$/BTU
	~	Charging electricity cost seen by the thermal battery fuel choice: the \
		share-weighted cost across the two charging modes, whose shares come from \
		the endogenous mode logit (nested choice: modes compete on cost here, and \
		this composite competes against the other heat technologies in the \
		equipment fuel choice, following the GCAM convention of passing a nest's \
		share-weighted cost upward).
	|

Thermal Battery Charging Electricity Demand[Industry Category]=
	SUM(
	  Thermal Battery Energy Service[Industry Category,industrial heating process!]
	  * Start Year Electric Heating Equipment Effective COP[industrial heating process!]
	  / TBRTE Thermal Battery Round Trip Efficiency[industrial heating process!]
	)
	~	BTU
	~	Electricity absorbed by thermal battery charging: heat service delivered \
		(electricity equivalent basis) grossed up by round trip losses. Kept \
		entirely outside Industrial Fuel Use so the flat industrial electricity \
		rate never prices it; it is paid for through the charging mode supply \
		chains instead (dedicated off-grid renewables and cheap-hour grid \
		purchases, split by the mode logit).
	|

Thermal Battery Charging Electricity Demand by Vintage[Industry Category,Model Run Vintage\
		]=
	SUM(
	  Output from Industrial Equipment Installed During Model Run[Industry Category,industrial heating process\
		!,Model Run Vintage]
	  * Last Year Thermal Battery Shares by Vintage[Industry Category,industrial heating process\
		!,Model Run Vintage]
	  * Start Year Electric Heating Equipment Effective COP[industrial heating process!]
	  / TBRTE Thermal Battery Round Trip Efficiency[industrial heating process!]
	)
	* Start Year Energy Intensity of Industrial Production in Electricity Equivalent[Industry Category\
		]
	~	BTU
	~	Charging electricity absorbed by each surviving thermal battery cohort: \
		the vintage term of Thermal Battery Energy Service before Demand Cap \
		grossed up by round trip losses. Cohort usage follows the equipment \
		vintage stock's output allocation, so existing thermal batteries are \
		utilized at the same rate as other industrial equipment as industries grow \
		or shrink.
	|

Thermal Battery Charging Mode:
	tb grid charging, tb off grid charging
	~	Dmnl
	~	The two ways an industrial thermal battery can source its charging \
		electricity: grid purchases during the charging-window hours \
		(price-hunting) or dedicated off-grid renewables (generation-following). \
		They compete on cost in a nested logit whose share-weighted cost feeds the \
		equipment fuel choice.
	|

Thermal Battery Charging Mode Choice Weight[Thermal Battery Charging Mode]=
	TBMS Thermal Battery Charging Mode Shareweights[Thermal Battery Charging Mode]
	* (
	  MAX(Thermal Battery Electricity Cost by Mode[Thermal Battery Charging Mode], 1e-07\
		)
	  / MAX(
	      VMIN(Thermal Battery Electricity Cost by Mode[Thermal Battery Charging Mode!])\
		,
	      1e-07
	    )
	) ^ TBMLE Thermal Battery Charging Mode Logit Exponent
	~	Dmnl
	~	Relative-cost (power function) logit weight for each charging mode, same \
		form as Industrial Equipment Fuel Choice Weight; the reference (cheapest \
		mode) cancels out of the shares.
	|

Thermal Battery Charging Mode Share[Thermal Battery Charging Mode]=
	ZIDZ(
	  Thermal Battery Charging Mode Choice Weight[Thermal Battery Charging Mode],
	  SUM(Thermal Battery Charging Mode Choice Weight[Thermal Battery Charging Mode!])
	)
	~	Dmnl
	~	Endogenous split of thermal battery charging between grid and dedicated \
		off-grid supply, applied to NEW additions only: it drives the blended cost \
		seen by the fuel choice and is latched into each cohort at installation \
		(Thermal Battery Charging Mode Share by Vintage). Existing capacity keeps \
		its install-year mode; fleet routing and payments use the vintage-weighted \
		shares (Thermal Battery Grid Charging Share by Industry).
	|

Thermal Battery Charging Mode Share by Vintage[Thermal Battery Charging Mode,Model Run Vintage\
		]=
	IF THEN ELSE(
	  This Year Model Run Vintage Flag[Model Run Vintage] = 1,
	  Thermal Battery Charging Mode Share[Thermal Battery Charging Mode],
	  IF THEN ELSE(
	    Dimensionless Time < Model Run Vintage to Year Mapping[Model Run Vintage],
	    0,
	    Last Year Thermal Battery Charging Mode Share by Vintage[Thermal Battery Charging Mode\
		,Model Run Vintage]
	  )
	)
	~	Dmnl
	~	Each thermal battery cohort's charging mode split, latched at the mode \
		logit's split in the cohort's installation year and held for the cohort's \
		life (latch-and-hold clone of Thermal Battery Production Subsidy Rate by \
		Vintage, without expiry). Only NEW additions choose their mode each year; \
		existing capacity keeps charging the way it was built to charge.
	|

Thermal Battery Charging Window[Electricity Timeslice,Hour]=
	IF THEN ELSE(
	  SUM(
	    IF THEN ELSE(
	      Last Year Five Year Average Marginal Dispatch Cost by Hour[Electricity Timeslice\
		,Hour for Comparison!]
	      < Last Year Five Year Average Marginal Dispatch Cost by Hour[Electricity Timeslice\
		,Hour]
	      :OR: (
	        Last Year Five Year Average Marginal Dispatch Cost by Hour[Electricity Timeslice\
		,Hour for Comparison!]
	        = Last Year Five Year Average Marginal Dispatch Cost by Hour[Electricity Timeslice\
		,Hour]
	        :AND: Hour Index[Hour for Comparison!] < Hour Index[Hour]
	      ),
	      1,
	      0
	    )
	  ) < TBCH Thermal Battery Daily Charging Hours,
	  1,
	  0
	)
	~	Dmnl
	~	1 for the hours of each timeslice day in which grid-charging thermal \
		batteries buy electricity, else 0 - computed ENDOGENOUSLY each year as the \
		TBCH cheapest hours of that timeslice day, ranked on the lagged five year \
		average marginal dispatch cost (an hour is selected when fewer than TBCH \
		hours are strictly cheaper, with equal-cost ties broken by hour of day). \
		The window therefore migrates as the generation mix evolves and as thermal \
		battery charging itself raises cheap-hour prices. The SAME window both \
		prices the load (Thermal Battery Grid Charging Electricity Cost per Unit \
		Energy) and places it on the hourly grid (Thermal Battery Grid Charging \
		Electricity Demand by Hour), so the model never buys power in hours it is \
		not consuming in. Replaces the former static TBCW input mask.
	|

Thermal Battery Choice Weight[Industry Category,industrial heating process]=
	10000
	* TBS Thermal Battery Shareweight
	* (
	  MAX(Thermal Battery Annual Costs and Annual Financing Repayment per Unit Energy in Electricity Equivalent\
		[Industry Category,industrial heating process], 1e-07)
	  / MAX(Minimum Eligible Fuel Cost per Unit Energy in Electricity Equivalent[Industry Category\
		,industrial heating process], 1e-07)
	) ^ IELC Industrial Equipment Logit Coefficient
	~	Dmnl
	~	Thermal battery entry in the new equipment relative-cost logit, as a \
		thirteenth competitor alongside the twelve Industrial Fuel elements. At \
		cement and other nonmetallic minerals 239 this now applies to the \
		non-cement RESIDUAL natively (the Phase 3a restructure scales the generic \
		equipment structure's data spine, so TB service and charging are \
		residual-sized by construction - the interim Phase 1b weight scaling is \
		retired). Identical form to Industrial Equipment Fuel Choice Weight. Using \
		the same reference cost as the fuel weights keeps shares exact: the \
		reference raised to the logit exponent multiplies every competitor's \
		weight equally and cancels out of the shares, so no reference adjustment \
		is needed even when thermal batteries are the cheapest option.
	|

Thermal Battery Demand Cap in Electricity Equivalent[Industry Category,industrial heating process\
		]=
	(
	  SUM(Output from Industrial Equipment Installed During Model Run[Industry Category,\
		industrial heating process,Model Run Vintage
!])
	  + Output from New Industrial Equipment[Industry Category,industrial heating process\
		]
	  + Output from Preexisting Industrial Equipment[Industry Category,industrial heating process\
		]
	)
	* Start Year Energy Intensity of Industrial Production in Electricity Equivalent[Industry Category\
		]
	~	BTU
	~	Total energy service demand for this industry and process on the \
		electricity equivalent basis, used as the ceiling on thermal battery heat \
		delivery, mirroring the distributed solar convention that behind the meter \
		output can never exceed on site demand (see Hourly Output from Distributed \
		Electricity Capacity by Building Type). With deployment flowing only \
		through the new equipment fuel choice the cap rarely binds; it guards \
		future subsidy or mandate driven deployment layers against overbuilding \
		past what the process can absorb.
	|

Thermal Battery Electricity Capacity Construction Cost[Electricity Source]=
	New Off Grid Electricity Capacity Serving Thermal Batteries[Electricity Source]
	* Construction Cost per Unit Capacity before Construction Subsidies[Electricity Source\
		]
	~	$
	~	Mirrors Cost of Green Hydrogen Electricity Capacity Construction.
	|

Thermal Battery Electricity Capacity Construction Subsidies Paid[Electricity Source]=
	New Off Grid Electricity Capacity Serving Thermal Batteries[Electricity Source]
	* Subsidy per Unit Electricity Capacity Built[Electricity Source]
	~	$
	~	Paid on capacity BUILT this year, matching the grid-sector convention \
		(Capacity Construction Subsidies Paid uses New and Retrofit Generation \
		Capacity) and this chain's own construction-cost basis. Deliberately \
		diverges from Green Hydrogen Electricity Capacity Construction Subsidies \
		Paid, which pays on the cumulative stock every year — a defect flagged \
		for the hydrogen off-grid review.
	|

Thermal Battery Electricity Cost by Mode[tb grid charging]=
	Grid Connected Thermal Battery Electricity Cost per Unit Energy ~~|
Thermal Battery Electricity Cost by Mode[tb off grid charging]=
	Off Grid Thermal Battery Electricity Cost per Unit Energy
	~	$/BTU
	~	The two charging supply options' electricity costs, gathered onto the \
		Thermal Battery Charging Mode subscript for the mode logit: grid purchases \
		during the charging-window hours versus dedicated off-grid renewables at \
		new-build levelized cost.
	|

Thermal Battery Electricity Fixed OM Costs[Electricity Source]=
	Off Grid Electricity Capacity Serving Thermal Batteries[Electricity Source]
	* Weighted Average Annual Fixed OM Cost per Unit Capacity[Electricity Source]
	~	$
	~	Mirrors Green Hydrogen Electricity Fixed OM Costs.
	|

Thermal Battery Electricity Generation Subsidies Paid[Electricity Source]=
	Thermal Battery Off Grid Electricity Demand by Power Plant Type[Electricity Source]
	* Subsidy per Unit Electricity Output Adjusted for Subsidy Duration[Electricity Source\
		]
	~	$
	~	Mirrors Green Hydrogen Electricity Generation Subsidies Paid.
	|

Thermal Battery Electricity Ongoing Capital Costs[Electricity Source]=
	Off Grid Electricity Capacity Serving Thermal Batteries[Electricity Source]
	* Weighted Average Annual Capital Costs per Unit Capacity[Electricity Source]
	~	$
	~	Mirrors Ongoing Capital Costs for Green Hydrogen Electricity.
	|

Thermal Battery Electricity Variable OM Costs[Electricity Source]=
	Thermal Battery Off Grid Electricity Demand by Power Plant Type[Electricity Source]
	* Weighted Average Variable OM Cost per Unit Electricity Output[Electricity Source]
	~	$
	~	Mirrors Green Hydrogen Electricity Variable OM Costs.
	|

Thermal Battery Energy Service[Industry Category,industrial heating process]=
	MIN(
	  Thermal Battery Energy Service before Demand Cap[Industry Category,industrial heating process\
		],
	  Thermal Battery Demand Cap in Electricity Equivalent[Industry Category,industrial heating process\
		]
	)
	~	BTU
	~	Heat delivered by thermal batteries (electricity equivalent service \
		basis), capped at the process's total service demand.
	|

Thermal Battery Energy Service before Demand Cap[Industry Category,industrial heating process\
		]=
	(
	SUM(
	  Output from Industrial Equipment Installed During Model Run[Industry Category,industrial heating process\
		,Model Run Vintage!]
	  * Last Year Thermal Battery Shares by Vintage[Industry Category,industrial heating process\
		,Model Run Vintage!]
	)

	+ Output from New Industrial Equipment[Industry Category,industrial heating process]
	* Thermal Battery Share of New Industrial Equipment[Industry Category,industrial heating process\
		]
	)
	* Start Year Energy Intensity of Industrial Production in Electricity Equivalent[Industry Category\
		]
	~	BTU
	~	Heat service delivered by thermal battery equipment cohorts, mirroring the \
		electricity branch of Industrial Equipment Energy Use by Fuel Type and \
		Process before Waste Heat Recovery: surviving vintage output times the \
		thermal battery share frozen at each vintage's installation, plus this \
		year's new equipment. Service is converted from output using the START \
		YEAR energy intensity (constant over time, matching the demand cap's \
		basis): thermal battery efficiency deliberately does not ride the \
		electric-equipment efficiency-improvement trajectory, pending a dedicated \
		thermal battery efficiency forecast input. Round trip losses are applied \
		in Thermal Battery Charging Electricity Demand, not here. No preexisting \
		equipment term: start year thermal battery stock is assumed zero.
	|

Thermal Battery Grid Charging Electricity Demand=
	SUM(
	  Thermal Battery Charging Electricity Demand[Industry Category!]
	  * Thermal Battery Grid Charging Share by Industry[Industry Category!]
	)
	* MWh per BTU
	~	MW*hour
	~	Grid-purchased (price-hunting) share of thermal battery charging \
		electricity. A standalone demand stream added to Total Electricity Demand \
		alongside Hydrogen Sector Grid Electricity Demand and BAU Data Center \
		Load, and to the hourly chain via Thermal Battery Grid Charging \
		Electricity Demand by Hour - never part of Industrial Fuel Use.
	|

Thermal Battery Grid Charging Electricity Demand by Hour[Electricity Timeslice,Hour]=
	Thermal Battery Grid Charging Electricity Demand
	* ZIDZ(
	    Thermal Battery Charging Window[Electricity Timeslice,Hour],
	    SUM(
	      Thermal Battery Charging Window[Electricity Timeslice!,Hour!]
	      * SHELF Days per Electricity Timeslice[Electricity Timeslice!]
	    )
	  )
	~	MW*hour
	~	Annual grid charging demand placed uniformly across the charging-window \
		hours: each representative-day cell gets annual demand divided by the \
		days-weighted count of window hours, so summing cells times SHELF Days per \
		Electricity Timeslice recovers the annual total. Added to Total \
		Electricity Demand by Hour before Net Imports (the same node where \
		distributed solar nets out), so dispatch, reliability, and next year's \
		marginal costs all see the flexible load in exactly the hours whose prices \
		it pays.
	|

Thermal Battery Grid Charging Share by Industry[Industry Category]=
	ZIDZ(
	  SUM(
	    Thermal Battery Charging Electricity Demand by Vintage[Industry Category,Model Run Vintage\
		!]
	    * Thermal Battery Charging Mode Share by Vintage[tb grid charging,Model Run Vintage\
		!]
	  )
	  + Thermal Battery New Charging Electricity Demand[Industry Category]
	    * Thermal Battery Charging Mode Share[tb grid charging],
	  SUM(Thermal Battery Charging Electricity Demand by Vintage[Industry Category,Model Run Vintage\
		!])
	  + Thermal Battery New Charging Electricity Demand[Industry Category]
	)
	~	Dmnl
	~	Grid-charged fraction of this industry's thermal battery fleet: cohort \
		charging weighted by each cohort's install-year mode latch, plus this \
		year's additions at the current mode split. Applied to the capped charging \
		total so mode routing, payments, and the demand cap stay mutually \
		consistent.
	|

Thermal Battery Grid Delivery Cost per Unit Electricity=
	ZIDZ(
	  Transmission OM Costs
	  + SYTaDC Start Year Transmission Capital Costs
	  + This Year Ongoing CES Costs,
	  Total Electricity Demand Plus Exports in BTU
	)
	~	$/BTU
	~	Endogenous delivery adder for grid-charged thermal batteries: average \
		transmission cost (OM plus the start year embedded capital rate base) plus \
		clean electricity standard compliance cost, per unit of delivered \
		electricity. Distribution and capacity market costs are deliberately \
		excluded: the model's own distribution cost driver is peak load growth \
		(Distribution Construction Costs) and the charging window is by \
		construction the cheapest off peak hours, so this load does not trigger \
		those costs; if its charging hours ever become the system peak, those \
		costs are booked system wide through the standard rate chain. Replaces the \
		industry retail residual adder, which was a start year calibration \
		artifact (negative in the US calibration) rather than a delivery cost.
	|

Thermal Battery Investment Subsidy Amount Paid[Industry Category]=
	SUM(
	  Perc Subsidy for Thermal Battery Equipment This Year[industrial heating process!]
	  * TBCC Thermal Battery Capital Cost per Unit Annual Energy Served[industrial heating process\
		!]
	  * Thermal Battery New Energy Service[Industry Category,industrial heating process!\
		]
	)
	~	$
	~	Government payments covering the subsidized fraction of this year's new \
		thermal battery equipment purchases (the thermal battery ITC). The capex \
		booking in New Industrial Equipment Capital Cost stays gross, matching the \
		clean industrial heat subsidy convention; this subsidy is booked \
		separately via Industry Sector Change in Government Subsidy Amount Paid.
	|

Thermal Battery New Charging Electricity Demand[Industry Category]=
	SUM(
	  Thermal Battery New Energy Service[Industry Category,industrial heating process!]
	  * Start Year Electric Heating Equipment Effective COP[industrial heating process!]
	  / TBRTE Thermal Battery Round Trip Efficiency[industrial heating process!]
	)
	~	BTU
	~	Charging electricity for heat service added by this year's new thermal \
		battery purchases (not yet in the installed-during-model-run stock); \
		charges at the CURRENT year's mode split, since new buyers make this \
		year's mode choice.
	|

Thermal Battery New Energy Service[Industry Category,industrial heating process]=
	Output from New Industrial Equipment[Industry Category,industrial heating process]
	* Thermal Battery Share of New Industrial Equipment[Industry Category,industrial heating process\
		]
	* Start Year Energy Intensity of Industrial Production in Electricity Equivalent[Industry Category\
		]
	~	BTU
	~	Annual heat service (electricity equivalent) added by this year's new \
		thermal battery equipment purchases; the basis for thermal battery \
		equipment capital cost accounting (times TBCC) in New Industrial Equipment \
		Capital Cost and the financing repayment ladder. Same product as the \
		new-equipment term of Thermal Battery Energy Service before Demand Cap.
	|

Thermal Battery Off Grid Capability Requirement=
	SUM(
	  Potential Output from Industrial Equipment Installed During Model Run[Industry Category\
		!,industrial heating process!,Model Run Vintage!]
	  * Last Year Thermal Battery Shares by Vintage[Industry Category!,industrial heating process\
		!,Model Run Vintage!]
	  * Start Year Electric Heating Equipment Effective COP[industrial heating process!]
	  / TBRTE Thermal Battery Round Trip Efficiency[industrial heating process!]
	  * Start Year Energy Intensity of Industrial Production in Electricity Equivalent[Industry Category\
		!]
	  * Thermal Battery Charging Mode Share by Vintage[tb off grid charging,Model Run Vintage\
		!]
	)
	* MWh per BTU
	~	MW*hour
	~	Full-utilization off-grid charging demand of the SURVIVING thermal battery \
		cohorts: potential (not utilized) vintage output times each cohort's \
		battery share and install-year off-grid mode latch. Because it uses \
		potential output, it falls only when equipment cohorts RETIRE, never on \
		utilization dips - so dedicated capacity is released as the cohorts it \
		served retire (per Dan O'Brien 2026-08-20), while temporary industry \
		downturns leave the fleet intact.
	|

Thermal Battery Off Grid Electricity Demand=
	SUM(
	  Thermal Battery Charging Electricity Demand[Industry Category!]
	  * (1 - Thermal Battery Grid Charging Share by Industry[Industry Category!])
	)
	* MWh per BTU
	~	MW*hour
	~	Total dedicated off-grid electricity demand for industrial thermal battery \
		charging. Deliberately NOT added to Total Electricity Demand or any hourly \
		grid demand chain: generation-following charging is off-grid by exclusion, \
		mirroring Hydrogen Sector Off Grid Electricity Demand for Green Hydrogen.
	|

Thermal Battery Off Grid Electricity Demand by Power Plant Type[Electricity Source]=
	Thermal Battery Off Grid Electricity Demand
	* ZIDZ(
	    Thermal Battery Off Grid Electricity Demand That is New This Year[Electricity Source\
		]
	    + Last Year Cumulative Thermal Battery Off Grid Electricity Demand by Power Plant Type\
		[Electricity Source]
	    - Retired Thermal Battery Off Grid Electricity Demand by Power Plant Type[Electricity Source\
		],
	    SUM(
	      Thermal Battery Off Grid Electricity Demand That is New This Year[Electricity Source\
		!]
	      + Last Year Cumulative Thermal Battery Off Grid Electricity Demand by Power Plant Type\
		[Electricity Source!]
	      - Retired Thermal Battery Off Grid Electricity Demand by Power Plant Type[Electricity Source\
		!]
	    )
	  )
	~	MW*hour
	~	ACTUAL off-grid charging demand allocated across power plant types by \
		their shares of the cumulative demand ledger. In growth years this equals \
		the ledger itself; when demand dips below the high-water mark, variable OM \
		and generation subsidies are paid on energy actually served rather than on \
		built capability - a deliberate departure from the hydrogen chain, which \
		pays on capability (utilization overstatement during dips flagged for its \
		review).
	|

Thermal Battery Off Grid Electricity Demand That is New This Year[Electricity Source]\
		=
	MAX(
	  Thermal Battery Off Grid Electricity Demand
	  - SUM(Last Year Cumulative Thermal Battery Off Grid Electricity Demand by Power Plant Type\
		[Electricity Source!]),
	
	  0
	)
	* Share of Off Grid Electricity Demand That is New This Year Met by Power Plant Type\
		[Electricity Source]
	~	MW*hour
	~	Demand beyond the supply capability of all dedicated capacity already \
		built (the cumulative high-water mark), NOT beyond last year demand: if \
		off-grid charging demand dips and later recovers - for example when the \
		charging mode logit shifts toward grid charging and back - the recovery is \
		served by existing idle capacity rather than triggering phantom new builds \
		that would permanently book capital and fixed OM. The hydrogen off-grid \
		chain this was cloned from still uses the last-year-demand baseline and \
		has the same dip-and-recover defect (flagged for upstream review).
	|

Thermal Battery Production Subsidy Amount Paid[Industry Category]=
	SUM(
	  Output from Industrial Equipment Installed During Model Run[Industry Category,industrial heating process\
		!,Model Run Vintage!]
	  * Last Year Thermal Battery Shares by Vintage[Industry Category,industrial heating process\
		!,Model Run Vintage!]
	  * Thermal Battery Production Subsidy Rate by Vintage[industrial heating process!,Model Run Vintage\
		!]
	)
	* Start Year Energy Intensity of Industrial Production in Electricity Equivalent[Industry Category\
		]

	+ SUM(
	    Thermal Battery New Energy Service[Industry Category,industrial heating process!\
		]
	    * Subsidy for Thermal Battery Heat Production This Year[industrial heating process\
		!]
	    / BpTPEU BTU per Medium Primary Energy Unit
	  )
	~	$
	~	Government payments per unit of heat delivered by thermal batteries (the \
		thermal battery PTC). Vintage-locked, mirroring Clean Industrial Heat \
		Production Subsidy Rate by Equipment Vintage: each vintage earns the rate \
		in effect at its installation for TBPD years, then expires. The second \
		term pays this year's new installs at the current rate (they are not yet \
		in the installed-during-model-run stock).
	|

Thermal Battery Production Subsidy Annuity Factor[Industry Category]= INITIAL(
	SUM(
	  ZIDZ(
	    IF THEN ELSE(
	      Numerical Future Year Definitions[Future Year!] - Time < 0,
	      0,
	      IF THEN ELSE(
	        Numerical Future Year Definitions[Future Year!] - Time >= TBPD Thermal Battery PTC Duration\
		,
	        0,
	        1
	      )
	    ),
	    Present Value Unmasked Decision Discount Factor[Industry Category,Future Year!]
	  )
	))
	~	Dmnl
	~	Sum of decision-rate discount factors across the thermal battery PTC \
		duration window, mirroring Clean Industrial Heat Subsidy Annuity Factor. \
		Used in the fuel choice so buyers value the PTC over its duration rather \
		than the full equipment lifetime; assumes the rate at purchase holds \
		through the duration (matching the vintage lock).
	|

Thermal Battery Production Subsidy Rate by Vintage[industrial heating process,Model Run Vintage\
		]=
	IF THEN ELSE(
	  This Year Model Run Vintage Flag[Model Run Vintage] = 1,
	  Subsidy for Thermal Battery Heat Production This Year[industrial heating process]
	  / BpTPEU BTU per Medium Primary Energy Unit,
	  IF THEN ELSE(
	    Dimensionless Time < Model Run Vintage to Year Mapping[Model Run Vintage],
	    0,
	    IF THEN ELSE(
	      Dimensionless Time >= Model Run Vintage to Year Mapping[Model Run Vintage] + TBPD Thermal Battery PTC Duration\
		/One Year,
	      0,
	      Last Year Thermal Battery Production Subsidy Rate by Vintage[industrial heating process\
		,Model Run Vintage]
	    )

	  )
	)
	~	$/BTU
	~	The thermal battery PTC rate each equipment vintage earns: latched at the \
		rate in effect when the vintage was installed, held for TBPD years, then \
		expired to zero. Clone of Clean Industrial Heat Production Subsidy Rate by \
		Equipment Vintage.
	|

Thermal Battery Share of New Industrial Equipment[Industry Category,industrial heating process\
		]=
	TBEM Thermal Battery Eligibility Mask[industrial heating process]
	* ZIDZ(
	  Thermal Battery Choice Weight[Industry Category,industrial heating process],
	  SUM(Industrial Equipment Fuel Choice Weight[Industry Category,Industrial Fuel!,industrial heating process\
		])
	  + Thermal Battery Choice Weight[Industry Category,industrial heating process]
	)
	~	Dmnl
	~	Share of new industrial equipment served by thermal batteries rather than \
		any Industrial Fuel: the TBEM-eligible market segment times the \
		thirteen-way logit share, so the share can never exceed TBEM. The \
		Industrial Fuel shares blend the same thirteen-way denominator with a \
		twelve-fuel logit for the ineligible segment, so all shares sum to one.
	|

Thermal Fuel Subsidies Paid[All Fuels,Sectors]=
	(Fuel Production[All Fuels]
	 * ZIDZ(
	     Total Energy Use by Sector[Sectors,All Fuels],
	
	     SUM(Total Energy Use by Sector[Sectors!,All Fuels])
	   )
	)
	* Subsidy for Thermal Fuels per Energy Unit Produced[All Fuels,Sectors]
	~	$
	~		|

This Year Battery Capacity Affecting Learning=
	Battery Storage Capacity Outside Modeled Region Affecting Learning
	+ Cumulative Battery Capacity Deployed
	~	MW*hr
	~		|

This Year Battery Capacity by Vehicle Type[Cargo Type,Vehicle Type,Vehicle Technology\
		]=
	Last Year Battery Capacity by Vehicle Type[Cargo Type,Vehicle Type,Vehicle Technology\
		]
	* (1 - AIiBCbVT Annual Improvement in Battery Capacity by Vehicle Type[Cargo Type,Vehicle Type\
		,Vehicle Technology])
	~	(kW*hr)/vehicle
	~		|

This Year Battery Cost as Fraction of Last Year Battery Cost=
	(1 - PDiBCpDoC Perc Decline in Battery Cost per Doubling of Capacity)
	^ MAX(0, LN(
	        This Year Battery Capacity Affecting Learning
	        / Last Year Battery Storage Affecting Learning
	      )
	    / LN(2)
	  )
	~	Dmnl
	~		|

This Year Battery Manufacturing Price After Subsidies[Vehicle Type,Cargo Type]=
	This Year Battery Manufacturing Price Before Subsidies by Vehicle Type[Vehicle Type,\
		Cargo Type]
	- Subsidy for Vehicle Battery Production Passed Through to Consumers
	~	$/(kW*hr)
	~		|

This Year Battery Manufacturing Price Before Subsidies=
	IF THEN ELSE(BPP Battery Pack Price > 0,
	
	  BPP Battery Pack Price,
	
	  IF THEN ELSE(BPP Battery Pack Price With One Year Delay > 0,
	
	    BPP Battery Pack Price With One Year Delay
	     * This Year Battery Cost as Fraction of Last Year Battery Cost,
	
	    Last Year Battery Manufacturing Price
	     * This Year Battery Cost as Fraction of Last Year Battery Cost
	  )
	)
	~	$/(kW*hr)
	~		|

This Year Battery Manufacturing Price Before Subsidies by Vehicle Type[Vehicle Type,Cargo Type\
		]=
	This Year Battery Manufacturing Price Before Subsidies
	* BPPM Battery Pack Price Multiplier[Vehicle Type,Cargo Type]
	~	$/(kW*hr)
	~		|

This Year Battery Portion of Vehicle Price[Cargo Type,Vehicle Type,Vehicle Technology\
		]=
	This Year Battery Capacity by Vehicle Type[Cargo Type,Vehicle Type,Vehicle Technology\
		]
	* This Year Battery Manufacturing Price After Subsidies[Vehicle Type,Cargo Type]
	* BPM Battery Pack Markup[Vehicle Type,Cargo Type]
	~	$/vehicle
	~		|

This Year Battery Subsidy Passed Through to Consumers by Vehicle Type[Cargo Type,Vehicle Type\
		,Vehicle Technology]=
	This Year Battery Capacity by Vehicle Type[Cargo Type,Vehicle Type,Vehicle Technology\
		]
	* Subsidy for Vehicle Battery Production Passed Through to Consumers
	~	$/vehicle
	~		|

This Year Capacity Market Repayment=
	DEPRECIATE STRAIGHTLINE(
	
	  Total Capacity Market Costs
	  / One Year,
	 
	  Repayment Period for Capacity Market Charges,
	
	  1,
	
	  0
	)
	~	$/Year
	~		|

This Year CCS Cost as Fraction of Last Year CCS Cost=
	(1 - PDiCECpDoC Perc Decline in CCS Eqpt Cost per Doubling of Capacity)
	^ MAX(0, LN(
	        This Year CO2 Sequestered Affecting Learning
	        / Last Year CO2 Sequestered Affecting Learning
	      )
	    / LN(2)
	  )
	~	Dmnl
	~	In the event we have no CCS in the base year, we do not decline CCS costs \
		based on installed capacity, because we cannot calculate the doublings of \
		capacity from the base year.  Also, we disallow increases in CCS cost, \
		which could otherwise happen if CCS capacity declines in any year relative \
		to the prior year.
	|

This Year CCS Subsidies Paid[Electricity Source]=
	CCS Subsidies Paid per Unit Electricity Output for Newly Eligible CCS Capacity[Electricity Source\
		]
	
	+Last Year CCS Subsidies Paid per Unit Electricity Output for Newly Eligible CCS Capacity\
		[Electricity Source]
	
	- CCS Subsidy Payments per Unit Electricity Output Expiring After CCS Subsidy Duration Period\
		[Electricity Source]
	~	$
	~		|

This Year Change in Distributed Electricity Source Capacity[Building Type,Electricity Source\
		] :EXCEPT: [Building Type,solar PV es]=
	BAU This Year Increase in Distributed Electricity Source Capacity[Building Type,Electricity Source\
		] ~~|
This Year Change in Distributed Electricity Source Capacity[Building Type,solar PV es\
		]=
	BAU This Year Increase in Distributed Electricity Source Capacity[Building Type,solar PV es\
		]
	+ Distributed Solar Capacity Caused by Policies This Year[Building Type] * One Year
	~	MW
	~		|

This Year Change in Hydrogen Produced by Pathway[Hydrogen Production Pathway]=
	Hydrogen Production by Pathway[Hydrogen Production Pathway]
	- Last Year Hydrogen Produced by Pathway[Hydrogen Production Pathway]
	~	BTU
	~		|

This Year Change in Industry Unit Costs as Share of BAU Output with Hold at Minimum Production\
		[Industry Category ISIC Code]=
	IF THEN ELSE(
	  1 + Percent Change in Production due to Policies[Industry Category] > 0.05,
	  (
	    Normalized Change in Amount Spent on Industry Fuels[Industry Category ISIC Code]
	
	    + Normalized Change in Industrial Nonfuel Expenditures[Industry Category ISIC Code\
		]
	    - Material Efficiency Intermediate Cost Savings Share by ISIC Code[Industry Category ISIC Code\
		]
	  )
	  * SoCiIEPTtB Share of Change in Industry Expenses Passed Through to Buyers[Industry Category\
		],
	  Last Year Change in Industry Unit Costs as Share of BAU Output with Hold at Minimum Production\
		[Industry Category ISIC Code
	]
	)
	~	Dmnl
	~	Change in the industry's production cost per unit of output, as a share of \
		BAU unit cost, passed through to buyers.  Fuel spending is measured per \
		unit of CURRENT production (BAU output times one plus Percent Change in \
		Production due to Policies) and compared with BAU fuel spending per unit \
		of BAU production, so that fuel spending changes caused merely by \
		producing more or less do not register as price changes.  Miscellaneous \
		expenditures, the CCS carbon tax rebate, and CCS and waste heat recovery \
		equipment costs are level changes taken over BAU output (the latter two \
		are policy-only costs with no volume component).  General new-equipment \
		capital costs enter only through their cost-per-unit component (see Change \
		in New Industrial Equipment Capital Costs due to Change in Cost per Unit \
		Output) so that volume-driven purchase changes do not register as price \
		changes.  The material efficiency intermediate cost savings share enters \
		here directly as a per-unit cost reduction.  When production has fallen \
		more than 95% the per-unit measure is unreliable, so the prior year's \
		value is held.  Loop safety: only the Last Year DELAY FIXED copy of this \
		variable feeds the smoothed price signal.
	|

This Year CO2 Sequestered[electricity sector]=
	SUM(This Year Pollutants Sequestered by Electricity Sector[CO2,Electricity Source!])
	/ Grams per Metric Ton CO2e
	/ Grams Pollutant per Gram CO2e Unit Converter ~~|
This Year CO2 Sequestered[industry sector]=
	SUM(This Year Pollutants Sequestered by Industry Sector[Industry Category!,CO2,Emissions Type\
		!])
	/ Grams per Metric Ton CO2e
	/ Grams Pollutant per Gram CO2e Unit Converter ~~|
This Year CO2 Sequestered[district heat and hydrogen sector]=
	This Year Pollutants Sequestered by Hydrogen Sector[CO2]
	/ Grams per Metric Ton CO2e
	/ Grams Pollutant per Gram CO2e Unit Converter
	~	metric tons CO2e
	~		|

This Year CO2 Sequestered Affecting Learning=
	This Year CO2 Sequestered Outside Modeled Region Affecting Learning
	+ SUM(Last Year CO2 Sequestered[CCS Sectors!])
	~	metric tons CO2e
	~		|

This Year CO2 Sequestered Outside Modeled Region Affecting Learning=
	MAX(
	  0,
	
	  (BCSG BAU CO2 Sequestered Globally
	    - SUM(BAU Last Year CO2 Sequestered[CCS Sectors!]))
	  * FoTOMRAEL Fraction of Technology Outside Modeled Region Affecting Endogenous Learning
	)
	~	metric tons CO2e
	~		|

This Year Cumulative Electricity Generation Capacity Affecting Learning[Electricity Source\
		]=
	Cumulative Electricity Capacity Built as of Last Year[Electricity Source]
	+ This Year Global Solar and Wind Capacities Affecting Learning[Electricity Source]
	~	
	~		|

This Year Cumulative Policy Mandated Grid Battery Storage=
	Last Year Cumulative Policy Mandated Grid Battery Storage
	+ Policy Mandated Grid Battery Storage Additions
	~	MW*hour
	~		|

